planation":"The source marks Nitrofurantoin orally. as the treatment/management choice for the clinical situation described."},{"id":"q_past_186","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 23","text":"The following is not a cause of massive enlarged spleen:","options":{"A":"Cytomegalovirus.","B":"Visceral leishmaniasis.","C":"Hyper-reactive tropical malarial syndrome.","D":"Major thalassemia."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks Cytomegalovirus. as the exception. The question is testing recognition of the statement that does not fit the expected features of the condition."},{"id":"q_past_187","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 23","text":"Blackwater fever is a special manifestation of malaria. What is the cause?","options":{"A":"Falciparum.","B":"Malariae.","C":"Ovale.","D":"Vivax."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Plasmodium falciparum is the malaria species associated with the most severe disease and major complications."},{"id":"q_past_188","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 23","text":"Which are not portals of entry for the organism in leptospirosis:","options":{"A":"Skin.","B":"Mucous membranes.","C":"Conjunctiva.","D":"Sexual contact."},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks Sexual contact. as the exception. The question is testing recognition of the statement that does not fit the expected features of the condition."},{"id":"q_past_189","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 23","text":"Which of these pulmonary conditions is most likely to be seen with a CD4 count between 200 and 500?","options":{"A":"Pulmonary TB.","B":"Cytomegalovirus.","C":"Pneumocystis carinii pneumonia.","D":"Cryptococcus."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks Pulmonary TB. as the most likely diagnosis/answer for the clinical pattern described."},{"id":"q_past_190","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 23","text":"A 54-year-old neutropenic man undergoing cancer chemotherapy presents with difficult painful swallowing. Endoscopy of the esophagus reveals multiple white plaques with no ulceration. Which organism is most likely the cause?","options":{"A":"Cytomegalovirus.","B":"Epstein-Barr virus.","C":"Candida albicans.","D":"Human immunodeficiency virus."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Candida albicans. as the most likely diagnosis/answer for the clinical pattern described."},{"id":"q_past_191","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 23","text":"A baby is born to a mother who is known to have chronic hepatitis B. The mother''s latest results are as follows: HBsAg positive, HBeAg positive. What is the most appropriate strategy to reduce the vertical transmission rate?","options":{"A":"Give the newborn hepatitis B vaccine.","B":"Give the newborn hepatitis B immunoglobulin.","C":"Give the newborn hepatitis B vaccine + hepatitis B immunoglobulin.","D":"Give the mother hepatitis B vaccine shortly before birth + the newborn hepatitis B vaccine."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Give the newborn hepatitis B vaccine + hepatitis B immunoglobulin.. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_192","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 23","text":"Which of the following organisms causes epidemic meningitis cases among military recruits?","options":{"A":"Haemophilus influenzae type B.","B":"Neisseria meningitidis.","C":"Streptococcus pneumoniae.","D":"Listeria monocytogenes."},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Neisseria meningitidis is a classic cause of acute bacterial meningitis and meningococcal outbreaks."},{"id":"q_past_193","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"The primary preventive strategy for community-acquired pneumonia is:","options":{"A":"Antibiotic therapy.","B":"Good hand hygiene.","C":"Adherence to healthy lifestyle behaviors.","D":"Immunization with pneumococcal and influenza vaccines."},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Pneumococcal and influenza vaccination is the key preventive measure highlighted for community-acquired pneumonia."},{"id":"q_past_194","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Release of which of the following results in fever?","options":{"A":"Interleukin (IL) 8.","B":"Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor.","C":"Interleukin (IL) 1.","D":"All of the above."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"IL-1 is an endogenous pyrogen that contributes to fever through hypothalamic prostaglandin-mediated temperature elevation."},{"id":"q_past_195","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"The single most useful clinical sign of severity of pneumonia in a person without underlying lung disease is:","options":{"A":"Respiratory rate >30/minute.","B":"Pulse rate >100/minute.","C":"Hypothermia.","D":"Postural fall of SBP >10 mm Hg."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Marked tachypnea is an important clinical marker of pneumonia severity."},{"id":"q_past_196","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"A 7-year school boy has been diagnosed with meningococcal meningitis. What is the advice for schoolmates and staff?","options":{"A":"Rifampicin for the whole class and family.","B":"Rifampicin for the whole school and family.","C":"Meningococcal vaccine for the family.","D":"Benzylpenicillin.","E":"IV cefotaxime."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Close contacts of meningococcal disease require chemoprophylaxis; rifampicin is the source-marked choice."},{"id":"q_past_197","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Most reliable clue to poor tissue perfusion in septic shock is:","options":{"A":"Hyponatraemia.","B":"High level of blood lactate.","C":"Hypochloraemia.","D":"Low level of HCO3."},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Elevated lactate reflects impaired tissue perfusion and anaerobic metabolism, making it a useful marker in septic shock."},{"id":"q_past_198","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Which of the following are the features of enteric fever in the first week?","options":{"A":"Continued pyrexia.","B":"Pea-soup diarrhoea.","C":"Relative tachycardia.","D":"Typhoid state."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks Continued pyrexia.. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_199","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Rat-bite fever is caused by the organism:","options":{"A":"Spirillum minus.","B":"A gram-positive cocci.","C":"Yersinia multocida.","D":"Treponema pertenue."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Spirillum minus is the organism classically associated with rat-bite fever in the source."},{"id":"q_past_200","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Cyclical periods of pyrexia alternating with apyrexia is found in all except:","options":{"A":"Relapsing fever.","B":"Leprosy.","C":"Hodgkin''s disease.","D":"Brucellosis."},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Leprosy is not classically characterized by cyclical fever alternating with afebrile periods, making it the exception here."},{"id":"q_past_201","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Cat-scratch disease is characterized by all except:","options":{"A":"Positive Hanger-Rose intradermal test.","B":"Erythema nodosum.","C":"Regional lymphadenopathy.","D":"Treated by antibiotic and corticosteroids."},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source identifies this treatment statement as the exception for cat-scratch disease."},{"id":"q_past_202","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Cerebral malaria is:","options":{"A":"Treated by oral quinine sulphate.","B":"Often complicated by multi-organ failure.","C":"Associated with signs of meningeal irritation.","D":"IV dexamethasone reduces mortality rate."},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Severe cerebral malaria can be complicated by systemic organ dysfunction and multi- organ failure."},{"id":"q_past_203","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Hospital-acquired pneumonia (HAP) is defined as pneumonia occurring how many hours after hospital admission?","options":{"A":"At least 12 hours.","B":"At least 24 hours.","C":"At least 36 hours.","D":"At least 48 hours."},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Pneumonia developing at least 48 hours after admission is classified as hospital-acquired pneumonia in this source."},{"id":"q_past_204","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Which is true in Widal test?","options":{"A":"Relapse bears relationship with agglutinin titre.","B":"Agglutinin against flagellar antigen is most important.","C":"Agglutinins begin to appear after 3rd day of infection.","D":"Agglutinins reach a peak titre during the fifth or sixth week."},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source states that Widal agglutinins reach peak titre during the fifth or sixth week."},{"id":"q_past_205","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"River blindness is due to:","options":{"A":"Toxoplasmosis.","B":"Trichinosis.","C":"Onchocerciasis.","D":"Cysticercosis."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Onchocerciasis due to Onchocerca volvulus is the cause of river blindness."},{"id":"q_past_206","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Drug of choice in Pneumocystis carinii pneumonia is:","options":{"A":"Pentamidine isethionate.","B":"Zidovudine.","C":"Cefoperazone.","D":"Aztreonam."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks pentamidine isethionate as the treatment choice for Pneumocystis pneumonia."},{"id":"q_past_207","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Which of the following is incorrect about CSF examination in viral meningitis?","options":{"A":"Low tension <180 mm H2O.","B":"Turbid aspect.","C":"High lymphocytes.","D":"High protein 50-100 mg/dl.","E":"Glucose >40 mg/dl."},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Bacterial meningitis commonly produces turbid CSF because of the high cellular/protein burden."},{"id":"q_past_208","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Mefloquine is used for prophylaxis against malaria:","options":{"A":"One day before travelling to endemic area.","B":"3 days before travelling.","C":"2 days before travelling.","D":"One week before travelling."},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks one week before travel as the timing for mefloquine prophylaxis."},{"id":"q_past_209","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"During premarital care, the following vaccine/s could be given:","options":{"A":"Hepatitis B.","B":"Tetanus toxoid.","C":"Rubella.","D":"All of the above.","E":"A & B."},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks All of the above.. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_210","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"The most affected WBC of the immune system with HIV infection is:","options":{"A":"CD8 T-lymphocyte.","B":"CD4 T-lymphocyte.","C":"Plasma cell.","D":"Macrophage.","E":"CD3 T-lymphocyte."},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"HIV preferentially infects CD4 T lymphocytes, causing progressive depletion of this cell population."},{"id":"q_past_211","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Acyclovir is an antiviral drug that is usually used in treatment of:","options":{"A":"Hepatitis C virus.","B":"Hepatitis B virus.","C":"Herpes Zoster virus.","D":"Human immunodeficiency virus.","E":"Cytomegalovirus."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Herpes Zoster virus. as the treatment/management choice for the clinical situation described."},{"id":"q_past_212","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Antimalarial drug that can be used in treatment of Rheumatoid Arthritis:","options":{"A":"Primaquine.","B":"Proguanil.","C":"Chloroquine.","D":"Mefloquine.","E":"Quinine."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Chloroquine. as the treatment/management choice for the clinical situation described."},{"id":"q_past_213","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"In neutropenic fever, absolute count of neutrophils is:","options":{"A":"≤ 1000 per mm3.","B":"≤ 500 per mm3.","C":"≤ 850 per mm3.","D":">1000 per mm3."},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks ≤ 500 per mm3.. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_214","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Which of the following organisms causes epidemic meningitis cases at military recruits?","options":{"A":"Haemophilus influenzae type b.","B":"Neisseria meningitidis.","C":"Streptococcus pneumoniae.","D":"Listeria monocytogenes."},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Neisseria meningitidis is a classic cause of acute bacterial meningitis and meningococcal outbreaks."},{"id":"q_past_215","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Lumbar puncture needle for CSF analysis is inserted between:","options":{"A":"L1-L2.","B":"L3-L4.","C":"L4-L5.","D":"L5-S1."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks L4-L5.. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_216","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Which of the following CSF results suggests TB meningitis?","options":{"A":"Elevated CSF neutrophil count, raised protein, and low glucose.","B":"Elevated CSF lymphocytes count, normal protein, and normal glucose.","C":"Elevated CSF lymphocytes count, raised protein, and low glucose.","D":"No CSF cell count, normal protein, and normal glucose."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Elevated CSF lymphocytes count, raised protein, and low glucose.. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_217","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Adequate treatment of a case is considered as:","options":{"A":"Primary prevention for case and contact.","B":"Primary prevention for case and secondary for contact.","C":"Secondary prevention for case and primary for contact.","D":"Primary prevention for contact.","E":"Secondary prevention of case."},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"The source marks Secondary prevention of case. as the treatment/management choice for the clinical situation described."},{"id":"q_past_218","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"One of these fungal infections has bad prognosis if associated with COVID-19:","options":{"A":"Cryptococcosis.","B":"Candidiasis.","C":"Mucormycosis.","D":"Histoplasmosis.","E":"Aspergillosis."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Mucormycosis.. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_219","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Non-gonococcal urethritis can be treated by:","options":{"A":"Oral single dose of azithromycin 500 mg once daily for 5 days.","B":"Oral single dose of azithromycin 500 mg once daily for 5 days plus metronidazole 2 gm daily for 7 days.","C":"7-day course of doxycycline 100 mg orally twice daily plus metronidazole 2 gm daily.","D":"7-day course of doxycycline 100 mg orally twice daily."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks 7-day course of doxycycline 100 mg orally twice daily plus metronidazole 2 gm daily.. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_220","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"The most effective way for healthcare providers to protect themselves, their family and their patients from influenza is to:","options":{"A":"Wear a surgical mask at all times at work.","B":"Stay at home if they have respiratory symptoms.","C":"Get an annual flu shot and encourage others to get the flu vaccine annually.","D":"Not go to work from November to April.","E":"Follow physical distance."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Get an annual flu shot and encourage others to get the flu vaccine annually.. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_221","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"The treatment of hepatitis C has been revolutionised most recently by which of the following?","options":{"A":"The use of interferon.","B":"A new vaccine.","C":"Monoclonal antibodies.","D":"Direct-acting antivirals such as daclatasvir and sofosbuvir.","E":"The use of interferon and vaccine."},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks Direct-acting antivirals such as daclatasvir and sofosbuvir. as the treatment/management choice for the clinical situation described."},{"id":"q_past_222","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Nipah is a zoonotic paramyxovirus; where did it originate?","options":{"A":"Originating in pigs.","B":"Originating in bats.","C":"Coming from humans.","D":"Coming from horses.","E":"Coming from birds."},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks Originating in bats.. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_223","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"A 38-year-old man with HIV/AIDS presents with 4 weeks of diarrhea, fever, and weight loss. Which of the following tests makes the diagnosis of cytomegalovirus (CMV) colitis?","options":{"A":"Colonoscopy with biopsy.","B":"Stool CMV antigen.","C":"CMV IgG.","D":"Serum CMV polymerase chain reaction (PCR).","E":"Stool CMV culture."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks AIDS as the exception in this aseptic-fever question."},{"id":"q_past_224","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Which are the two types of leptospirosis?","options":{"A":"Edematous and non-edematous.","B":"Icteric and anicteric.","C":"Haemorrhagic and non-haemorrhagic.","D":"None of the above."},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Weil disease is the severe icteric form of leptospirosis."},{"id":"q_past_225","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"One of the modern-day worries is about emerging infections. Which of the following is an emerging infection with the greatest global impact?","options":{"A":"H1N1 infection.","B":"Human papilloma virus infection.","C":"H5N1 infection.","D":"HIV/AIDS.","E":"SARS."},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"The source marks SARS.. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_226","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Which of the following is not an RNA virus?","options":{"A":"Hepatitis A virus.","B":"Hepatitis B virus.","C":"Hepatitis C virus.","D":"Hepatitis D virus."},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Hepatitis B virus is a DNA virus, unlike the other listed hepatitis viruses that contain RNA."},{"id":"q_past_227","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Malta fever is caused by:","options":{"A":"Pasteurella tularensis.","B":"Haemophilus influenzae.","C":"Brucella abortus.","D":"Pseudomonas mallei."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Brucella abortus is a major cause of brucellosis and is associated with the classic occupational/animal exposure setting."},{"id":"q_past_228","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Woolsorter''s disease is:","options":{"A":"Plague.","B":"Legionnaires'' disease.","C":"Klebsiella-induced pneumonia.","D":"Anthrax."},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Woolsorter ’ s disease is the inhalational form of anthrax associated with contaminated animal products."},{"id":"q_past_229","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Which is not a cause of aseptic fever?","options":{"A":"Crush injury.","B":"SLE.","C":"AIDS.","D":"Pontine haemorrhage."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks AIDS as the exception in this aseptic-fever question."},{"id":"q_past_230","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"The best score in Glasgow Coma Scale is:","options":{"A":"10.","B":"15.","C":"20.","D":"22.","E":"12."},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The maximum possible Glasgow Coma Scale score is 15."},{"id":"q_past_231","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"The most dangerous type of malaria is:","options":{"A":"Vivax.","B":"P. malariae.","C":"Falciparum.","D":"Ovale."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Plasmodium falciparum is the malaria species associated with the most severe disease and major complications."},{"id":"q_past_232","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Generalized lymphadenopathy is considered if there is:","options":{"A":"Enlargement of more than two non-contiguous lymph node groups.","B":"Enlargement of less than two non-contiguous lymph node groups.","C":"Enlargement of more than two contiguous lymph node groups.","D":"Enlargement of one lymph node group."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Generalized lymphadenopathy means involvement of more than two non-contiguous lymph-node groups."},{"id":"q_past_233","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"One of the following is not a method of HIV infection:","options":{"A":"Feco-oral route.","B":"IV drug users and sharing needles.","C":"Sexual relations.","D":"Vertical transmission of infected mothers.","E":"Lactation."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks Feco-oral route. as the exception. The question is testing recognition of the statement that does not fit the expected features of the condition."},{"id":"q_past_234","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 22","text":"Inflammation of the brain parenchyma is referred to as:","options":{"A":"Meningitis.","B":"Myelitis.","C":"Encephalitis.","D":"Meningoencephalitis."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Encephalitis.. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_235","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Hospital-acquired pneumonia (HAP) is defined as pneumonia occurring how many hours after hospital admission?","options":{"A":"At least 12 hours","B":"At least 24 hours","C":"At least 36 hours","D":"At least 48 hours"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Pneumonia developing at least 48 hours after admission is classified as hospital-acquired pneumonia in this source."},{"id":"q_past_236","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"A 7-year school-boy has been diagnosed with meningococcal meningitis. What is the advice for schoolmates and staff?","options":{"A":"Rifampicin for the whole class and family","B":"Rifampicin for the whole school and family","C":"Meningococcal vaccine for the family","D":"Benzylpenicillin","E":"IV cefotaxime"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Close contacts of meningococcal disease require chemoprophylaxis; rifampicin is the source-marked choice."},{"id":"q_past_237","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Which of the following CSF results suggests TB meningitis?","options":{"A":"Elevated CSF neutrophil count, raised protein, and low glucose","B":"Elevated CSF lymphocytes count, normal protein, and normal glucose","C":"Elevated CSF lymphocytes count, raised protein, and low glucose","D":"No CSF cell count, normal protein, and normal glucose"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Elevated CSF lymphocytes count, raised protein, and low glucose. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_238","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Mefloquine is used for prophylaxis against malaria:","options":{"A":"One day before travelling to endemic area","B":"3 days before travelling","C":"2 days before travelling","D":"One week before travelling"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks one week before travel as the timing for mefloquine prophylaxis."},{"id":"q_past_239","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Most reliable clue to poor tissue perfusion in septic shock is:","options":{"A":"Hyponatremia","B":"High level of blood lactate","C":"Hypochloremia","D":"Low level of HCO3"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Elevated lactate reflects impaired tissue perfusion and anaerobic metabolism, making it a useful marker in septic shock."},{"id":"q_past_240","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Drug of choice in Pneumocystis carinii pneumonia?","options":{"A":"Cefoperazone","B":"Zidovudine","C":"Aztreonam","D":"Pentamidine isethionate"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks pentamidine isethionate as the treatment choice for Pneumocystis pneumonia."},{"id":"q_past_241","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Release of which of the following results in fever?","options":{"A":"Interleukin (IL) 8","B":"Granulocyte colony-stimulating factor","C":"Interleukin (IL) 1","D":"Tumor necrosis factor"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"IL-1 is an endogenous pyrogen that contributes to fever through hypothalamic prostaglandin-mediated temperature elevation."},{"id":"q_past_242","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"In neutropenic fever, absolute count of neutrophils is?","options":{"A":"≤ 1000 per mm3","B":"≤ 500 per mm3","C":"≤ 850 per mm3","D":"> 1000 per mm3"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks ≤ 500 per mm3. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_243","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Male patient 50 years old, farmer, came to ER by hematemesis, splenic size was 25 cm, esophageal varices, etiology of splenomegaly in such case is:","options":{"A":"Myelofibrosis","B":"Egyptian splenomegaly","C":"Syphilis","D":"Hyperthyroidism"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source associates marked splenomegaly with portal-hypertension/Schistosoma- related Egyptian splenomegaly in this clinical scenario."},{"id":"q_past_244","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Which of the following is false regarding Widal test?","options":{"A":"Titre of O-antigen above 1:640 is often very significant in enteric fever","B":"Antibiotic therapy may depress the agglutination titres","C":"In vaccinated subjects, the O-titre disappears in time but H-agglutinins persist for years","D":"An agglutination reaction"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks In vaccinated subjects, the O-titre disappears in time but H-agglutinins persist for years as the exception. The question is testing recognition of the statement that does not fit the expected features of the condition."},{"id":"q_past_245","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Which is not a recognised complication of acute shigellosis?","options":{"A":"Haemolytic-uraemic syndrome","B":"Reiter''s syndrome","C":"Liver abscess","D":"Endotoxic shock"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Liver abscess is not a recognized typical complication of acute shigellosis in the source."},{"id":"q_past_246","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Cysticercosis is the larval stage of:","options":{"A":"Taenia solium","B":"Hymenolepis nana","C":"Taenia saginata","D":"Ancylostoma duodenale"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Cysticercosis is caused by the larval stage of Taenia solium."},{"id":"q_past_247","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Which of the following does not produce hypothermia?","options":{"A":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","B":"Panhypopituitarism","C":"Peripheral circulatory failure","D":"Myxoedema coma"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks neuroleptic malignant syndrome as the condition that does not produce hypothermia."},{"id":"q_past_248","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"As regard campylobacter food poisoning the following are correct except:","options":{"A":"The most common causes of foodborne illness","B":"Pets may also be a source of infection","C":"Unpasteurized milk not cause campylobacter food poisoning","D":"It is present in the gut of a wide range of animals, especially birds"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Unpasteurized milk not cause campylobacter food poisoning as the exception. The question is testing recognition of the statement that does not fit the expected features of the condition."},{"id":"q_past_249","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Giving routine iron and folic acid to all pregnant women is a form of:","options":{"A":"Primary prevention","B":"Secondary prevention","C":"Tertiary prevention","D":"Chemoprophylaxis"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks Primary prevention. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_250","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"The following are risk factor for candida infection except?","options":{"A":"HIV and AIDS","B":"Long term steroids use","C":"HAV","D":"Immunosuppressive therapy","E":"Long term antibiotics use"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks HAV as the exception. The question is testing recognition of the statement that does not fit the expected features of the condition."},{"id":"q_past_251","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"The main causative agent of urinary tract infections is:","options":{"A":"Klebsiella pneumonia","B":"Staphylococcus saprophyticus","C":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa","D":"Escherichia coli"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks Escherichia coli. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_252","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Regarding primary amoebic meningoencephalitis, which is false?","options":{"A":"Almost invariably fatal","B":"Caused by Naegleria fowleri","C":"Dehydroemetine is drug of choice","D":"Acquired by swimming in fresh warm water"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks dehydroemetine as the false statement for primary amoebic meningoencephalitis."},{"id":"q_past_253","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"A newly married female has decided to receive (MMR) vaccine, the doctor advised her to postpone pregnancy for:","options":{"A":"Only one month","B":"For at least 3 months","C":"For one year","D":"Not at all"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"After an MMR vaccine, pregnancy is postponed for the interval specified in the source."},{"id":"q_past_254","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"The WHO established a standardized vaccination schedule for the original EPI which included the following vaccines except:","options":{"A":"Bacillus Calmette-Gu é rin (BCG)","B":"Rota virus vaccine","C":"Diphtheria-tetanus-pertussis (DTP)","D":"Oral polio and Measles"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The original EPI vaccine package did not include rotavirus vaccine in the schedule described by the source."},{"id":"q_past_255","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Toxoplasmosis is infection with Toxoplasma gondii. The only known hosts of this organism are domestic cats and their relatives. Which of the following is the most common mode of infection in humans?","options":{"A":"Blood transfusion","B":"Ingestion of oocysts","C":"Ingestion of tissue cysts","D":"Transplacental transmission"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Oocyst ingestion is a recognized route of Toxoplasma gondii acquisition in humans."},{"id":"q_past_256","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Which one of the following causes hypothalamic reset?","options":{"A":"Prostaglandin F","B":"T-regulator cells","C":"Prostaglandin E2","D":"Cell adhesion molecule"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source identifies Prostaglandin E2 as the causative organism or etiologic answer tested by this question."},{"id":"q_past_257","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Which of the following organisms causes epidemic meningitis cases at military recruits?","options":{"A":"Haemophilus influenzae type b","B":"Neisseria meningitidis","C":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","D":"Listeria monocytogenes"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Neisseria meningitidis is a classic cause of acute bacterial meningitis and meningococcal outbreaks."},{"id":"q_past_258","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Cinchonism is a possible side effect for:","options":{"A":"Primaquine","B":"Proguanil","C":"Chloroquine","D":"Mefloquine","E":"Quinine"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"The source marks Quinine. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_259","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Acyclovir is an antiviral drug that is usually used in treatment of:","options":{"A":"Hepatitis C virus","B":"Herpes Zoster virus","C":"Hepatitis B virus","D":"Human immunodeficiency virus","E":"Cytomegalovirus"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks Herpes Zoster virus as the treatment/management choice for the clinical situation described."},{"id":"q_past_260","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Improper contact lens-care practices may lead to:","options":{"A":"River blindness","B":"Acanthamoeba keratitis","C":"African eye worm","D":"Ocular toxocariasis","E":"Ocular toxoplasmosis"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks Acanthamoeba keratitis. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_261","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Retinochoroiditis is caused by eye infection with:","options":{"A":"Loa loa","B":"Onchocerca volvulus","C":"Demodex folliculorum","D":"Toxoplasma gondii","E":"Toxocara canis"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source identifies Toxoplasma gondii as the causative organism or etiologic answer tested by this question."},{"id":"q_past_262","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"SARS is described as a zoonotic virus. What does this mean?","options":{"A":"They emerge from animals to cross the species barrier infrequently","B":"They cause pandemics","C":"Such viruses are confined to animals","D":"They do not cause disease in humans"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks They emerge from animals to cross the species barrier infrequently. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_263","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"The chain of infection has five main components. The habitat (animals, human or environment) in which the agent normally lives, grows, and multiplies is known as:","options":{"A":"The reservoir of the infectious agent","B":"The portal of exit","C":"Portal of entry","D":"Mode of transmission of the infectious agent","E":"The susceptible host to the infectious agent"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks The reservoir of the infectious agent. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_264","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"A 19-year-old student presents with a fifteen-week history of diarrhoea. He has lost 2 kg in weight, and has no recent travel abroad. A smear of a duodenal biopsy reveals many trophozoites. What is the best treatment option?","options":{"A":"Ciprofloxacin","B":"Gluten free diet","C":"Metronidazole","D":"Prednisolone","E":"Quinine"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Metronidazole as the treatment/management choice for the clinical situation described."},{"id":"q_past_265","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"A person with liver disease caused by Schistosoma mansoni would be most likely to have:","options":{"A":"Ascites","B":"Esophageal varices","C":"Gynecomastia","D":"Jaundice","E":"Spider nevi"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks Esophageal varices as the most likely diagnosis/answer for the clinical pattern described."},{"id":"q_past_266","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"All of the following antifungal medications may be used for the treatment of Candida albicans fungemia except:","options":{"A":"Amphotericin B","B":"Caspofungin","C":"Fluconazole","D":"Terbinafine","E":"Voriconazole"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks Terbinafine as the exception. The question is testing recognition of the statement that does not fit the expected features of the condition."},{"id":"q_past_267","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"River blindness is due to:","options":{"A":"Toxoplasmosis","B":"Trichinosis","C":"Onchocerciasis","D":"Cysticercosis"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Onchocerciasis due to Onchocerca volvulus is the cause of river blindness."},{"id":"q_past_268","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"One of these fungal infections has bad prognosis if associated with COVID-19:","options":{"A":"Cryptococcosis","B":"Candidiasis","C":"Mucormycosis","D":"Histoplasmosis","E":"Aspergillosis"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Mucormycosis. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_269","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"The most affected WBC of the immune system with HIV infection is:","options":{"A":"CD8 T-lymphocyte","B":"CD4 T-lymphocyte","C":"Plasma cell","D":"Macrophage","E":"CD3 T-lymphocyte"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"HIV preferentially infects CD4 T lymphocytes, causing progressive depletion of this cell population."},{"id":"q_past_270","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"In the developed world, seroprevalence of Helicobacter pylori infection is currently:","options":{"A":"Decreasing","B":"Increasing","C":"Staying the same","D":"Unknown"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks Decreasing. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_271","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Malta fever is caused by:","options":{"A":"Pasteurella tularensis","B":"Haemophilus influenzae","C":"Brucella abortus","D":"Pseudomonas mallei"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Brucella abortus is a major cause of brucellosis and is associated with the classic occupational/animal exposure setting."},{"id":"q_past_272","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Woolsorter''s disease is:","options":{"A":"Plague","B":"Legionnaires'' disease","C":"Klebsiella-induced pneumonia","D":"Anthrax"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Woolsorter ’ s disease is the inhalational form of anthrax associated with contaminated animal products."},{"id":"q_past_273","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Which is not a cause of aseptic fever?","options":{"A":"Crush injury","B":"SLE","C":"AIDS","D":"Pontine haemorrhage"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks AIDS as the exception in this aseptic-fever question."},{"id":"q_past_274","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"The best score in Glasgow coma scale is:","options":{"A":"10","B":"15","C":"20","D":"22","E":"12"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The maximum possible Glasgow Coma Scale score is 15."},{"id":"q_past_275","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"Generalized lymphadenopathy is considered if there is:","options":{"A":"Enlargement of more than two non-contiguous lymph node groups","B":"Enlargement of less than two non-contiguous lymph node groups","C":"Enlargement of more than two contiguous lymph node groups","D":"Enlargement of one lymph node group"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Generalized lymphadenopathy means involvement of more than two non-contiguous lymph-node groups."},{"id":"q_past_276","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group B 22","text":"One of the following is not a method of HIV infection:","options":{"A":"Feco-oral route","B":"IV drug users and sharing needle","C":"Sexual relations","D":"Vertical transmission of infected mothers","E":"Lactation"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks Feco-oral route as the exception. The question is testing recognition of the statement that does not fit the expected features of the condition."},{"id":"q_past_277","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"A 7-year school-boy has been diagnosed with meningococcal meningitis. What is the advice for schoolmates and staff?","options":{"A":"Rifampicin for the whole class and family","B":"Rifampicin for the whole school and family","C":"Meningococcal vaccine for the family","D":"Benzylpenicillin","E":"IV cefotaxime"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Close contacts of meningococcal disease require chemoprophylaxis; rifampicin is the source-marked choice."},{"id":"q_past_278","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"Which one of the following is the most likely presentation of Staphylococcus aureus food poisoning?","options":{"A":"Tenesmus","B":"Watery diarrhoea","C":"Dysentery","D":"Severe vomiting"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks Severe vomiting as the most likely diagnosis/answer for the clinical pattern described."},{"id":"q_past_279","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"All of the following diseases can cause huge splenomegaly except:","options":{"A":"HCV infection","B":"Biharziasis","C":"Thalassemia","D":"Malaria"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks HCV infection as the exception. The question is testing recognition of the statement that does not fit the expected features of the condition."},{"id":"q_past_280","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"Which type of bite represents a potential medical emergency in an asplenic patient?","options":{"A":"Cat bite","B":"Dog bite","C":"Fish bite","D":"Human bite"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"In an asplenic patient, dog bites can be a medical emergency because of severe risk from encapsulated/zoonotic organisms."},{"id":"q_past_281","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"A 47-year-old man was admitted with right flank and right iliac fossa pain, of 1 month ’ s duration. He described weight loss, night sweats, fever and an intermittent non- productive cough on direct questioning. Chest radiograph showed right upper lobe and left mid-zone infiltration. The left cardiac border was indistinct. Small cavities were visible in both infiltrates. what is the most probable diagnosis","options":{"A":"Intestinal T.B","B":"Colorectal cancer","C":"CMV colitis","D":"Bacterial gastroenteritis"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks Intestinal T.B as the most likely diagnosis/answer for the clinical pattern described."},{"id":"q_past_282","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"Rate of transmission of HCV after needle stick injury is:","options":{"A":"1 %","B":"0.3 %","C":"3 %","D":"30 %"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks 3 %. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_283","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"Chronic hepatitis is considered when duration of more than:","options":{"A":"3 months","B":"4 months","C":"5 months","D":"6 months"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks 6 months. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_284","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"A 10 years old child comes to the clinic complaining of fever of 2 days duration, sore throat and fatigue. He reports that there is painful swelling in his neck. His CBC shows lymphocytosis with many atypical cells. What is the appropriate treatment of this patient?","options":{"A":"Symptomatic treatment","B":"corticosteroid","C":"Broad spectrum antibiotic","D":"acyclovir"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks Symptomatic treatment as the treatment/management choice for the clinical situation described."},{"id":"q_past_285","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"A 53-year-old man is evaluated in the hospital for fever. He underwent elective coronary artery bypass graft surgery 2 days ago. urinary catheter was inserted in the operating room. Medical history is unremarkable, and he takes no medications. On physical examination, temperature is 38.4 ° C, blood pressure is 130/84 mm Hg, pulse rate is 88/min, and respiration rate is 18/min. Lungs are clear to auscultation. The sternotomy incision site is clean and dry. He has mild suprapubic tenderness but no costovertebral angle or flank tenderness. Urine analysis reveals 10 to 20 leukocytes/hpf. Urine culture reveals > 400 colonies gram-negative rods. Chest radiograph shows no infiltrate. In addition to removing the urinary catheter, which of the following is the most appropriate management?","options":{"A":"wait antimicrobial sensitivity before beginning antimicrobial therapy","B":"Begin antimicrobial therapy now","C":"Repeat urinalysis and urine culture in 24 hours","D":"Provide no additional treatment"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks Begin antimicrobial therapy now as the treatment/management choice for the clinical situation described."},{"id":"q_past_286","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"Each attack of FMF usually lasts for:","options":{"A":"6 hours to 2 days","B":"12 hours to 3 days","C":"24 hours to 7 days","D":"3 days to 10 days"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks 12 hours to 3 days. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_287","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"Which of the following produce hypothermia","options":{"A":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","B":"Heat stroke","C":"Peripheral circulatory failure","D":"Thyrotoxicosis"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Peripheral circulatory failure. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_288","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"Cinchonism is a possible side effect for:","options":{"A":"Primaquin","B":"Mefloquine","C":"Chloroquine","E":"Quinine"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"The source marks Quinine. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_289","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"A 31-year-old man is evaluated in the emergency department for a 5-day history of abdominal discomfort, vomiting, and loose stools, accompanied by progressively increasing fever. He also has a moderate headache and a rash. On physical examination, temperature is 40 ° C , pulse rate is 64/min, normal blood pressure and respiratory rate, The patient appears sick. There are blanching erythematous macules on the lower chest and upper abdominal wall. The pharynx is erythematous. Abdominal examination reveals tender hepatosplenomegaly. The remainder of the examination is normal .His CBC shows leucopenia with lymphopenia. liver profile shows elevated liver enzymes. Two sets of aerobic blood cultures show gram-negative bacilli . Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Brucellosis","B":"Lassa fever","C":"Leptospirosis","D":"Typhoid fever"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks Typhoid fever as the most likely diagnosis/answer for the clinical pattern described."},{"id":"q_past_290","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"A 45-year-old man presents to the emergency department with nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain. He states that the symptoms are worse with eating, and that his abdominal pain has been increasing over the past three to four months. He reports a history of travel to endemic area with parasites five months ago. After that he developed a cough associated with mild shortness of breath which he attributed to a viral respiratory infection, as it resolved shortly thereafter. His physical examination is significant for moderate periumbilical tenderness. His laboratory results are significant for an eosinophil count of 20 percent. A CT of the abdomen and pelvis showed small bowel dilation proximal to a whirlpool-like mass in the mid-jejunum causing intestinal obstruction. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Clonorchis sinensis","B":"Ascaris lumbricoides","C":"Trichuris trichiura","D":"Cystoisospora belli","E":"Taenia saginata"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks Ascaris lumbricoides as the most likely diagnosis/answer for the clinical pattern described."},{"id":"q_past_291","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"A 19-year-old man plans on traveling to Nigeria. He comes to clinic interested in travel advice and any vaccinations he may need. He has no medical history and takes no medicines. In addition to DEET and mosquito netting, which of the following recommendations would be important for prophylaxis against malaria?","options":{"A":"Tetracyclin","B":"Mefloquine","C":"Primaquin","D":"Atovaquone"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks Mefloquine. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_292","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"One of the following bundle has been shown to reduce ventilator associated pneumonia (VAP ) rates:","options":{"A":"elevate the head of the bed to between 90 and 120 degrees.","B":"daily “ sedation ” and intubation","C":"daily oral care with chlorhexidine.","D":"no prophylaxis for peptic ulcer is needed.","E":"early ambulation to prevent anti-deep venous thrombosis."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks daily oral care with chlorhexidine.. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_293","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"12 patients presented to a local emergency department with similar symptoms of severe nausea, profuse vomiting, and severe abdominal cramps. A few patients reported diarrhea. The patients report that they were all present at a lunch party two hours ago. The dishes were served from slow cookers, and the food handlers did not wear gloves. None of the patients were found to be febrile. The patients were treated with supportive care and hydration. Within 24 hours, all the patients had resolution of their symptoms. What is the most likely cause of this disease outbreak?","options":{"A":"Salmonella infection.","B":"Norovirus infection","C":"S. aureus preformed ingestion toxin","D":"perfringens infection."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks S. aureus preformed ingestion toxin as the exception. The question is testing recognition of the statement that does not fit the expected features of the condition."},{"id":"q_past_294","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"A 24 years old male coming to you complaining of epigastic discomfort and vomiting ,You requested testing for H.pylori which was positive .In addition to PPI .Choose the most suitable antimicrobials","options":{"A":"Ceftriaxone","B":"Clarithromycin, and amoxicillin","C":"Azithromycin","D":"Doxycyclin"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks Clarithromycin, and amoxicillin. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_295","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"Refering to the last question, one month after receiving a 14-day course of the antimicrobials, you prescribed for Helicobacter pylori. He still has mild dyspepsia and pain after meals. What is the appropriate next step in management?","options":{"A":"Empirical long-term proton pump inhibitor therapy","B":"Endoscopy with biopsy to rule out gastric adenocarcinoma","C":"Second-line therapy for H. pylori with omeprazole, bismuth subsalicylate, tetracycline, and metronidazole","D":"Urea breath test","E":"H. pylori serology testing"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks Urea breath test as the treatment/management choice for the clinical situation described."},{"id":"q_past_296","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"All of the following are clinical manifestations of Ascaris lumbricoides infection except:","options":{"A":"asymptomatic carriage","B":"fever, headache, photophobia, neck rigidity, and eosinophilia","C":"non-productive cough and pleurisy with eosinophilia","D":"right upper quadrant pain and fever","E":"small-bowel obstruction"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks fever, headache, photophobia, neck rigidity, and eosinophilia as the exception. The question is testing recognition of the statement that does not fit the expected features of the condition."},{"id":"q_past_297","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"A 19-year-old college student is brought to the emergency department by friends for confusion and altered mental status. They state that many colleagues have upper respiratory tract infections. He does not use alcohol or illicit drugs. His physical examination is notable for confusion, fever, and a rigid neck. Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) examination reveals a white blood cell count of 1800 cells/ μ L with 98% neutrophils, glucose of 1.9 mmol/L (35 mg/dL), and protein of 1.0 g/L (100 mg/dL). What is the most probable diagnosis:","options":{"A":"Bacterial meningitis","B":"Viral meningitis","C":"Viral encephalitis","D":"Tubercoulus meningitis"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Confusion is a recognized additional neurologic manifestation of bacterial meningitis and indicates CNS dysfunction."},{"id":"q_past_298","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"Referring to the last question: Which of the following antibiotic regimens is most appropriate as initial therapy?","options":{"A":"Ampicillin plus vancomycin","B":"Ampicillin plus gentamycin","C":"Cefotaxime plus vancomycin","D":"Cefotaxime plus doxycyclin"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Cefotaxime plus vancomycin. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_299","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"Which of the following statements regarding Clostridium difficile – associated disease relapses is true?","options":{"A":"A first recurrence does not imply greater risk of further recurrences.","B":"Most recurrences are due to antibiotic resistance.","C":"Recurrent C. difficile – associated disease has been associated with a higher risk of colon cancer.","D":"Recurrent disease is associated with serious complications.","E":"Testing for clearance of C. difficile is warranted after treating recurrences."},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks Recurrent disease is associated with serious complications.. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_300","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"Retinochroiditis is caused by eye infection with:","options":{"A":"Loa-loa","B":"Onchocerca volvulous","C":"Demodex folliculorum","D":"Toxoplasma gondi","E":"Toxocara cani"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source identifies Toxoplasma gondi as the causative organism or etiologic answer tested by this question."},{"id":"q_past_301","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 22","text":"A 23-year-old previously healthy female letter carrier works in an area in which the presence of rabid foxes have been documented. She is bitten by a bat, which then flies away. Initial examination reveals a clean break in the skin in the right upper forearm. She has no history of receiving treatment for rabies and is unsure about vaccination against tetanus. The physician should:","options":{"A":"cleans the wound with a 20% soap solution","B":"cleans the wound with a 20% soap solution and administer tetanus toxoid","C":"cleans the wound with a 20% soap solution, administer tetanus toxoid, and administer human rabies immune globulin intramuscularly","D":"clean the wound with a 20% soap solution, administer tetanus toxoid, administer human rabies immune globulin IM, and administer human diploid cell vaccine","E":"clean the wound with a 20% soap solution and administer human diploid cell vaccine"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks cleans the wound with a 20% soap solution, administer tetanus toxoid, and administer human rabies immune globulin intramuscularly as the treatment/management choice for the clinical situation described"},{"id":"q_past_302","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 2025","text":"All of the following are skin manifestations of COVID-19 except:","options":{"A":"Acral papules and vesicles","B":"Pseudo chilblains","C":"Urticarial lesions","D":"Lichen planus"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks Lichen planus as the exception. The question is testing recognition of the statement that does not fit the expected features of the condition."},{"id":"q_past_303","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 2025","text":"Which of the following is the primary mode of transmission of Leishmaniasis to human?","options":{"A":"Mosquito bites","B":"Sandfly bites","C":"Tick bites","D":"Contaminated food or water"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks Sandfly bites. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_304","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 2025","text":"In treatment of taeniasis solium it is preferable to use:","options":{"A":"Praziquantel.","B":"Niclosamide.","C":"Atebrine.","D":"Mebendazole.","E":"Albendazole."},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks Niclosamide. as the treatment/management choice for the clinical situation described."},{"id":"q_past_305","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 2025","text":"River blindness is caused by:","options":{"A":"Filariform larva of Loa loa","B":"Dead adults of Loa loa","C":"Microfilaria of Loa loa","D":"Microfilaria of Onchocerca volvulus."},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source identifies Microfilaria of Onchocerca volvulus. as the causative organism or etiologic answer tested by this question."},{"id":"q_past_306","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 2025","text":"Ocular cysticercosis is caused by:","options":{"A":"Ingestion of undercooked pork containing cysticercous cellulose.","B":"Ingestion of food or water containing egg of Taenia solium.","C":"External autoinfection in a patient has Taeniasis saginata.","D":"Ingestion of undercooked beef containing cysticercous bovis.","E":"Ingestion of undercooked beef containing cysticercous cellulose."},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Cysticercosis is caused by the larval stage of Taenia solium."},{"id":"q_past_307","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 2025","text":"One of the best example of organisms causing One Health Disease is:","options":{"A":"Corynebacterium diphtheriae","B":"Treponema pallidum","C":"Candida albicans","D":"Brucella abortus"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Brucella abortus is a major cause of brucellosis and is associated with the classic occupational/animal exposure setting."},{"id":"q_past_308","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 2025","text":"Who is responsible for implementing Infection Prevention and Control?","options":{"A":"The healthcare facility manager","B":"The healthcare facility''s IPC committee","C":"The healthcare facility IPC","D":"The healthcare facility worker"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks The healthcare facility worker. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_309","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module Group A 2025","text":"A frontline doctor is infected with hepatitis B while at work due to a needle stick injury. What is the legal position of the infection?","options":{"A":"Personal negligence","B":"No-fault compensation event","C":"Ask him to follow standard precautions","D":"Occupational and work-related injury"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks Occupational and work-related injury. This is the key association being 