biotic for community acquired pneumonia in healthy adults is usually:","options":{"A":"Amoxicillin or macrolide (Azithromycin)","B":"Penicillin G","C":"Vancomycin","D":"Linezolid"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks Amoxicillin or macrolide (Azithromycin). This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_480","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"Which of the following antibiotic combination is commonly used in triple therapy of H. pylori infection:","options":{"A":"Amoxicillin + Metronidazole + Tetracycline","B":"Clarithromycin + Amoxicillin + PPI","C":"Metronidazole + Bismuth Tetracycline","D":"Ciprofloxacin Clarithromycin + PPI"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks. Clarithromycin + Amoxicillin + PPI. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_481","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"A 65-year-old patient with nuchal rigidity and headache undergoes lumbar puncture. CSF contains markedly increased number of lymphocytes, normal protein and normal glucose level. The most causative organism is:","options":{"A":"Cysticercosis","B":"H. influenza","C":"Human herpes virus","D":"TB"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source identifies H. influenza as the causative organism or etiologic answer tested by this question."},{"id":"q_past_482","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"According to WHO classification, a category III of rabies exposure (eg: tissue dermal bite or licking of broken skin) in patients who have been previously immunized, which of the following is required:","options":{"A":"No treatment","B":"Vaccine only","C":"Rabies immunoglobulins","D":"Vaccine plus rabies immunoglobulin"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks No treatment. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_483","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"Contamination with contact lenses could lead to eye infection with:","options":{"A":"Acanthamoeba","B":"Loa Loa","C":"Naegleria fowleri","D":"Toxocara canis"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks Acanthamoeba. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_484","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"The earliest marker to appear after HIV infection is:","options":{"A":"Anti-HIV antibodies","B":"HIV p24 antigen","C":"CD4 decline","D":"HIV RNA detectable by PCR"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marksHIV RNA detectable by PCRThis is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_485","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"Which chronic hepatitis virus cannot be prevented by vaccination?","options":{"A":"HAV","B":"HBV","C":"HCV","D":"HEV"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks. HCV as the exception. The question is testing recognition of the statement that does not fit the expected features of the condition."},{"id":"q_past_486","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"First-line treatment for invasive candidiasis in critically ill neutropenic patients is:","options":{"A":"Fluconazole","B":"Amphotericin B","C":"Echinocandin","D":"Ketoconazole"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks Amphotericin B as the treatment/management choice for the clinical situation described."},{"id":"q_past_487","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"Because prompt identification of the cause of encephalitis via PCR is difficult, treatment should not be withheld pending test results and should consist which of the following:","options":{"A":"Acyclovir","B":"Interferon","C":"Immunoglobulins","D":"Prednisone"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks Acyclovir as the exception. The question is testing recognition of the statement that does not fit the expected features of the condition."},{"id":"q_past_488","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"A 60-year-old febrile patient developed sepsis after urinary tract infection, the most common causative organism is:","options":{"A":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa","B":"Escherichia coli","C":"Klebsiella pneumoniae","D":"Staphylococcus aureus"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source identifiesB. Escherichia coli as the causative organism or etiologic answer tested by this question."},{"id":"q_past_489","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"The gold standard diagnostic test for visceral leishmaniasis is:","options":{"A":"rk39 rapid test","B":"Bone marrow / splenic aspiration showing amastigotes","C":"ELISA for anti-Leishmania antibodies","D":"PCR for Leishmania DNA"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks Bone marrow / splenic aspiration showing amastigotes. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_490","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"The most common clinical presentation of cryptococcosis in AIDS patients is:","options":{"A":"Pulmonary nodules","B":"Skin ulcers","C":"Chronic meningitis","D":"Endocarditis"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks AIDS as the exception in this . Chronic meningitis"},{"id":"q_past_491","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"Rift valley fever virus primarily affects which organ in severe human disease?","options":{"A":"Lung and kidneys","B":"Liver and retina","C":"CNS and heart","D":"Spleen and bone marrow"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks B. Liver and retina. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_492","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"Which of the following is a common parasitic cause of lung infection acquired by eating raw or undercooked crustaceans?","options":{"A":"Ascaris lumbricoides","B":"Paragonimus species","C":"Echinococcus granulosus","D":"Strongyloides stercoralis"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source identifies Paragonimus species as the causative organism or etiologic answer tested by this question."},{"id":"q_past_493","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"Descending flaccid paralysis with diplopia and ptosis after food ingestion suggests:","options":{"A":"Clostridium perfringens","B":"Clostridium botulinum","C":"Salmonella","D":"Shigella"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks. Clostridium botulinums. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_494","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"Heat acclimatization:","options":{"A":"Decreases the exercise heart rate","B":"Increases the risk of heat injuries","C":"Increases body temperature","D":"Needs more research before it should even be considered"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks Decreases the exercise heart rate. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_495","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"Which pathogen is known for forming spores and causing difficult-to-eradicate hospital acquired diarrhea?","options":{"A":"C. difficile infection","B":"Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus","C":"Escherichia coli infection","D":"Klebsiella pneumoniae infection"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source identifies C. difficile infection as the causative organism or etiologic answer tested by this question."},{"id":"q_past_496","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"Which zoonotic disease is associated with camels?","options":{"A":"Ebola","B":"MERS-CoV","C":"SARS","D":"Yellow fever"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks MERS-CoV This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_497","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"Alcohol-based hand rubs are ineffective against:","options":{"A":"Gram-negative bacteria","B":"Enveloped viruses","C":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis","D":"Bacterial spores"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks Bacterial spores. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_498","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"Leptospirosis is commonly associated with exposure to:","options":{"A":"Airborne dust","B":"Animal urine - contaminated water","C":"Raw milk","D":"Mosquito bites"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks Airborne dust. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_499","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"Staphylococcal toxins can cause all the following except:","options":{"A":"Bullous impetigo","B":"Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome","C":"Toxic shock syndrome","D":"Erysipelas"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks Erysipelas The question is testing recognition of the statement that does not fit the expected features of the condition."},{"id":"q_past_500","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"The gold standard diagnostic test for malaria remains:","options":{"A":"Rapid antigen test","B":"PCR","C":"Peripheral blood smear","D":"Serology"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Peripheral blood smear. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_501","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group B 26","text":"An infant born to an HIV-positive mother tests positive on ELISA at 2 months. What is the next best step?","options":{"A":"Start ART immediately","B":"Confirm with Western blot","C":"Order HIV DNA PCR","D":"Repeat ELISA at 6 months"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks HIV DNA PCR. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_502","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"A 43-year-old Asian man presents with headache and neck stiffness. CT brain is normal and a lumbar puncture is performed with the following results: Opening pressure Cloudy 3.3 mmol/l 0.7 g/l 100 / mm ³ (70% lymphocytes). What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Bacterial meningitis","B":"Viral meningitis","C":"Tuberculous meningitis","D":"Cryptococcal meningitis"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"A CSF profile showing lymphocytic predominance (70%) with normal glucose (3.3 mmol/L, which is >60% of normal blood glucose) and mildly elevated protein (0.7 g/L) is highly characteristic of viral (aseptic) meningitis"},{"id":"q_past_503","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"The characteristic stool appearance in cholera is described as:","options":{"A":"Bloody with mucus","B":"Foul-smelling and greenish","C":"Rice-water stool","D":"Mucoid with pus"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Vibrio cholerae produces an enterotoxin that causes massive, non-painful secretory diarrhea. The stool classically lacks blood and inflammatory cells, resembling rice- water"},{"id":"q_past_504","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"A 42-year-old dentist is reviewed in the medical clinic complaining of persistent lethargy. Routine blood tests show abnormal liver function tests and a hepatitis screen is sent. HBsAg Positive, Anti-HBc Positive. What do these results most likely demonstrate?","options":{"A":"Hepatitis B infection","B":"Hepatitis C infection","C":"Hepatitis B and C infection","D":"Hepatitis C infection with previous hepatitis B vaccination"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The presence of HBsAg (Hepatitis B Surface Antigen) indicates active infection , while Anti-HBc (Antibody to Hepatitis B Core Antigen) confirms exposure to the actual virus rather than vaccine-induced immunity"},{"id":"q_past_505","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"A health care assistant sustains a needle stick injury whilst taking blood from a patient who is known to be HIV positive. Following thorough washing of the wound what is the most appropriate management?","options":{"A":"HIV test in 3 months to determine treatment","B":"Immediate HIV test to determine treatment","C":"Oral antiretroviral therapy for 4 weeks","D":"Oral antiretroviral therapy for 3 months"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP) for HIV exposure should be initiated as soon as possible (ideally within hours) using a 4-week (28 days) regimen of oral antiretroviral drugs"},{"id":"q_past_506","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"A 29-year-old woman presents with fever 2 weeks after returning from Kenya. Malaria is suspected. Which investigation is the gold standard for diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Rapid antigen test","B":"PCR testing","C":"Thick and thin blood films","D":"Serology"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Microscopic examination of thick and thin peripheral blood smears remains the undisputed gold standard for detecting, quantifying, and identifying Plasmodium species"},{"id":"q_past_507","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"Which of the following is most characteristic of brucellosis?","options":{"A":"Sudden high fever with rigors","B":"Chronic relapsing fever with sweats","C":"Rash and lymphadenopathy","D":"Persistent cough"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_508","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"A 29-year-old woman develops severe vomiting four hours after having lunch at a local restaurant. What is the most likely causative organism?","options":{"A":"Escherichia coli","B":"Campylobacter","C":"Salmonella","D":"Staphylococcus aureus"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_509","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"The surviving sepsis campaign recommends which of the following as part of initial management:","options":{"A":"Immediate corticosteroids administration","B":"Delayed fluid resuscitation","C":"Broad spectrum antibiotics within one hour","D":"Vasopressors before IV fluids"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_510","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"The most common site for reactivation of latent lung TB is:","options":{"A":"Lower lung lobe","B":"Pleura","C":"Middle lung lobe","D":"Apical segment of upper lung lobe"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_511","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"The most common route of human infection with Toxoplasma gondii is:","options":{"A":"Inhalation of droplets from cats","B":"Bite from infected mosquito","C":"Ingestion of undercooked meat containing tissue cysts","D":"Direct human-to-human contact"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_512","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"The following organism is a frequent cause of hospital-acquired pneumonia?","options":{"A":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","B":"Haemophilus influenzae","C":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa","D":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_513","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"Which of the following statements is correct regarding zoonotic viral emerging infections?","options":{"A":"They are always caused by viruses that are new to humans","B":"They often spill over from animals and may mutate for human-to-human spread","C":"They usually have stable transmission and low mutation rates","D":"They never cause epidemics or pandemic"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_514","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"The most common mode of transmission of health care associated infection is:","options":{"A":"Air-borne transmission","B":"Vector-borne transmission","C":"Contact transmission","D":"Food-borne transmission"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_515","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"Gonococcal urethritis can be treated by:","options":{"A":"Ceftriaxone 250 mg intramuscularly for 7 days","B":"Azithromycin 1g daily for 5 days","C":"Single dose of ceftriaxone 250 mg IM plus oral dose of Metronidazole 2 gm","D":"Single dose of ceftriaxone 250 mg IM plus oral dose of azithromycin 1g"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_516","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"River blindness is a clinical manifestation of infection with:","options":{"A":"Acanthamoeba","B":"Onchocerca volvulus","C":"Wuchereria bancrofti","D":"Loa Loa"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_517","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"Which one of the following is the most sensitive and specific test for H. Pylori infection before starting therapy?","options":{"A":"Serology for H. pylori Ab","B":"Rapid urease test for gastric biopsy","C":"Urea breath test","D":"Stool Ag testing"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The Urea Breath Test (UBT) is the most sensitive and specific non-invasive test for detecting active H. pylori infection before treatment. Stool antigen testing is also highly accurate, but UBT remains the non-invasive gold standard ."},{"id":"q_past_518","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"Which of the following is most characteristic of classical dengue fever?","options":{"A":"Gradual onset without headache","B":"Acute high-grade fever, retro-orbital pain, myalgia, and rash","C":"Jaundice and hepatomegaly","D":"Severe diarrhea and dehydration"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Classical Dengue Fever (\"breakbone fever\") is characterized by a sudden onset of high fever, severe headache with classic retro-orbital pain (pain behind the eyes), intense myalgia/arthralgia, and a maculopapular rash."},{"id":"q_past_519","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"Symptoms of heat exhaustion are:","options":{"A":"Profuse sweating and clammy skin","B":"Increased blood pressure","C":"Seizures","D":"Hot, red, dry skin"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":": Heat exhaustion is marked by profuse sweating and cool, pale, clammy skin as the body attempts to cool down. In contrast, hot, red, dry skin and altered mental status are signs of life-threatening heat stroke ."},{"id":"q_past_520","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"All of the following tapeworms are transmitted by ingestion of undercooked meat, EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Taenia saginata","B":"Taenia solium","C":"Diphyllobothrium latum","D":"Echinococcus granulosus"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_521","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"54-year-old neutropenic man undergoing cancer chemotherapy presents with difficult painful swallowing. Endoscopy of the esophagus reveals multiple white plaques with no ulceration. Which organism is most likely the cause:","options":{"A":"Cytomegalovirus","B":"Epstein Barr virus","C":"Candida albicans","D":"Human immunodeficiency virus"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_522","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"How is leptospirosis transmitted?","options":{"A":"Exposure of mucous membranes or abraded skin to the contaminated water","B":"By mosquito bites","C":"By contact with the infected person","D":"By eating properly cooked food"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_523","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"The diagnostic test of choice for detecting microfilariae of W. bancrofti is:","options":{"A":"Thin blood smear (daytime)","B":"Thick blood smear (night sample)","C":"Stool microscopy","D":"Urine antigen test only"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_524","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"A 62-year-old man with uncontrolled diabetes presents with facial pain, swelling around the eye, black nasal discharge, and fever for 5 days. On examination, there is periorbital edema and necrotic nasal mucosa. Question: What is the most likely causative organism?","options":{"A":"Aspergillus fumigatus","B":"Candida albicans","C":"Rhizopus spp.","D":"Cryptococcus neoformans"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_525","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"The most common pathological lesion in intestinal schistosomiasis is:","options":{"A":"Ulceration due to adult worms","B":"Granuloma formation around eggs","C":"Mucosal atrophy","D":"Toxin-mediated necrosis"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_526","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End semester 26","text":"The treatment of choice for visceral leishmaniasis in most endemic areas is:","options":{"A":"Pentavalent antimonials (Sodium stibogluconate)","B":"Amphotericin B (liposomal form)","C":"Miltefosine","D":"All of the above, depending on region and resistance."},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_527","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"The filarial larva can be collected from sample of:","options":{"A":"Liver biopsy","B":"Smear of intestinal contents","C":"Splenic aspirate","D":"Peripheral blood at midnight"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_528","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"In patients with UTI, the ultrasonography indicated in:","options":{"A":"Patients with severe flank pain","B":"Patients with high grade fever","C":"Patients with severe pyuria","D":"Patients not improved after 3 days of treatment"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_529","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"The ''Step-Ladder'' pattern of fever is characteristic of:","options":{"A":"Typhoid fever","B":"Malaria","C":"Brucellosis","D":"Dengue fever"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_530","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"The cornerstone of treatment in Cholera is:","options":{"A":"Antibiotic therapy","B":"Rehydration therapy","C":"Anti spasmodic drugs","D":"Corticosteroids"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_531","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Which of the following is not an immediate complication of meningitis:","options":{"A":"Blindness","B":"Coma","C":"Seizures","D":"Septic shock"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_532","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Which test is the most useful in detecting active Schistosomiasis infection?","options":{"A":"Serological test (ELISA)","B":"Microscopic detection of eggs","C":"Skin test","D":"Complement fixation test"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_533","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Chronic S. haematobium infection may lead to:","options":{"A":"Hepatocellular carcinoma","B":"Bladder squamous cell carcinoma","C":"Colorectal carcinoma","D":"Pancreatic carcinoma"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_534","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"All the following intestinal nematodes inhabit the large intestine except:","options":{"A":"Trichuris trichiura","B":"Enterobius vermicularis","C":"Ascaris lumbricoides","D":"Strongyloides stercoralis"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_535","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Black water fever is special manifestation of malaria caused by:","options":{"A":"P. falciparum","B":"P. malariae","C":"P. ovale","D":"P. vivax"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_536","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"All the following are true regarding leptospirosis except:","options":{"A":"It is zoonosis","B":"Man is the dead-end host","C":"Man is an accidental host","D":"Lice act as reservoirs of infection"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_537","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"The gold standard for TB diagnosis is:","options":{"A":"Tuberculin skin test","B":"Sputum AFB bacilli","C":"Mycobacterial culture","D":"Chest X ray"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_538","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Interferon Gamma Release (IGRA) is preferred over Mantoux test because:","options":{"A":"Being more sensitive in active TB","B":"Not affected by BCG vaccination","C":"Less expensive","D":"Detect all mycobacteria"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_539","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Fever of unknown origin (FUO) in adults is defined as:","options":{"A":"Fever 38.3 ° C on several occasions lasting 3 weeks with no diagnosis after 1 week hospital admission","B":"Fever 37.8 ° C lasting 2 weeks with unknown cause","C":"Fever 38 ° C lasting more than 1 week with no diagnosis after initial tests","D":"Fever 39 ° C lasting 1 week with negative blood cultures"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_540","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"The recommended antibiotic combination to treat brucellosis is:","options":{"A":"Amoxicillin + Clavulanic acid","B":"Doxycycline + Rifampicin","C":"Ciprofloxacin + Metronidazole","D":"Erythromycin + Ceftriaxone"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_541","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Markedly elevated ESR ( ≥ 100) in FUO suggests:","options":{"A":"Factitious fever","B":"Viral infection","C":"Malignancy","D":"Drug induced fever"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_542","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"The first serological marker to appear in acute HBV infection is:","options":{"A":"Anti-HBs","B":"HBcAg","C":"HBsAg","D":"Anti-HBc IgG"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_543","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"The main treatment for acute hepatitis A infection is:","options":{"A":"Antiviral therapy","B":"Interferon","C":"Supportive care only","D":"Ribavirin"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_544","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"The first line antibiotic for community acquired pneumonia in healthy adults is usually:","options":{"A":"Amoxicillin or macrolide (Azithromycin)","B":"Penicillin G","C":"Vancomycin","D":"Linezolid"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_545","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Which of the following antibiotic combination is commonly used in triple therapy of H. pylori infection:","options":{"A":"Amoxicillin + Metronidazole + Tetracycline","B":"Clarithromycin + Amoxicillin + PPI","C":"Metronidazole + Bismuth + Tetracycline","D":"Ciprofloxacin + Clarithromycin + PPI"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_546","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"A 65-year-old patient with nuchal rigidity and headache undergoes lumbar puncture. CSF contains markedly increased number of lymphocytes, normal protein and normal glucose level. The most causative organism is:","options":{"A":"Cysticercosis","B":"H. influenzae","C":"Human herpes virus","D":"TB"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_547","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"According to WHO classification, a category III of rabies exposure requires:","options":{"A":"No treatment","B":"Vaccine only","C":"Rabies immunoglobulins","D":"Vaccine plus rabies immunoglobulins"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_548","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Which of the following regarding P. falciparum is not true:","options":{"A":"Causes more severe disease in pregnancy","B":"Associated with recurrent relapses after initial treatment because of liver hypnozoites","C":"Is the only parasite causing more than 20% parasitemia","D":"Infection is typically associated with thrombocytopenia"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Plasmodium falciparum is the malaria species associated with the most severe disease and major complications."},{"id":"q_past_549","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"The following statements are true regarding leptospirosis except:","options":{"A":"It is zoonosis","B":"Man is the dead-end host","C":"Man is an accidental host","D":"Lice act as reservoirs of infection"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_550","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Which hepatitis virus most commonly causes chronic hepatitis:","options":{"A":"HAV","B":"HBV","C":"HCV","D":"HEV"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source identifies HCV as the causative organism or etiologic answer tested by this question."},{"id":"q_past_551","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"HIV-HBV coinfected patient can be treated with one of the following regimen:","options":{"A":"Dolutegravir + Zidovudine","B":"Dolutegravir + Lopinavir + Entecavir","C":"Dolutegravir + Tenofovir + Lamivudine","D":"Raltegravir + Darunavir + Efavirenz"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Dolutegravir + Tenofovir + Lamivudine. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_552","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Because prompt identification of the cause of encephalitis via PCR is difficult, treatment should not be withheld pending test results and should consist of:","options":{"A":"Acyclovir","B":"Interferon","C":"Immunoglobulins","D":"Prednisone"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_553","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"A 60-year-old ICU patient developed septic shock due to pseudomonas infection. Despite IV fluids, his MAP is less than 65 mmHg. Best next step is:","options":{"A":"Start dopamine infusion","B":"Start norepinephrine infusion","C":"Give corticosteroids first","D":"Use vasopressin as first line"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_554","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"The best diagnostic sample for visceral leishmaniasis is obtained from:","options":{"A":"Peripheral blood smear","B":"CSF","C":"Urine sediment","D":"Bone marrow or splenic aspirate"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_555","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Amphotericin B is used for:","options":{"A":"Viral infection","B":"Severe systemic fungal infection","C":"Helminthic infestation","D":"Protozoal infection"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_556","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Which of the following vaccine can be given post exposure to prevent disease:","options":{"A":"MMR vaccine","B":"HBV vaccine","C":"Rabies vaccine","D":"All the above"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"No explanation is supplied because the source file does not contain a marked answer for this question."},{"id":"q_past_557","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"A 45-year-old man is diagnosed with AIDS. Which of the following statements is true regarding AIDS defining criteria?","options":{"A":"Low CD4+ T cell count (<200 cells/mm ³ )","B":"It occurs at the early stage of HIV infection","C":"The person with AIDS can live up to 10 years","D":"The virus is usually dormant during this stage"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source marks AIDS as the exception in this aseptic-fever question."},{"id":"q_past_558","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"The following does not describe lymphatic filariasis:","options":{"A":"Caused by Brugia malayi and Wuchereria bancrofti","B":"One manifestation is chyluria","C":"Chiefly affects the lower limb","D":"Intermediate vector is mollusc"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks Intermediate vector is mollusc as the exception. The question is testing recognition of the statement that does not fit the expected features of the condition."},{"id":"q_past_559","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Which of the following statements regarding Candida albicans is false?","options":{"A":"Forms germ tubes in serum at 37 ° C","B":"Part of normal flora","C":"Obligate intracellular","D":"Can cause systemic disease in neutropenic patients"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Forms germ tubes in serum at 37 ° C as the exception. The question is testing recognition of the statement that does not fit the expected features of the condition."},{"id":"q_past_560","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Which pathogen is known for forming spores and causing difficult-to-eradicate hospital acquired diarrhea?","options":{"A":"Clostridioides difficile","B":"MRSA","C":"E. coli","D":"Klebsiella pneumoniae"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The source identifies Clostridioides difficile as the causative organism or etiologic answer tested by this question."},{"id":"q_past_561","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Under the One Health framework, which is a key preventive strategy for zoonoses?","options":{"A":"Ignoring animal health","B":"Surveillance of animal populations and environmental reservoirs","C":"Human vaccination only","D":"Eliminate all human-animal interaction"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks Surveillance of animal populations and environmental reservoirs. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_562","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Which of the following is a part of standard precautions?","options":{"A":"Airborne precautions","B":"Contact precautions","C":"Droplet precautions","D":"Hand hygiene"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The source marks Hand hygiene. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_563","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"Which of the following is the preferred initial imaging test in suspected liver hydatid cyst?","options":{"A":"CT scan","B":"MRI","C":"Ultrasound","D":"X-ray"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Ultrasound. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_564","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"What is the characteristic pattern seen on X-ray in a patient with suspected L ö ffler''s syndrome?","options":{"A":"Single large cavity","B":"Diffuse reticulonodular opacities","C":"Solitary pulmonary nodule","D":"Pleural effusion"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks Diffuse reticulonodular opacities. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_past_565","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"A neonate presents with chorioretinitis, hydrocephalus and intracranial calcifications. The most likely diagnosis is:","options":{"A":"CMV infection","B":"Toxoplasmosis","C":"Rubella","D":"Herpes simplex infection"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The source marks Toxoplasmosis as the most likely diagnosis/answer for the clinical pattern described."},{"id":"q_past_566","source":"Past Exam","topic":"End module group A 26","text":"An infant born to an HIV-positive mother tests positive on ELISA at 2 months. What is the next best step?","options":{"A":"Start ART immediately","B":"Confirm with Western blot","C":"Order HIV DNA PCR","D":"Repeat ELISA at 6 months"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The source marks Order HIV DNA PCR. This is the key association being tested by the question."},{"id":"q_college_1","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"The main virulence factor responsible for acid-fastness of Mycobacterium tuberculosis is:","options":{"A":"Peptidoglycan","B":"Mycolic acid","C":"Lipopolysaccharide","D":"Teichoic acid"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Mycolic acids are long-chain fatty acids forming a thick, waxy, lipid-rich coat in the cell envelope of M. tuberculosis. This waxy barrier prevents conventional Gram staining and resists alcohol-acid decolorization once stained with carbolfuchsin (acid-fastness). Peptidoglycan and teichoic acids are typical of standard Gram-positive bacterial cell walls, while lipopolysaccharide (LPS) is characteristic of Gram-negative outer membranes."},{"id":"q_college_2","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"Primary TB lesion in the lung is called:","options":{"A":"Ghon focus","B":"Ranke complex","C":"Simon focus","D":"Cavitary lesion"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"A Ghon focus is the initial subpleural, parenchymal inflammatory/granulomatous lesion formed in the mid-to- lower lung zones following the first exposure to inhaled tubercle bacilli. The Ranke complex represents a late, calcified evolution of this lesion along with involved lymph nodes. Simon foci represent apical seedings from hematogenous spread during primary infection, and cavitary lesions are typical of post-primary (reactivation) disease."},{"id":"q_college_3","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"The combination of Ghon focus and hilar lymph node involvement is:","options":{"A":"Ghonظآs complex","B":"Simonظآs focus","C":"Rankeظآs complex","D":"Pottظآs disease"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The combination of a parenchymal subpleural Ghon focus, inflamed draining lymphatics, and enlarged ipsilateral hilar or paratracheal lymph nodes constitutes the classic Ghon complex (primary complex). Once this complex undergoes fibrous scarring and radiologic calcification, it is designated as a Ranke complex. Pott''s disease is tuberculous spondylitis."},{"id":"q_college_4","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"The most common site for reactivation TB is:","options":{"A":"Lower lobe of lung","B":"Middle lobe","C":"Apical segment of upper lobe","D":"Pleura"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Reactivation (secondary) pulmonary tuberculosis most commonly establishes in the apical and posterior segments of the upper lobes (or the superior segment of the lower lobes). These lung areas have the highest local ventilation-perfusion ($V/Q$) ratios, creating high oxygen tension ($PaO_2$) which favors the survival and replication of strictly aerobic mycobacteria. ."},{"id":"q_college_5","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"Classic triad of pulmonary TB includes:","options":{"A":"Cough, hemoptysis, night sweats","B":"Cough, fever, chest pain","C":"Dyspnea, cough, sputum","D":"Weight gain, fever, rash"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"he classical presentation of chronic pulmonary tuberculosis is characterized by a persistent productive cough (lasting $>3$ weeks), hemoptysis caused by caseating cavitary erosion of bronchial vessels, and prominent systemic B-symptoms, particularly drenching night sweats and significant weight loss."},{"id":"q_college_6","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"Pottظآs disease refers to:","options":{"A":"TB of kidney","B":"TB of lymph nodes","C":"TB of spine","D":"TB meningitis"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Pott''s disease is tuberculosis of the spine (tuberculous spondylitis). Hematogenous seeding usually targets the anterior subchondral margin of the vertebral bodies, leading to disk herniation, collapse of adjacent vertebrae, focal kyphosis (gibbus deformity), and cold abscess formation with potential spinal cord compression.."},{"id":"q_college_7","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"The most common form of extrapulmonary TB worldwide is:","options":{"A":"Renal TB","B":"Lymph node TB","C":"CNS TB","D":"Bone TB"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Tuberculous lymphadenitis (scrofula), typically involving the cervical lymph node chain, is the most common manifestation of extrapulmonary tuberculosis globally. It presents as persistent, painless, non-tender, matted lymphadenopathy that may break down into cold abscesses or draining sinus tracts."},{"id":"q_college_8","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"The gold standard for TB diagnosis is:","options":{"A":"Tuberculin skin test","B":"Sputum AFB smear","C":"Mycobacterial culture","D":"Chest X-ray"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Mycobacterial culture (utilizing solid Lowenstein-Jensen media or automated liquid broth systems) remains the definitive gold standard for tuberculosis diagnosis. It can detect as few as $10$ to $100$ bacilli/mL, confirms mycobacterial viability, differentiates species, and provides essential isolates for automated drug susceptibility testing (DST)."},{"id":"q_college_9","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"Ziehl-Neelsen staining is used to detect:","options":{"A":"Gram-negative bacilli","B":"Acid-fast bacilli","C":"Fungal hyphae","D":"Spirochetes"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The Ziehl-Neelsen (ZN) technique is an acid-fast staining protocol. Heat-driven carbolfuchsin penetrates the thick lipid coat of the bacterial wall; subsequent treatment with acid-alcohol fails to decolorize the acid-fast mycobacteria, leaving them bright red/pink against a contrasting blue (methylene blue) background."},{"id":"q_college_10","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"Mantoux test is considered positive at ظëح5 mm induration in:","options":{"A":"Healthy individuals with no risk","B":"HIV-positive patients","C":"BCG-vaccinated children","D":"General population"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Because patients living with HIV have compromised cell-mediated immunity ($CD4^+$ T-cell depletion), their cutaneous delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction is blunted. Therefore, a lower threshold of $\\ge 5\\text{ mm}$ of palpable induration is designated as positive in HIV-infected individuals, organ transplant recipients, and recent close contacts of active TB cases."},{"id":"q_college_11","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"The standard first-line anti-TB regimen (2HRZE/4HR) includes:","options":{"A":"Isoniazid, Rifampicin, Pyrazinamide, Ethambutol","B":"Streptomycin, Rifampicin, Ethambutol, Isoniazid","C":"Isoniazid, Rifampicin, Ciprofloxacin, Ethambutol","D":"Isoniazid, Rifampicin, Clarithromycin, Pyrazinamide"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"tandard treatment for drug-susceptible tuberculosis consists of an initial intensive phase lasting 2 months with four bactericidal agents: Isoniazid (H), Rifampicin (R), Pyrazinamide (Z), and Ethambutol (E), followed by a 4-month continuation phase of Isoniazid and Rifampicin (2HRZE/4HR) to clear persistent dormant bacilli."},{"id":"q_college_12","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"Hepatotoxicity is seen with all the following EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Isoniazid","B":"Rifampicin","C":"Pyrazinamide","D":"Ethambutol"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"soniazid, Rifampicin, and Pyrazinamide are all hepatotoxic agents (Pyrazinamide carries the highest risk per dose, and Rifampicin frequently induces cholestatic jaundice). In contrast, Ethambutol is metabolized primarily by the kidneys and does not cause significant hepatic injury; its principal dose-dependent toxicity is optic/retrobulbar neuritis."},{"id":"q_college_13","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"Peripheral neuropathy due to isoniazid is prevented by:","options":{"A":"Vitamin C","B":"Vitamin B1","C":"Vitamin B6","D":"Vitamin D"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"soniazid competitively inhibits the enzyme pyridoxine phosphokinase and enhances the urinary excretion of pyridoxine (Vitamin B6). This induced deficiency impairs neurotransmitter synthesis, causing peripheral paresthesias and sensory neuropathy. Prophylactic co-administration of oral pyridoxine prevents this neurotoxicity."},{"id":"q_college_14","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"Interferon-Gamma Release Assays (IGRA) are preferred over the Mantoux test because they:","options":{"A":"Are more sensitive in active TB","B":"Are not affected by BCG vaccination","C":"Are less expensive","D":"Detect all mycobacteria"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"IGRAs (such as QuantiFERON-TB Gold or T-SPOT.TB) measure T-cell release of interferon-gamma in response to specific M. tuberculosis antigens (ESAT-6 and CFP-10). These specific genes are absent from all strains of BCG and most non-tuberculous environmental mycobacteria, eliminating cross-reactivity and false-positive results seen with the tuberculin skin test (PPD)."},{"id":"q_college_15","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"A 30-year-old man comes for review. He lives with a woman who has recently been diagnosed with tuberculosis. The man was born in the UK, has no significant past medical history, and is currently asymptomatic. What is the most appropriate test to check for latent tuberculosis?","options":{"A":"Heaf test","B":"Mantoux test","C":"Sputum culture","D":"Chest X-ray","E":"Interferon-gamma blood test"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"In adult household contacts being evaluated for latent tuberculosis infection (LTBI), an Interferon-Gamma Release Assay (IGRA) is the preferred diagnostic modality due to high specificity, lack of cross-reactivity, and convenience (requiring a single blood draw without a follow-up 48-72 hour in-person clinic visit)."},{"id":"q_college_16","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"A 25-year-old woman has recently moved to the United Kingdom from sub-Saharan Africa to attend university. She comes from an area where there is a high prevalence of tuberculosis (TB). The patient is not pregnant and is currently asymptomatic. She thinks she may have had a BCG vaccination in the past but is not sure. She has no other medical history and is a non-smoker. Which test should initially be used to screen this lady for TB?","options":{"A":"Mantoux test","B":"Interferon-gamma blood test","C":"Early morning urine sample","D":"Chest X-ray","E":"Send three sputum samples"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Screening immigrants or entrants from high-incidence countries who have a previous or uncertain BCG vaccination status is best conducted using an IGRA. The Mantoux test frequently yields false-positive results in individuals with prior BCG immunization, whereas IGRA antigens (ESAT-6, CFP-10) are completely unaffected by prior BCG exposure."},{"id":"q_college_17","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"All of the following factors influence the likelihood of transmitting active tuberculosis EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Duration of contact with an infected person","B":"Environment in which contact occurs","C":"Presence of extrapulmonary tuberculosis","D":"Presence of laryngeal tuberculosis","E":"Probability of contact with an infectious person"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Transmission of M. tuberculosis occurs via airborne droplets generated by coughing, shouting, or singing from individuals with infectious pulmonary or laryngeal disease. Isolated extrapulmonary tuberculosis (e.g., lymph nodes, bones, kidneys) is non-contagious and does not generate infectious respiratory aerosols."},{"id":"q_college_18","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"All of the following statements regarding BCG vaccination are true EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"BCG dissemination may occur in severely immunosuppressed patients.","B":"BCG vaccination is recommended at birth in countries with high TB prevalence.","C":"BCG vaccination may cause a false-positive tuberculin skin test.","D":"BCG vaccine provides protection for infants and children from TB meningitis and miliary disease.","E":"BCG vaccine provides protection from TB in HIV-infected patients."},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"The BCG vaccine is a live attenuated strain of Mycobacterium bovis. It is contraindicated in symptomatic HIV-infected or immunocompromised individuals due to the life-threatening risk of disseminated BCG disease (BCG-osis). It does not provide reliable protection in HIV-infected patients. Its proven efficacy lies in protecting infants and young children against severe, disseminated pediatric manifestations (meningitis and miliary TB)."},{"id":"q_college_19","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"A 25-year-old woman has recently moved to the United Kingdom from sub-Saharan Africa to attend university. She comes from an area where there is a high prevalence of tuberculosis (TB). The patient is not pregnant and is currently asymptomatic. She thinks she may have had a BCG vaccination in the past but is not sure. She has no other medical history and is a non-smoker. A chest X-ray is normal. She has a Mantoux test which is positive and subsequently an interferon- gamma release assay, which is also positive. What is the best management option for this patient?","options":{"A":"Isoniazid and pyridoxine for 6 months","B":"No treatment indicated at present","C":"Rifampicin and isoniazid with pyridoxine for 6 months","D":"Arrange a bronchoscopy and lavage","E":"Rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol for 6 months"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Once active disease has been ruled out (asymptomatic status, normal chest radiograph), confirmed latent tuberculosis infection is treated with preventive therapy to avert future reactivation. Standard regimens include isoniazid monotherapy with pyridoxine or combined rifampicin-isoniazid therapy to eliminate non- replicating bacilli sequestered in resting granulomas."},{"id":"q_college_20","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"A 50-year-old man is admitted to the hospital for active pulmonary tuberculosis. He is HIV positive. What is the most appropriate initial therapy?","options":{"A":"Isoniazid, rifampin, ethambutol, and pyrazinamide","B":"Isoniazid, rifampin, ethambutol, and pyrazinamide; initiate antiretroviral therapy","C":"Isoniazid, rifampin, ethambutol, pyrazinamide, and streptomycin","D":"Isoniazid, rifampin, and ethambutol","E":"Withhold therapy until sensitivities are available."},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Management of concurrent HIV and active tuberculosis requires full standard quadruple anti-TB therapy (Rifampin, Isoniazid, Pyrazinamide, and Ethambutol) alongside early implementation of antiretroviral therapy (ART). Initiating anti-TB therapy promptly curtails disease progression, while integrating ART significantly improves overall survival in immunocompromised patients.."},{"id":"q_college_21","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"A 20-year-old girl is taking anti-tuberculosis treatment. She presents with polyneuropathy. The most likely cause of her symptoms is a side effect of:","options":{"A":"Isoniazid","B":"Rifampicin","C":"Ethambutol","D":"Pyrazinamide","E":"Streptomycin"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Isoniazid (INH) interferes with vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) metabolism by forming inactive hydrazones and inhibiting pyridoxal phosphokinase. The resulting pyridoxine deficiency impairs peripheral nerve myelin maintenance, leading to length-dependent, symmetric sensory polyneuropathy presenting as distal \"glove- and-stocking\" numbness and paresthesias."},{"id":"q_college_22","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"A 39-year-old female who has recently emigrated from sub-Saharan Africa is screened for tuberculosis. She reports being fit and well with no past medical history and has never had a BCG vaccination. Her chest X-ray is normal, so she has a Mantoux test, which is positive. An interferon- gamma test is also performed, which is positive. An HIV test is requested, which is negative. What treatment would you recommend?","options":{"A":"3 months of isoniazid (with pyridoxine) and rifampicin OR 6 months of isoniazid (with pyridoxine)","B":"Rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol for 6 months","C":"Observe","D":"Rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol for 2 months, then step down to rifampicin and isoniazid for 4 months","E":"3 months of pyrazinamide and isoniazid OR 6 months of pyrazinamide"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The patient has confirmed latent tuberculosis infection (positive Mantoux and IGRA, clear chest radiograph, no symptoms). International guidelines (including NICE and WHO) recommend either a 3-month course of daily rifampicin and isoniazid with pyridoxine (3HR) or a 6-month course of daily isoniazid with pyridoxine (6H). Pyrazinamide is not utilized in latent TB preventive protocols due to high risks of fatal hepatotoxicity."},{"id":"q_college_23","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"A 36-year-old woman is evaluated for a six-week history of weight loss, fever, night sweating, and productive cough. Chest X-ray shows a small cavity in the right and left upper lobes with hilar infiltrates. Sputum culture is positive for acid-fast bacilli. The appropriate initial treatment will be:","options":{"A":"Clarithromycin and ethambutol","B":"Isoniazid","C":"Isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol","D":"Rifampicin, levofloxacin, and pyrazinamide"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"This patient presents with active, smear/culture-positive, cavitary pulmonary tuberculosis. Standard empirical first-line therapy for active TB requires a four-drug induction regimen consisting of Isoniazid, Rifampicin, Pyrazinamide, and Ethambutol (HRZE) for 2 months to rapidly reduce the high bacterial burden and prevent the emergence of drug-resistant mutants."},{"id":"q_college_24","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"A 67-year-old man is referred to the respiratory clinic. He has a past history of tuberculosis as a child but is otherwise fit and well. Over the past two months, he has had a cough, lost one stone in weight, and had four episodes of hemoptysis. A chest X-ray shows a solid mass occupying the right upper zone. Investigation results include: Aspergillus precipitin antibody: Positive What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Lung abscess","B":"Invasive aspergillosis","C":"Aspergilloma","D":"Reactivation of primary tuberculosis","E":"Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"An aspergilloma (mycetoma or fungal ball) is formed by saprophytic colonization of a pre-existing healed pulmonary cavity (most commonly secondary to past tuberculosis). It typically presents with recurrent hemoptysis in an otherwise non-immunocompromised patient, with positive serum Aspergillus precipitin (IgG) antibodies and a rounded upper-lobe mass inside a cavity."},{"id":"q_college_25","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"A patient with a previous history of tuberculosis now complains of hemoptysis. Chest X-ray reveals an upper lobe mass with a cavity and a crescent-shaped air-fluid level. What is the etiology?","options":{"A":"Aspergillus flavus","B":"Coccidioides immitis","C":"Human parvovirus","D":"Cytomegalovirus (CMV)"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The classic radiographic hallmark of an aspergilloma is the \"Monod sign\" or \"air crescent sign,\" representing air rimming around a mobile fungal ball inside a chronic tuberculous cavity. The tangled mass of hyphae, fibrin, and cellular debris is most commonly formed by Aspergillus species (Aspergillus fumigatus or Aspergillus flavus)."},{"id":"q_college_26","source":"College MCQ","topic":"TUBERCULOSIS – TB","text":"The most common site of abdominal tuberculosis is:","options":{"A":"Ileocecal region","B":"Small intestine","C":"Stomach","D":"Esophagus"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The ileocecal area is the most frequent site of gastrointestinal tuberculosis (accounting for >75% of cases) due to the high density of lymphoid tissue (Peyer''s patches), physiological stasis, and prolonged mucosal contact."},{"id":"q_college_27","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Inflammation of the brain parenchyma is referred to as:","options":{"A":"Meningitis","B":"Myelitis","C":"Encephalitis","D":"Meningoencephalitis"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Encephalitis is defined specifically as inflammation of the brain parenchyma itself, typically manifesting with altered mental status, motor or sensory deficits, and seizures. In contrast, meningitis represents inflammation confined to the meninges, myelitis refers to spinal cord inflammation, and meningoencephalitis denotes concurrent involvement of both the meningeal coverings and brain tissue."},{"id":"q_college_28","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Meningitis reflects infection of the:","options":{"A":"Arachnoid mater and pia mater","B":"Arachnoid mater and subarachnoid space","C":"Dura mater and arachnoid mater","D":"Subarachnoid space and pia mater"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Meningitis pathologically involves the leptomeninges (the delicate inner membranes: arachnoid and pia mater) along with the CSF-filled subarachnoid space that lies between them. Infection and inflammatory exudates diffuse throughout this space, bathing the pial vessels and adjacent cortical surfaces."},{"id":"q_college_29","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Death could result if meningitis is caused by:","options":{"A":"Virus","B":"Bacteria","C":"Fungus","D":"Parasites"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"cute pyogenic bacterial meningitis carries a substantial mortality rate and severe risk of long-term neurologic sequelae due to profound subarachnoid purulent inflammation, rapid cerebral edema, intracranial hypertension, and septic shock. By comparison, viral meningitis follows an overwhelmingly benign, self- limited clinical course"},{"id":"q_college_30","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Which of the following is a medical emergency with high mortality and morbidity?","options":{"A":"Viral meningitis","B":"Aseptic meningitis","C":"Bacterial meningitis","D":"TB meningitis"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Bacterial meningitis is an acute infectious emergency requiring immediate recognition and parenteral antimicrobial therapy within minutes of presentation. Without rapid intervention, bacterial proliferation and the resulting host cytokine cascade lead to neuronal apoptosis, cerebral ischemia, cortical herniation, and death."},{"id":"q_college_31","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Risk factors for meningitis include the following, EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Extremes of age (<5 years or >60 years)","B":"Diabetes mellitus","C":"Pregnancy","D":"Chronic kidney failure"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Extremes of age (<5 years and >60 years), diabetes mellitus, and chronic kidney failure are established systemic risk factors that compromise baseline humoral and cellular immunity, predisposing patients to encapsulated bacterial meningitis. While pregnancy alters cell-mediated immunity (predisposing to Listeria monocytogenes), it is classically excluded as a general host risk factor compared to severe chronic metabolic or organ failures."},{"id":"q_college_32","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"An infectious agent can gain access to the CNS and cause meningeal disease via any of the following pathways, EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Hematogenous spread","B":"Retrograde neuronal spread","C":"Ingestion","D":"Direct contiguous spread"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Pathogens reach the central nervous system through three defined anatomical routes: hematogenous seeding following mucosal colonization (the most common route), direct contiguous spread from adjacent structures (e.g., otitis media, sinusitis, or skull fractures), and retrograde axonal transport along cranial or peripheral nerves (e.g., rabies, Naegleria). Ingestion is a portal of entry into the GI tract (e.g., Listeria, enteroviruses) but not a direct physical pathway into the meninges."},{"id":"q_college_33","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Which of the following bacteria is not a cause of bacterial meningitis?","options":{"A":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","B":"Neisseria meningitidis","C":"Escherichia coli","D":"Haemophilus influenzae"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria meningitidis, and Haemophilus influenzae are the classic primary causes of community-acquired bacterial meningitis in immunocompetent children and adults. While Escherichia coli is an important pathogen in neonates (<3 months) and post-neurosurgical states, it is not a typical cause of community-acquired meningitis in the general older pediatric and adult population."},{"id":"q_college_34","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Which of the following organisms causes epidemic meningitis in military recruits?","options":{"A":"Haemophilus influenzae type b","B":"Neisseria meningitidis","C":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","D":"Listeria monocytogenes"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Neisseria meningitidis (meningococcus) is transmitted via respiratory droplets and uniquely causes explosive, localized outbreaks and epidemics in closed, crowded environments such as military barracks, college dormitories, and refugee camps. S. pneumoniae occurs predominantly as sporadic cases rather than contagious outbreaks."},{"id":"q_college_35","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Which of the following groups of viruses is most likely to cause meningitis?","options":{"A":"Adenoviruses","B":"Human papillomaviruses","C":"Poxviruses","D":"Enteroviruses"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Non-polio enteroviruses (including Coxsackieviruses A and B, and Echoviruses) account for the vast majority (>80ظô90%) of all identified cases of viral (aseptic) meningitis worldwide, particularly during summer and autumn months."},{"id":"q_college_36","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Aseptic meningitis is diagnosed when:","options":{"A":"Organisms are demonstrated by Gram stain but not by bacterial cultures","B":"No organisms are demonstrated by Gram stain but detected by bacterial cultures","C":"Organisms are demonstrated by Gram stain and bacterial cultures","D":"No organisms are demonstrated by Gram stain or bacterial cultures"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Aseptic meningitis is a clinical syndrome characterized by meningeal inflammation in which standard CSF Gram stains and routine pyogenic bacterial cultures demonstrate no growth. It is most commonly caused by viral pathogens, but may also stem from adverse drug reactions or systemic autoimmune disorders."},{"id":"q_college_37","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Classic symptoms of meningitis usually include:","options":{"A":"Backache, hallucinations, indigestion","B":"Rash, inner ear pain, itching","C":"Dry skin, dehydration, cold sores","D":"Fever, headache, stiff neck"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The classic symptomatic triad of acute meningitis consists of high fever, severe generalized headache, and nuchal rigidity (stiff neck), resulting from acute purulent irritation and tension on the inflamed cervical nerve roots and leptomeninges."},{"id":"q_college_38","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"The classic triad of meningitis requires the following to be present, EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Fever","B":"Cranial nerve abnormalities","C":"Nuchal rigidity","D":"Altered mental status"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The diagnostic triad defining acute bacterial meningitis consists of fever, nuchal rigidity, and altered mental status (confusion, lethargy, or coma). While cranial nerve palsies (especially III, IV, VI, and VII) may arise as localized complications due to basal inflammatory exudates, they are not part of the defining primary triad."},{"id":"q_college_39","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Which of the following signs is not a sign of meningeal irritation?","options":{"A":"Nuchal rigidity","B":"Kernigظآs sign","C":"Babinskiظآs sign","D":"Brudzinskiظآs sign"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Nuchal rigidity, Kernig''s sign (resistance/pain upon passive knee extension with the hip flexed), and Brudzinski''s sign (involuntary flexion of the hips and knees upon passive neck flexion) are classic bedside maneuvers assessing meningeal inflammation. Babinski''s sign indicates an upper motor neuron (pyramidal tract) lesion, not meningeal irritation."},{"id":"q_college_40","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Non-blanching petechiae and cutaneous hemorrhages may be present in meningitis caused by:","options":{"A":"Viruses","B":"Parasites","C":"Bacteria","D":"TB"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"A non-blanching petechial or purpuric rash is characteristic of bacterial meningitis, classically caused by Neisseria meningitidis (meningococcemia). It results from acute bacterial endotoxemia, microvascular thrombosis, leukocyte plugging, and subsequent capillary fragility/hemorrhage into the dermis."},{"id":"q_college_41","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 26-year-old female presented with fever, malaise, headache, and neck stiffness for the last two days. On examination, which cranial nerves can be affected?","options":{"A":"I, II, III","B":"III, IV, VI","C":"V, IX, X","D":"VIII, X, XI"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The ocular motor nervesظ¤cranial nerves III (oculomotor), IV (trochlear), and VI (abducens)ظ¤are the most frequently compromised cranial nerves in acute meningitis. They traverse the subarachnoid space and cavernous sinus, rendering them vulnerable to direct inflammatory entrapment in purulent basal exudates and compression from raised intracranial pressure."},{"id":"q_college_42","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Which procedure is most likely used to test for meningitis?","options":{"A":"Throat culture","B":"Lumbar puncture","C":"Tuberculosis skin test","D":"Urine analysis"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Lumbar puncture (LP) with cerebrospinal fluid aspiration and analysis is the definitive diagnostic procedure for confirming meningitis. It allows immediate evaluation of CSF opening pressure, cell differential count, protein and glucose concentrations, Gram staining, and microbiological culture."},{"id":"q_college_43","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Which of the following is not an immediate complication of meningitis?","options":{"A":"Septic shock","B":"Blindness","C":"Coma","D":"Seizures"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Acute/immediate complications of bacterial meningitis occur within the first hours to days of presentation and include status epilepticus, septic shock, acute cerebral herniation, and coma. Blindness is a chronic, late- stage sequela resulting from secondary optic atrophy, prolonged papilledema, or organized arachnoiditis."},{"id":"q_college_44","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"The differential diagnoses of meningitis include the following, EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Encephalitis","B":"Brain neoplasms","C":"Pneumonia","D":"Stroke"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Neurological emergencies that mimic meningitis by presenting with acute headache, altered mental status, meningism, or focal deficits include encephalitis, acute ischemic or hemorrhagic stroke, and intracranial neoplasms. Uncomplicated pneumonia presents primarily with lower respiratory symptoms without primary central nervous system dysfunction."},{"id":"q_college_45","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Blood culture is positive in meningitis caused by:","options":{"A":"Neisseria meningitidis","B":"TB","C":"Aseptic meningitis","D":"Parasites"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Because meningococcal meningitis is initiated by hematogenous dissemination following mucosal colonization of the nasopharynx, blood cultures yield positive bacterial growth in up to 50ظô75% of untreated Neisseria meningitidis cases. In contrast, blood cultures are routinely sterile in viral (aseptic), tuberculous, and parasitic meningitides."},{"id":"q_college_46","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A lumbar puncture needle for CSF analysis is inserted between:","options":{"A":"L1ظôL2","B":"L3ظôL4","C":"L4ظôL5","D":"L5ظôS1"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"In adults, the spinal cord (conus medullaris) terminates at the lower border of the L1 or upper L2 vertebra. To safely access the subarachnoid space and cauda equina without risking spinal cord puncture, lumbar puncture needles are inserted at the L3ظôL4 or L4ظôL5 intervertebral spaces (identified clinically along Tuffier''s line connecting the superior aspects of the iliac crests)."},{"id":"q_college_47","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Which of the following CSF results suggests bacterial meningitis?","options":{"A":"Elevated CSF lymphocyte count, normal protein, and normal glucose","B":"Elevated CSF neutrophil count, raised protein, and low glucose","C":"Elevated CSF lymphocyte count, raised protein, and low glucose","D":"No CSF cell count, normal protein, and normal glucose"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The classic CSF profile of acute pyogenic bacterial meningitis demonstrates a marked pleocytosis dominated by polymorphonuclear neutrophils (>80%), significantly elevated total protein (>100 mg/dL) due to blood-brain barrier breakdown, and a low glucose level (<40% of simultaneous serum glucose) consumed by bacterial metabolism and impaired hexose transport."},{"id":"q_college_48","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Which of the following is true about the CSF changes in patients with tuberculous meningitis?","options":{"A":"The cell count is often elevated, and the cells are mainly neutrophils","B":"The cell count is normal, and the cells are mainly neutrophils","C":"The cell count is often elevated, and the cells are mainly lymphocytes","D":"The cell count is normal, and the cells are mainly lymphocytes"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Tuberculous meningitis typically produces a chronic subacute CSF pattern characterized by a moderate-to- high mononuclear pleocytosis dominated by lymphocytes (often alongside markedly elevated protein and profoundly depressed glucose levels). A neutrophilic predominance is typical of acute bacterial meningitis"},{"id":"q_college_49","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with a 1-day history of severe headache, fever, discomfort when looking at lights, and neck stiffness. He has had diabetes mellitus for 10 years. There is a non-blanching rash on his trunk. On examination, his temperature is 39┬░C. The most likely causative organism is:","options":{"A":"Strongyloides stercoralis","B":"Neisseria meningitidis","C":"Human herpesvirus","D":"TB"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The combination of acute onset meningeal signs (headache, fever, photophobia, nuchal rigidity) accompanied by a characteristic petechial/purpuric non-blanching rash is the pathognomonic presentation of Neisseria meningitidis bacteremia and meningitis."},{"id":"q_college_50","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 45-year-old male known to be HIV-positive presented with fever, malaise, headache, and neck stiffness for the last two days. He undergoes lumbar puncture. The CSF contains markedly increased numbers of neutrophils, high protein, and low glucose. What is the antibiotic treatment of choice?","options":{"A":"Ampicillin plus cefotaxime","B":"Ampicillin plus aminoglycoside","C":"Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone","D":"Vancomycin plus ampicillin plus ceftriaxone"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"In immunocompromised patients (including HIV-infected adults), the empirical antimicrobial regimen must cover standard community pathogens (S. pneumoniae and N. meningitidis) as well as Listeria monocytogenes. Ceftriaxone provides broad Gram-negative and standard pneumococcal coverage, Vancomycin protects against cephalosporin-resistant pneumococci, and Ampicillin is obligatory to treat Listeria, which possesses intrinsic resistance to all cephalosporins."},{"id":"q_college_51","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Lumbar puncture with CSF analysis is the most definitive method for diagnosing meningitis. However, sometimes lumbar puncture is delayed so that contrast-enhanced CT or MRI can be performed. For which of the following conditions is this delay most likely implemented?","options":{"A":"Bleeding disorder","B":"Increased intracranial pressure","C":"Infection at the needle insertion site","D":"Parameningeal lumbar infection"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"When significant focal intracranial mass effect or markedly raised intracranial pressure is suspected (suggested by focal neurological deficits, new-onset seizures, or papilledema), neuroimaging must precede lumbar puncture to evaluate the risk of precipitating fatal brainstem herniation caused by a sudden lumbar pressure gradient."},{"id":"q_college_52","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Meningitis is defined as inflammation of the meninges and subarachnoid space and may be classified into several categories. Which of the following types is particularly serious because of the speed of its progression?","options":{"A":"Acute bacterial meningitis","B":"Aseptic meningitis","C":"Noninfectious meningitis","D":"Viral meningitis"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Acute bacterial meningitis can progress from mild initial constitutional symptoms to catastrophic neurological damage, septic shock, and death within 24 hours. Viral and aseptic forms typically follow an indolent, subacute, and self-limiting trajectory."},{"id":"q_college_53","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"When diagnosing meningitis, which of the following findings is a key indicator of meningeal irritation?","options":{"A":"Fever","B":"Headache","C":"Myalgia","D":"Nuchal rigidity"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Nuchal rigidity (inability to passively flex the neck forward onto the sternum due to involuntary spasm of extensor spinal musculature) is the classic physical hallmark reflecting inflammation and stretch irritation of the cervical meninges. Fever, headache, and myalgia are non-specific constitutional features seen across numerous infectious syndromes."},{"id":"q_college_54","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"If a patient has bacterial meningitis, which of the following findings will be seen on cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis?","options":{"A":"Elevated glucose levels","B":"Elevated protein levels","C":"Low white blood cell count","D":"Low percentage of neutrophils"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Severe inflammation and endothelial damage along the blood-brain barrier cause large amounts of serum albumin and immunoglobulins to leak into the subarachnoid space, producing marked elevations in CSF protein levels (frequently $>100-500\\text{ mg/dL}$) alongside a marked neutrophilic pleocytosis and low glucose."},{"id":"q_college_55","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Vaccines are available that can protect against some types of meningitis. Which of the following is an example of a meningitis vaccine?","options":{"A":"MMR vaccine","B":"Influenza vaccine","C":"Pneumococcal vaccine","D":"COVID-19 vaccine"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Pneumococcal conjugate (PCV13/15/20) and polysaccharide (PPSV23) vaccines directly target Streptococcus pneumoniae, the leading cause of bacterial meningitis across all age groups beyond early infancy. They dramatically reduce invasive pneumococcal disease and bacteremia."},{"id":"q_college_56","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Which of the following is a complication of severe bacterial meningitis?","options":{"A":"Improved vision","B":"Reduced risk of infection","C":"Brain damage","D":"Faster recovery time"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Complications of severe bacterial meningitis encompass irreversible parenchymal brain damage, sensorineural hearing loss, hydrocephalus, motor deficits, cognitive impairment, and focal epilepsy arising from vasculitis, cortical venous thrombosis, and cerebral ischemia."},{"id":"q_college_57","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Which of the following is a positive sign of meningitis when performing Brudzinski''s test?","options":{"A":"The patient''s knees and hips flex involuntarily when the neck is flexed","B":"The patient''s arms reflexively move away from the chest","C":"The patient experiences pain when pressing on the forehead","D":"The patient''s pupils dilate"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Brudzinski''s neck sign is elicited with the patient lying supine. Passive forced flexion of the neck pulls on the inflamed dura and spinal cord, triggering an involuntary reflex flexion of both the hips and knees to relieve meningeal tension."},{"id":"q_college_58","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"What is a classic symptom of meningitis?","options":{"A":"Sore throat","B":"Stiff neck","C":"Rash on the trunk","D":"Swollen lymph nodes in the neck"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"A stiff neck (nuchal rigidity) is the cardinal physical sign of meningitis, resulting from painful reflex contracture of the paraspinal muscles upon stretching of inflamed leptomeninges."},{"id":"q_college_59","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"What is the definitive diagnostic test for meningitis?","options":{"A":"CT scan of the brain","B":"Blood test","C":"Lumbar puncture (spinal tap)","D":"MRI of the brain"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Lumbar puncture provides direct access to cerebrospinal fluid for microscopic cytocentrifugation, biochemical quantification (protein, glucose), Gram staining, bacterial antigen testing, and culture, making it the definitive standard to confirm or exclude meningitis."},{"id":"q_college_60","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"What are the most common causes of meningitis in the United States?","options":{"A":"Bacteria and parasites","B":"Viruses","C":"Fungi","D":"Prions"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Overall, viruses (primarily enteroviruses such as coxsackievirus and echovirus, followed by HSV-2 and arboviruses) cause the majority of all meningitis cases annually in the United States and worldwide, significantly exceeding the incidence of bacterial and fungal infections."},{"id":"q_college_61","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 43-year-old Asian man presents with headache and neck stiffness. CT brain is normal, and a lumbar puncture is performed with the following results: Serum glucose: 4.7 mmol/L Lumbar puncture reveals: Opening pressure: 15 cm CSF Appearance: Cloudy Glucose: 3.3 mmol/L Protein: 0.7 g/L White cells: 100/mm┬│ (70% lymphocytes) What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Bacterial meningitis","B":"Viral meningitis","C":"Tuberculous meningitis","D":"Normal CSF result","E":"Cryptococcal meningitis"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The CSF demonstrates a lymphocytic pleocytosis (100 cells/$\\text{mm}^3$ with 70% lymphocytes) alongside a normal CSF-to-serum glucose ratio ($3.3 / 4.7 = 0.7$, well above the normal $>0.6$ threshold) and only mildly elevated protein ($0.7\\text{ g/L}$). This normal glucose preservation paired with a modest lymphocytic response is characteristic of viral (aseptic) meningitis, whereas TB and cryptococcal infections cause marked CSF glucose depletion."},{"id":"q_college_62","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 28-year-old man from Zimbabwe presents to the emergency department with a 2-week history of fever, cough, headache, vomiting, and neck stiffness. He is known to be HIV-positive and is on treatment. His most recent CD4 count was 450 cells/mm┬│. On examination, he has no focal neurological signs but appears drowsy and confused. You suspect meningitis and perform a lumbar puncture. The results show: Opening pressure: 25 mm HظééO Appearance: Cloudy White cells: 200 cells/mm┬│ Cells: 90% lymphocytes CSF protein: 3 g/L CSF glucose: 1.1 mmol/L Blood glucose: 6.8 mmol/L What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Meningococcal meningitis","B":"TB meningitis","C":"Cryptococcal meningitis","D":"Partially treated bacterial meningitis","E":"Herpes simplex meningitis"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"A subacute presentation (2-week duration) in a patient from an endemic region presenting with lymphocytic pleocytosis (90% lymphocytes), markedly elevated protein ($3\\text{ g/L}$), and severely reduced glucose ratio ($1.1 / 6.8 = 0.16$, $<0.3$) is the classic profile of tuberculous meningitis. Cryptococcal meningitis typically occurs in profoundly immunosuppressed patients ($CD4 < 100\\text{ cells/mm}^3$) and often shows minimal cellular reaction."},{"id":"q_college_63","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Which of the following types of viral meningitis may be characteristically associated with a low cerebrospinal fluid glucose level?","options":{"A":"Mumps","B":"Cytomegalovirus","C":"Measles","D":"HIV","E":"Echovirus"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"While the majority of viral meningitides exhibit completely normal CSF glucose levels, specific viral etiologiesظ¤notably Cytomegalovirus (CMV), Mumps, and occasionally HSVظ¤can present atypically with hypoglycorrhachia (low CSF glucose), mimicking tuberculous or fungal diseas"},{"id":"q_college_64","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 19-year-old man presents with a two-day history of diffuse headache and sore throat. He is pyrexial at 37.8┬░C and is reluctant to have fundoscopy due to photophobia. A lumbar puncture is performed. Serum glucose: 5.9 mmol/L Lumbar puncture reveals: Appearance: Clear Glucose: 4.1 mmol/L Protein: 0.3 g/L White cells: Lymphocytes: 2/mm┬│ Polymorphs: 0/mm┬│ What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Guillain-Barr├ر syndrome","B":"Viral meningitis","C":"Bacterial meningitis","D":"Cerebral malaria","E":"Normal CSF result"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"Normal adult CSF reference values are: WBC count $<5/\\text{mm}^3$ (all mononuclear), protein between $0.15-0.45\\text{ g/L}$, and CSF glucose $>60\\%$ of concurrent serum glucose ($4.1 / 5.9 = 0.69$). All CSF values in this patient fall entirely within physiologic limits, confirming a normal CSF study."},{"id":"q_college_65","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 20-year-old woman was admitted overnight with suspected meningitis. You are asked to review the initial microscopy results from the lumbar puncture. The laboratory reports that the culture is growing gram-negative diplococci. What is the most likely organism?","options":{"A":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","B":"Listeria monocytogenes","C":"Escherichia coli","D":"Haemophilus influenzae","E":"Neisseria meningitidis"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"Neisseria meningitidis is a Gram-negative diplococcus characteristically appearing as paired, kidney-bean- shaped cocci within or outside neutrophils. S. pneumoniae is a Gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococcus, H. influenzae is a pleomorphic Gram-negative coccobacillus, and Listeria is a Gram-positive rod."},{"id":"q_college_66","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"An 18-year-old male is admitted with fever, headache, and neck stiffness. He receives intravenous ceftriaxone. Lumbar puncture and culture of his cerebrospinal fluid reveal Neisseria meningitidis. What is the appearance of this bacterium on Gram staining?","options":{"A":"Gram-positive cocci","B":"Gram-negative cocci","C":"Gram-positive rods","D":"Gram-negative rods","E":"Poorly staining organism"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Members of the genus Neisseria are Gram-negative cocci (specifically diplococci with adjacent flattened sides)."},{"id":"q_college_67","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Which one of the following vaccines uses a protein that attaches to the polysaccharide outer coat to make the pathogen more immunogenic?","options":{"A":"Rabies","B":"Yellow fever","C":"Oral polio","D":"Measles","E":"Meningococcus"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"Unconjugated capsular polysaccharide antigens elicit a T-cell-independent immune response that fails to induce immune memory or protect children $<2$ years of age. Conjugate meningococcal vaccines chemically link the capsular polysaccharide to an immunogenic carrier protein (such as diphtheria or tetanus toxoid), converting it into a T-cell-dependent antigen capable of inducing long-term immune memory."},{"id":"q_college_68","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 47-year-old lady is referred by her GP with a two-day history of fever and headache. She is normally fit and well and has no significant past medical history. On examination, you note nuchal rigidity. Investigations show: Serum glucose: 4.9 mmol/L Lumbar puncture reveals: ... What is the most likely infective agent?","options":{"A":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","B":"Escherichia coli","C":"Listeria monocytogenes","D":"Enterovirus","E":"Streptococcus pyogenes"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Streptococcus pneumoniae is the most common etiologic agent of community-acquired acute bacterial meningitis in immunocompetent adults across all age groups (accounting for $>50\\%$ of adult cases)."},{"id":"q_college_69","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 57-year-old female presents with headache and fever to the emergency department. On examination, neck stiffness is noted along with a positive Kernigظآs sign. A lumbar puncture is performed. CSF culture: Gram-positive bacilli. What is the most likely causative organism?","options":{"A":"Cryptococcus","B":"Haemophilus influenzae","C":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","D":"Escherichia coli","E":"Listeria monocytogenes"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"Listeria monocytogenes is a facultatively anaerobic, non-spore-forming, motile Gram-positive bacillus (rod). It is an opportunistic cause of meningitis in older adults, pregnant females, and immunocompromised individuals"},{"id":"q_college_70","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 31-year-old woman presents to the emergency department complaining of a headache. She has had flu-like symptoms for the past three days, with the headache developing gradually yesterday. The headache is described as generalized and worsens with bright light or neck flexion. On examination: Temperature: 38.2┬░C Pulse: 96/min Blood pressure: 116/78 mmHg Neck stiffness is present No focal neurological signs Petechiae are noted on her torso She has been cannulated and blood samples, including cultures, have been taken. What is the most appropriate next step?","options":{"A":"IV cefotaxime","B":"Arrange a CT head","C":"Perform a lumbar puncture","D":"IV dexamethasone","E":"Intramuscular benzylpenicillin"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"In acute bacterial meningitis, door-to-antibiotic time directly influences survival. Once blood cultures have been drawn in a patient displaying classic meningeal irritation alongside a petechial/purpuric rash (pathognomonic of meningococcemia), intravenous bactericidal antibiotic therapy (such as IV cefotaxime or ceftriaxone) must be administered immediately. Lumbar puncture should not delay antimicrobial delivery."},{"id":"q_college_71","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A patient with severe headache, nausea, and vomiting presents to the emergency department. Examination reveals neck stiffness and a positive Kernigظآs sign. Lumbar puncture reveals purulent CSF. She is urgently started on ceftriaxone. Which class of antibiotics does ceftriaxone belong to?","options":{"A":"Macrolides","B":"Tetracyclines","C":"Aminoglycosides","D":"Beta-lactams","E":"Lincosamides"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Ceftriaxone is an extended-spectrum third-generation cephalosporin. Cephalosporins are classified structurally under the beta-lactam antibiotic superfamily (characterized by a core four-membered beta- lactam ring). They exert bactericidal activity by covalently binding penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs), thereby halting peptidoglycan cross-linking in the bacterial cell wall."},{"id":"q_college_72","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 19-year-old man presents with a 12-hour history of headache and fever. On examination, you elicit neck stiffness, photophobia, and a positive Kernigظآs sign. He scores 15 on the Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS), and there has been no behavioral change or evidence of raised intracranial pressure. He is hemodynamically stable and has no rash. Blood cultures are taken, and a lumbar puncture is performed. You decide to treat empirically for bacterial meningitis and prescribe intravenous cefotaxime every 6 hours. Which of the following is most appropriate in addition to intravenous cefotaxime?","options":{"A":"Give intravenous acyclovir","B":"Give intravenous dexamethasone with the first antibiotic dose and continue every 6 hours","C":"Give intravenous dexamethasone with the first antibiotic dose only","D":"Await the cerebrospinal fluid results and prescribe intravenous dexamethasone only if Streptococcus pneumoniae is isolated","E":"Give intravenous amoxicillin"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Adjunctive corticosteroid therapy (intravenous dexamethasone 10 mg every 6 hours for 4 days) dampens the exuberant subarachnoid inflammatory cascade and cerebral edema triggered by rapid bacteriolysis. To prevent neurological sequelaeظ¤particularly sensorineural hearing lossظ¤dexamethasone should be administered before or concurrently with the first dose of antimicrobial therapy."},{"id":"q_college_73","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 74-year-old female presents with headache and neck stiffness to the emergency department. Following a lumbar puncture, the patient was started on IV ceftriaxone. CSF culture grows Listeria monocytogenes. What is the most appropriate treatment?","options":{"A":"Add IV amoxicillin","B":"Change to IV amoxicillin + gentamicin","C":"Add IV ciprofloxacin","D":"Add IV co-amoxiclav","E":"Continue IV ceftriaxone as monotherapy"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Listeria monocytogenes exhibits complete intrinsic resistance to all cephalosporins due to low binding affinity for cephalosporin-targeted PBPs. The established regimen of choice for invasive CNS listeriosis is high- dose aminopenicillin (IV amoxicillin or ampicillin) paired synergistically with an aminoglycoside (IV gentamicin) to achieve bactericidal synergy and clear intracellular organisms."},{"id":"q_college_74","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 19-year-old student is brought to the emergency department by friends because of severe headache and drowsiness. On examination, he has a widespread purpuric rash. Meningococcal infection is strongly suspected, but he is known to be penicillin-allergic (previous anaphylaxis). What is the antibiotic of choice?","options":{"A":"Chloramphenicol","B":"Meropenem","C":"Teicoplanin","D":"Erythromycin","E":"Ciprofloxacin"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"In acute suspected bacterial meningitis where severe immediate IgE-mediated anaphylaxis to beta-lactams (penicillins and cephalosporins) precludes their use, high-dose intravenous chloramphenicol is the classical second-line bactericidal agent. It has lipid solubility that allows CSF concentrations to reach therapeutic levels across the blood-brain barrier."},{"id":"q_college_75","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"You are speaking to the partner of a 28-year-old man who has recently been admitted with pyrexia and neck stiffness. Investigations: Serum glucose: 5.0 mmol/L Lumbar puncture: Appearance: Cloudy Glucose: 1.2 mmol/L Protein: 1.8 g/L White cells: 450/mm┬│ (85% polymorphs) Microscopy: Gram-negative diplococci No other serotype information is available. The partner is 27 years old and has no significant past medical history other than depression treated with fluoxetine. She completed a MenC vaccination course 8 years ago. What is the most appropriate next step to reduce her chance of developing meningitis?","options":{"A":"No further action is required","B":"MenC booster dose","C":"Oral ciprofloxacin + MenC booster dose","D":"Oral ciprofloxacin","E":"Oral rifampicin"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Close household or intimate contacts of individuals with invasive Neisseria meningitidis (Gram-negative diplococci) carry an elevated risk of contracting secondary disease. Urgent chemoprophylaxis is indicated to eradicate nasopharyngeal carriage. A single oral dose of ciprofloxacin (500 mg) is the preferred first-line chemoprophylactic regimen for adult non-pregnant contacts."},{"id":"q_college_76","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 19-year-old man presents asking for advice. His girlfriend has recently been diagnosed with meningococcal meningitis. He is worried that he may have contracted the infection. What is the recommended antibiotic prophylaxis for close contacts?","options":{"A":"Oral co-amoxiclav","B":"Oral phenoxymethylpenicillin","C":"Oral rifampicin","D":"Oral erythromycin","E":"Intramuscular cefotaxime"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Oral rifampicin (600 mg twice daily for two consecutive days) achieves high mucosal secretions and serves as an established standard chemoprophylactic agent to clear meningococcal nasopharyngeal colonization among close contacts."},{"id":"q_college_77","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 7-year-old boy is admitted to the hospital after presenting with fever, headache, and neck stiffness. A diagnosis of pneumococcal meningitis is made. There are no other reports of meningitis in the local area during the previous four weeks. How should the close contacts of this boy be managed?","options":{"A":"No action is needed","B":"Pneumococcal vaccine booster","C":"Oral amoxicillin","D":"Oral amoxicillin + pneumococcal vaccine booster","E":"Oral ciprofloxacin"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Unlike Neisseria meningitidis and Haemophilus influenzae type b, Streptococcus pneumoniae does not pose a heightened secondary attack rate among casual or household contacts. Consequently, routine chemoprophylaxis or contact tracing is unnecessary following sporadic pneumococcal meningitis."},{"id":"q_college_78","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"Which one of the following statements is true regarding Listeria monocytogenes?","options":{"A":"Multiplies rapidly at high temperatures","B":"The organism is resistant to ampicillin","C":"It is a Gram-negative bacillus","D":"It is diagnosed by the presence of urinary antigen","E":"May cause ataxia"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"Listeria monocytogenes exhibits marked neurotropism for the brainstem and cerebellum, causing rhombencephalitis. This focal presentation leads to classic cerebellar signs including ataxia, dysmetria, and nystagmus, alongside cranial nerve deficits. It is a psychrotolerant Gram-positive rod that is susceptible to ampicillin."},{"id":"q_college_79","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 30-year-old pregnant woman develops sepsis with headache and neck stiffness after eating coleslaw, soft cheese, and cantaloupes at a potluck dinner. Two other women who attended the event develop self-limiting abdominal cramps, nausea, and vomiting. The causative organism is:","options":{"A":"Enterococcus faecalis","B":"Herpes simplex virus","C":"Listeria monocytogenes","D":"Staphylococcus saprophyticus"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Listeria monocytogenes is a foodborne bacterium capable of propagating under refrigeration temperatures, commonly contaminating unpasteurized dairy, soft cheeses, deli meats, and unwashed vegetables. Healthy individuals typically develop self-limiting gastroenteritis, whereas pregnant women are susceptible to systemic bacteremia and CNS infection due to altered cell-mediated immunity."},{"id":"q_college_80","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 54-year-old hypertensive man with rheumatoid arthritis, currently taking methotrexate, presents to the emergency department with fever, headache, neck stiffness, and increased sensitivity to light. Neurological examination is normal except for fundus examination, which reveals bilateral blurring of the optic disc margins (papilledema) and retinal venous engorgement. What should be the next step?","options":{"A":"CT scan","B":"Lumbar puncture","C":"MRI brain","D":"Empiric antibiotic therapy"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The presence of papilledema points to elevated intracranial pressure. Performing an immediate lumbar puncture in this scenario risks precipitating tentorial or tonsillar herniation due to the sudden decompression of the spinal thecal space. A non-contrast head CT scan must be executed first to rule out an intracranial mass lesion or midline shift."},{"id":"q_college_81","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Meningitis","text":"A 20-year-old man develops fever, neck stiffness, and headache. On examination, his blood pressure is normal, but he is tachycardic and febrile. Neck flexion is painful. There are no focal neurological signs. Examination of the ears, throat, and sinuses is normal. Which of the following is the most likely causative organism?","options":{"A":"Neisseria meningitidis","B":"Haemophilus influenzae","C":"Listeria monocytogenes","D":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","E":"Staphylococcus"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Neisseria meningitidis is a leading bacterial cause of acute community-acquired meningitis among teenagers and young adults living in communal or collegiate environments. Childhood vaccination has minimized H. influenzae, while Listeria predominantly affects neonates and immunocompromised elderly patients."},{"id":"q_college_82","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fungal Infections","text":"All of the following are risk factors for fungal infections, EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Diabetes mellitus (DM)","B":"Hypertension (HTN)","C":"Immunosuppressive drugs","D":"HIV","E":"Steroid therapy"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Invasive opportunistic fungal infections require defects in phagocytic host defense, neutrophil quantity, or cell-mediated T-cell immunity (seen with DM, ketoacidosis, neutropenia, high-dose corticosteroids, chemotherapy, and HIV/AIDS). Essential hypertension does not cause host immunosuppression or confer risk for fungal invasion."},{"id":"q_college_83","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fungal Infections","text":"The highest dose of amphotericin B is used in which of the following?","options":{"A":"Candidiasis","B":"Histoplasmosis","C":"Coccidioidomycosis","D":"Mucormycosis"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Mucorales are opportunistic molds that cause aggressive, destructive vascular thrombosis and tissue infarction. Penetration into necrotic tissue is poor, and standard antifungal regimens often fail; thus, liposomal amphotericin B is prescribed at elevated doses (typically 5 to 10 mg/kg/day, compared to 3 to 5 mg/kg/day for Candida or endemic mycoses) alongside surgical debridement."},{"id":"q_college_84","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fungal Infections","text":"The first-line treatment of aspergilloma is:","options":{"A":"Antifungal therapy alone","B":"Surgery","C":"Antifungal therapy + surgery","D":"Antifungal therapy + steroids"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"A simple aspergilloma represents a saprophytic ball composed of fungal hyphae, mucus, and cellular debris resting inside a pre-existing scarred pulmonary cavity. Systemic antifungals demonstrate negligible penetration into the avascular cavitary lumen. Complete surgical excision (lobectomy or wedge resection) is definitive, curative, and prevents fatal hemoptysis."},{"id":"q_college_85","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fungal Infections","text":"Which of the following may cause vascular invasion?","options":{"A":"Mucormycosis","B":"Coccidioidomycosis","C":"Aspergillosis","D":"Candidiasis"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Mucormycosis (caused by fungi of the order Mucorales, such as Rhizopus and Mucor) exhibits marked angioinvasiveness. Fungal broad, non-septate, right-angle branching hyphae directly penetrate the internal elastic lamina of arterial walls, resulting in lumen thrombosis, microvascular occlusion, and extensive downstream ischemic tissue necrosis."},{"id":"q_college_86","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fungal Infections","text":"Systemic fungal infection may be associated with all of the following, EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Urinary catheter","B":"Common bile duct plastic stent","C":"Double-J catheter","D":"Central line","E":"Superficial wound dressing"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"Systemic and candidemic fungal infections frequently originate from indwelling intravascular catheters, urinary stents, or biliary prostheses, which provide non-biological surfaces allowing biofilm formation and transluminal hematogenous invasion. An external, non-invasive superficial wound dressing does not breach sterile vascular compartments."},{"id":"q_college_87","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fungal Infections","text":"Glucocorticoids have been shown to provide therapeutic clinical benefit in all of the following infections EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Invasive Aspergillus fumigatus pneumonia","B":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis tuberculous pericarditis","C":"Hypoxic Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PaO2 < 70 mmHg)","D":"Severe typhoid fever presenting with delirium and shock","E":"Streptococcus pneumoniae acute bacterial meningitis"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Adjunctive corticosteroids reduce inflammatory tissue injury and mortality in TB pericarditis/meningitis, hypoxic PCP, severe typhoid shock, and pneumococcal meningitis, but are strictly contraindicated and exacerbate invasive fungal infections such as pulmonary aspergillosis."},{"id":"q_college_88","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fungal Infections","text":"A 38-year-old HIV-infected patient with a CD4 count of 45 cells/╬╝L presents with a progressive headache, low-grade fever, and mild confusion. On physical examination, there is slight neck stiffness. A lumbar puncture is performed, and India ink preparation of the CSF reveals encapsulated budding yeast cells. What is the induction treatment of choice for this patient?","options":{"A":"Oral Fluconazole","B":"Intravenous Voriconazole","C":"Intravenous Amphotericin B combined with Flucytosine","D":"Intravenous Caspofungin"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"This patient has Cryptococcal meningitis, a severe opportunistic fungal infection common in advanced AIDS. The standard first-line induction therapy is a combination of Liposomal Amphotericin B and oral Flucytosine for at least 2 weeks, followed by consolidation therapy with Fluconazole."},{"id":"q_college_89","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fungal Infections","text":"In the management and prophylaxis of Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP) in severely immunocompromised patients (such as those with HIV and CD4 < 200 cells/╬╝L), which of the following is the first-line drug of choice?","options":{"A":"Pentamidine","B":"Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX)","C":"Itraconazole","D":"Fluconazole"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole (Co-trimoxazole) is the first-line agent of choice for both the treatment and prophylaxis of PCP. It works by inhibiting microbial folate synthesis. Second-line alternatives include dapsone, atovaquone, or intravenous pentamidine for patients with severe sulfa allergies."},{"id":"q_college_90","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fungal Infections","text":"A patient undergoing intensive chemotherapy for leukemia is noted to have creamy white, curd- like patches on the tongue and buccal mucosa. Which of the following is correct regarding this presentation?","options":{"A":"Scraping the white plaques reveals an erythematous, bleeding base","B":"The causative organism is an obligate intracellular pathogen","C":"These patches cannot be scraped off easily without causing deep ulceration","D":"It should be treated primarily with systemic Griseofulvin"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"This is oral thrush (pseudomembranous candidiasis) caused by Candida albicans. A hallmark feature that distinguishes pseudomembranous candidiasis from other oral lesions (such as oral hairy leukoplakia or lichen planus) is that the pseudomembrane can be scraped off with a tongue depressor, exposing an underlying hyperemic, raw, and bleeding mucosal base."},{"id":"q_college_91","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Encephalitis","text":"Which of the following is not a typical feature of herpes infection?","options":{"A":"Dysuria","B":"Seizures","C":"Rash","D":"Epistaxis"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Herpes simplex virus (HSV-1 and HSV-2) causes painful mucocutaneous vesicles (vesicular rash), painful genital ulcers leading to dysuria, and necrotizing temporal lobe encephalitis manifesting with focal seizures. Spontaneous epistaxis (nasal bleeding) is not a manifestation of HSV pathology.."},{"id":"q_college_92","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Encephalitis","text":"A 33-year-old primigravida woman presents to her GP at 22 weeksظآ gestation with a 2-day history of painful shallow ulcers on the labia and vagina. She has had one prior outbreak of herpes 2 years previously, with a viral swab performed at that time positive for HSV-2. She is otherwise well, and her 20-week scan was normal. She is concerned about how HSV may affect her pregnancy and whether it will be safe for her to deliver vaginally. What should you advise her?","options":{"A":"There is a high risk of blood-borne neonatal transmission of HSV regardless of treatment.","B":"All antiviral medications normally used in herpes are teratogenic and should be avoided.","C":"Ibuprofen and salt-water bathing are recommended for analgesia.","D":"Most women with outbreaks of recurrent HSV during pregnancy are recommended to deliver by elective caesarean section.","E":"Suppressive treatment with aciclovir from 36 weeksظآ gestation may be considered."},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"In pregnant women with recurrent genital herpes, suppressive therapy with oral acyclovir starting at 36 weeks of gestation significantly reduces clinical recurrences and viral shedding at term, which in turn reduces the need for delivery via Caesarean section."},{"id":"q_college_93","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Encephalitis","text":"In the United States, which of the following is the most common cause of sporadic","options":{"A":"Cytomegalovirus","B":"Herpes simplex virus","C":"Lymphocytic choriomeningitis virus","D":"Varicella-zoster virus"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Herpes Simplex Virus Type 1 (HSV-1) is the leading etiologic agent of non-epidemic, sporadic, fatal viral encephalitis in developed nations across all age brackets. It classically causes asymmetric, hemorrhagic necrosis of the temporal and inferior frontal lobes."},{"id":"q_college_94","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Encephalitis","text":"Because prompt identification of the cause of encephalitis via PCR is difficult, treatment should not be withheld pending test results and should consist of which of the following?","options":{"A":"Acyclovir","B":"Ganciclovir","C":"Immune globulin","D":"Prednisone"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"HSV encephalitis carries a mortality rate exceeding 70% if untreated. Empirical therapy with high-dose intravenous acyclovir (10 mg/kg every 8 hours) should be initiated as soon as encephalitis is clinically suspected, without awaiting CSF PCR confirmation, as delays in treatment correlate with permanent neurologic disability and mortality."},{"id":"q_college_95","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Encephalitis","text":"Which of the following findings in patients with encephalitis suggests severe brain inflammation and poor prognosis?","options":{"A":"Edema in the orbitofrontal or temporal areas of the brain","B":"Gastrointestinal or respiratory prodrome","C":"Olfactory seizures","D":"Status epilepticus"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Status epilepticus reflects widespread cerebral cortical excitability and uncontrolled neuronal metabolic demand. In acute encephalitis, prolonged seizure activity causes progressive ischemic brain damage and correlates with elevated mortality and lasting cognitive or motor deficits."},{"id":"q_college_96","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Encephalitis","text":"A 34-year-old patient presents with high fever, rapidly progressive confusion, and olfactory hallucinations. What is the most sensitive and specific non-invasive diagnostic test to confirm Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV) encephalitis?","options":{"A":"Electroencephalogram (EEG) showing temporal spikes","B":"Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR) for HSV DNA in the CSF","C":"Serology for HSV IgG antibodies in peripheral blood","D":"CT scan of the brain with contrast"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"CSF PCR for HSV DNA is the diagnostic gold standard for HSV encephalitis due to its high sensitivity (>95%) and specificity. While EEG and MRI are valuable supportive modalities that demonstrate focal temporal lobe edema, they cannot provide definitive microbiological confirmation."},{"id":"q_college_97","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Encephalitis","text":"Which of the following clinical features most reliably distinguishes acute viral encephalitis from acute uncomplicated bacterial or viral meningitis?","options":{"A":"High-grade fever and severe generalized headache","B":"The presence of significant photophobia and phonophobia","C":"Altered mental status, confusion, and focal neurological deficits","D":"Marked nuchal rigidity and positive Kernig''s sign"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Both meningitis and encephalitis share meningeal symptoms such as fever, headache, and photophobia. However, encephalitis specifically involves infection and inflammation of the cerebral parenchymal tissue, manifesting as altered consciousness, cognitive dysfunction, hallucinations, focal motor or sensory deficits, and seizures, which are characteristically absent in uncomplicated meningitis."},{"id":"q_college_98","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Encephalitis","text":"An EEG is performed on a patient with suspected encephalitis. The results show Periodic Lateralized Epileptiform Discharges (PLEDs) localized over the temporal and frontotemporal lobes. Which pathogen is most strongly associated with this finding?","options":{"A":"Enterovirus","B":"West Nile Virus","C":"Herpes Simplex Virus Type 1","D":"Cytomegalovirus"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"HSV-1 exhibits strong neurotropism for the temporal and inferior frontal lobes (limbic system). Periodic lateralized epileptiform discharges (PLEDs) and focal sharp wave activity localized over one or both temporal lobes on electroencephalography provide supportive neurophysiological evidence of HSV-1"},{"id":"q_college_99","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Encephalitis","text":"A patient presents with acute encephalitis. During the review of systems, the family reports that the patient complained of experiencing a strange smell of \"burnt rubber\" just before becoming confused and having a seizure. This olfactory aura suggests localized inflammation in which brain region?","options":{"A":"Occipital lobe","B":"Cerebellum","C":"Temporal lobe","D":"Parietal lobe"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Olfactory hallucinations or auras (often described as foul, burning, or chemical odors) are classic localizing features of irritation in the uncus of the medial temporal lobe (parahippocampal gyrus). This is a hallmark focal sign of Herpes Simplex Virus encephalitis, which preferentially invades temporal and orbitofrontal structures."},{"id":"q_college_100","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Septicemia & Septic Shock","text":"Sepsis is a serious body-wide response to bacteremia or another type of infection, plus malfunction or failure of an essential system in the body. Which of the following is always one of the body''s responses to sepsis?","options":{"A":"Abnormally high or low number of white blood cells","B":"Abnormally high temperature (fever) or low temperature (hypothermia)","C":"Rapid breathing rate","D":"Rapid heart rate"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Systemic host dysregulation classically manifests as pyrexia ($>38^\\circ\\text{C}$) driven by pro- inflammatory pyrogens (IL-1, TNF-$\\alpha$, prostaglandins), or as paradoxical hypothermia ($<36^\\circ\\text{C}$), which is indicative of vascular collapse, exhaustion of thermal regulation, and poor prognosis, particularly in elderly or immunocompromised patients."},{"id":"q_college_101","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Septicemia & Septic Shock","text":"Sepsis is caused by infection with certain types of bacteria. In which of the following locations is an infection leading to septic shock most often acquired?","options":{"A":"In schools","B":"In hospitals","C":"From contaminated food in restaurants and cafeterias","D":"In workplaces"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Hospital-acquired (nosocomial) infections frequently cause severe sepsis and septic shock due to vulnerable host populations, invasive vascular or urinary instrumentation, and colonization by multidrug-resistant pathogens (such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa, MRSA, and carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales)."},{"id":"q_college_102","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Septicemia & Septic Shock","text":"Each of the following increases the likelihood of developing an infection that will lead to sepsis, EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Being between the ages of 5 and 15 years","B":"Having a disorder that weakens the immune system, such as AIDS or cancer","C":"Having a medical device, such as a catheter or breathing tube, inserted into the body","D":"Using recreational drugs that are injected into the bloodstream"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Children and young adolescents aged 5 to 15 have mature adaptive immune systems, low rates of chronic comorbidity, and low rates of invasive medical devices, placing them at the lowest epidemiological risk of developing sepsis compared to the elderly, infants, or immunocompromised individuals."},{"id":"q_college_103","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Septicemia & Septic Shock","text":"Which of the following statements regarding sepsis is not true?","options":{"A":"Sepsis is SIRS plus documented infection.","B":"A causative organism is found in 90% of cases.","C":"A high serum lactate is evidence of poor tissue perfusion.","D":"More than 50% of ICU patients develop sepsis during the course of their admission.","E":"The true incidence of sepsis is likely to be lower than estimated."},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"In routine clinical practice, blood cultures and microbiological diagnostics are culture-negative in roughly 30% to 50% of patients meeting clinical criteria for sepsis, due in part to prior empiric antimicrobial exposure, low circulating bacterial loads, or non-bacterial etiologies."},{"id":"q_college_104","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Septicemia & Septic Shock","text":"The following may be required to diagnose SIRS:","options":{"A":"Positive blood cultures","B":"White cell count <4 ├ù 10ظ╣/L","C":"Temperature >38┬░C","D":"PaCOظéé >6.5 kPa","E":"Heart rate >90 beats/min"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"The Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome (SIRS) is defined by meeting at least two of four clinical criteria: (1) Temperature $>38^\\circ\\text{C}$ or $<36^\\circ\\text{C}$, (2) Heart rate $>90\\text{ beats/min}$, (3) Respiratory rate $>20\\text{ breaths/min}$ or $\\text{PaCO}_2 <4.3\\text{ kPa}$ ($<32\\text{ mmHg}$ due to hyperventilation), and (4) WBC count $>12\\times 10^9/\\text{L}$ or $<4\\times 10^9/\\text{L}$ or $>10\\%$ immature bands. Heart rate $>90\\text{ bpm}$ is a primary hemodynamic criterion."},{"id":"q_college_105","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Septicemia & Septic Shock","text":"The following may precipitate severe sepsis or SIRS:","options":{"A":"Peritonitis","B":"Massive transfusion","C":"Pneumonia","D":"Candidemia","E":"Burns"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"Extensive thermal burns cause widespread coagulative tissue necrosis, breakdown of the skin barrier, and release of damage-associated molecular patterns (DAMPs). This triggers systemic inflammation and capillary leak, leading to non-infectious SIRS that can evolve into secondary severe sepsis."},{"id":"q_college_106","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Septicemia & Septic Shock","text":"The following substances are involved in the causation of the clinical features of sepsis:","options":{"A":"Inducible nitric oxide","B":"Interleukin-6","C":"Acetylcysteine","D":"Tumor necrosis factor"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Tumor Necrosis Factor-alpha (TNF-$\\alpha$) is an early primary pro-inflammatory cytokine released by macrophages in response to endotoxin (LPS). It activates endothelial adhesion molecules, increases microvascular permeability, triggers the extrinsic coagulation cascade, and causes vasodilation, driving the systemic manifestations of septic shock."},{"id":"q_college_107","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Septicemia & Septic Shock","text":"The following features may be present in a patient presenting with severe sepsis :","options":{"A":"Hypotension","B":"Oliguria","C":"Hypertension","D":"Confusion","E":"Cool peripheries"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Severe sepsis involves systemic vasodilation, diffuse capillary leak, and microvascular hypoperfusion. This produces arterial hypotension, diminished end-organ perfusion causing oliguria (acute renal tubular ischemia), altered cerebral perfusion (confusion), and peripheral vasoconstriction presenting as cool peripheries in cold shock."},{"id":"q_college_108","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Septicemia & Septic Shock","text":"In patients with severe sepsis:","options":{"A":"Administration of antibiotics is time dependent.","B":"Vasopressors should be given to those who remain hypotensive despite fluid resuscitation.","C":"A central venous oxygen saturation of 80% reliably excludes tissue hypoxia.","D":"Management should be in an ICU setting."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The answer reflects the systemic inflammatory response, risk factors, organ dysfunction, or time-critical management principles tested in sepsis. The best answer is a) Administration of antibiotics is time dependent; b) Vasopressors should be given if hypotension persists despite fluids; d) ICU-level management may be required because it is the defining association tested by this question."},{"id":"q_college_109","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Septicemia & Septic Shock","text":"According to international Surviving Sepsis Campaign guidelines, initial fluid resuscitation for a patient presenting with sepsis-induced tissue hypoperfusion or a lactate level ظëح 4 mmol/L should consist of:","options":{"A":"At least 10 mL/kg of intravenous crystalloids within 1 hour","B":"At least 30 mL/kg of intravenous crystalloids within 3 hours","C":"Immediate blood transfusion regardless of hemoglobin level","D":"Administering 50 mL/kg of synthetic colloid solutions"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The Surviving Sepsis Campaign recommends administering at least 30 mL/kg of balanced intravenous crystalloid fluids (such as Lactated Ringer''s or normal saline) within the first 3 hours of presentation for patients with sepsis-induced hypotension or a serum lactate ظëح 4 mmol/L."},{"id":"q_college_110","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Septicemia & Septic Shock","text":"A patient in the intensive care unit with severe urosepsis remains hypotensive with a Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) of 55 mmHg despite adequate intravenous fluid resuscitation. What is the first-line vasopressor of choice to restore perfusion pressure?","options":{"A":"Dopamine","B":"Epinephrine","C":"Norepinephrine","D":"Vasopressin"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Norepinephrine is the first-line vasopressor in septic shock. Its potent alpha-1 vasoconstrictive activity increases systemic vascular resistance and MAP with minimal beta-1 chronotropic effects, offering lower tachyarrhythmia rates and superior survival compared to dopamine."},{"id":"q_college_111","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Septicemia & Septic Shock","text":"Which of the following laboratory markers is the most reliable clinical tool to evaluate tissue hypoperfusion, anaerobic metabolism, and adequacy of resuscitation in a patient with septic shock?","options":{"A":"White blood cell count","B":"Blood lactate level","C":"Serum creatinine level","D":"Procalcitonin level"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Serum lactate serves as a surrogate biochemical marker of cellular hypoxia and anaerobic glycolysis secondary to microvascular hypoperfusion. Monitoring lactate clearance over time provides objective guidance on the adequacy of fluid and vasopressor resuscitation; failure to clear lactate correlates directly with increased mortality."},{"id":"q_college_112","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Septicemia & Septic Shock","text":"To satisfy the clinical definition of \"Septic Shock\" under the Sepsis-3 consensus criteria, a patient must have documented sepsis accompanied by which of the following despite adequate fluid resuscitation?","options":{"A":"Requirement for vasopressors to maintain MAP ظëح 65 mmHg AND serum lactate > 2 mmol/L","B":"Transient hypotension responsive to a 250 mL fluid challenge","C":"Persistent core body temperature above 39┬░C and profound leukocytosis","D":"Need for invasive mechanical ventilation and a positive blood culture"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Sepsis-3 defines septic shock as a subset of sepsis in which underlying circulatory and cellular/metabolic abnormalities substantially increase mortality. It is clinically operationalized as persistent hypotension requiring vasopressors to maintain a MAP ظëح 65 mmHg AND a serum lactate level > 2 mmol/L (18 mg/dL) despite adequate volume resuscitation."},{"id":"q_college_113","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Septicemia & Septic Shock","text":"Which clinical bundle of interventions is recommended to be initiated within the first hour of identifying a patient with suspected sepsis (\"Hour-1 Bundle\")?","options":{"A":"Complete a 14-day course of oral antibiotics","B":"Measure lactate level, obtain blood cultures before antibiotics, and administer broad-spectrum IV antimicrobials","C":"Insert a central venous catheter and initiate invasive mechanical ventilation","D":"Perform an emergent non-contrast brain CT scan"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The Hour-1 Bundle for sepsis mandates rapid execution: (1) measure blood lactate level (and remeasure if initial lactate is > 2 mmol/L), (2) obtain blood cultures prior to antibiotic administration, (3) administer broad- spectrum intravenous antimicrobials, (4) rapidly infuse 30 mL/kg crystalloids for hypotension or lactate ظëح 4 mmol/L, and (5) apply vasopressors if hypotensive during or after fluid administration."},{"id":"q_college_114","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Septicemia & Septic Shock","text":"When assessing volume responsiveness in a mechanically ventilated patient with septic shock, which parameter provides the highest diagnostic accuracy compared to static markers like Central Venous Pressure (CVP)?","options":{"A":"Single measurement of CVP","B":"Stroke volume variation (SVV) or passive leg raise response","C":"Absolute resting heart rate","D":"Serum sodium concentration"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Static measures such as central venous pressure (CVP) or pulmonary capillary wedge pressure fail to predict fluid responsiveness reliably. Dynamic physiological parametersظ¤such as Stroke Volume Variation (SVV), Pulse Pressure Variation (PPV), or real-time cardiac output changes during a Passive Leg Raise (PLR) testظ¤accurately guide fluid administration and prevent volume overload."},{"id":"q_college_115","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Brucellosis","text":"Which of the following is false regarding brucellosis?","options":{"A":"Acquired through contact with infected animals or ingestion of raw goat''s milk","B":"Brucella melitensis is the most common type of infection","C":"Complement fixation test (CFT) is more important in acute infection than the agglutination reaction","D":"May present as pyrexia of unknown origin"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"In acute brucellosis, the Standard Tube Agglutination test (STA / Wright test) is the primary serological tool because it readily detects high titers of early agglutinating IgM (and IgG) antibodies appearing by the second week of illness. In contrast, non-agglutinating tests like the Complement Fixation Test (CFT) and 2- Mercaptoethanol test are reserved for diagnosing chronic, localized, or relapsing brucellosis where non- agglutinating IgG and IgA antibodies predominate."},{"id":"q_college_116","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Brucellosis","text":"Malta fever is caused by:","options":{"A":"Pasteurella tularensis","B":"Haemophilus influenzae","C":"Brucella abortus","D":"Pseudomonas mallei"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Malta fever (undulant fever) is human brucellosis. Classically and epidemiologically, the most virulent and common etiologic species worldwide is Brucella melitensis (hosted by sheep and goats). However, among the answer choices provided in this legacy exam question, Brucella abortus (primarily infecting cattle) is the only member of the genus Brucella listed."},{"id":"q_college_117","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Brucellosis","text":"A 29-year-old male butcher presents to the outpatient clinic with fever characterized by periods of fever followed by afebrile periods, associated with profuse sweating and back pain. His CBC shows mild anemia and leukocytosis. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Typhoid fever","B":"Malaria","C":"Non-Hodgkin lymphoma","D":"Brucellosis"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Occupational exposure in a butcher handling infected animal tissues, combined with the hallmark \"undulant\" fever pattern (waves of pyrexia alternating with afebrile remissions), drenching night sweats, and musculoskeletal back pain (sacroiliitis or lumbar spondylitis), is diagnostic of brucellosis."},{"id":"q_college_118","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Brucellosis","text":"A 30-year-old man returns from a cheese and wine tasting holiday in Portugal. He reports eating large amounts of unpasteurized cheese. He presents complaining of fluctuating temperatures, transient pain in his joints and muscles, and excessive sweating with a peculiar ظ£wet hayظإ smell. What is the most likely causative organism?","options":{"A":"Yersinia pestis","B":"Brucella melitensis","C":"Wuchereria bancrofti","D":"Bartonella henselae","E":"Plasmodium falciparum"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Consumption of unpasteurized soft cheese or raw dairy products from Mediterranean/southern European regions is the primary mode of transmission for Brucella melitensis. The clinical presentation of undulating fever, migratory arthralgia/myalgia, and profuse diaphoresis with a pathognomonic sour, moldy \"wet hay\" odor is typical of acute B. melitensis infection"},{"id":"q_college_119","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Brucellosis","text":"A 58-year-old man presents with fever, chills, and back pain for the past four weeks. Two months ago, he stayed with family on a sheep farm in Cyprus. Chest X-ray and urine culture are unremarkable. Brucellosis is suspected. Which one of the following tests is most likely to confirm the diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Stool culture","B":"Blood cultures","C":"Brucella serology","D":"Liver biopsy","E":"Urinary antigen"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"While automated prolonged blood culture remains the definitive microbiological proof (or bone marrow culture, which has a 90% yield), in regular clinical practice Brucella serology (Standard Tube Agglutination or ELISA) is the most widely available, sensitive, and rapid test used to confirm the diagnosis, showing titers $>1:160$ or a fourfold rise. Blood cultures can be negative in subacute or localized osteoarticular phases."},{"id":"q_college_120","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Brucellosis","text":"Which of the following conditions is associated with sinus bradycardia?","options":{"A":"Brucellosis","B":"Malaria","C":"Pneumonia","D":"Giardiasis","E":"Tuberculosis"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Brucellosis causes relative bradycardia (Faget''s sign), wherein the pulse rate fails to rise proportionally with the rise in body temperature (a dissociation between heart rate and high fever). This clinical sign is also classically seen in typhoid fever, yellow fever, and legionellosis."},{"id":"q_college_121","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Brucellosis","text":"Brucellosis:","options":{"A":"Causes spondylitis","B":"Is treated with tetracycline","C":"Is a recognized cause of chronic depression","D":"Is contracted from unpasteurized milk","E":"All of the above"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"All listed statements are accurate: Brucella species cause lumbar spondylitis (most commonly at the L4 level); require tetracyclines (specifically oral Doxycycline) as the backbone of therapy; trigger chronic neurobrucellosis presenting with neuropsychiatric exhaustion and depression; and are predominantly transmitted by the ingestion of raw, unpasteurized milk and dairy products"},{"id":"q_college_122","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Brucellosis","text":"The agglutination test for Brucella mainly identifies which class of antibodies?","options":{"A":"IgG","B":"IgM","C":"IgA","D":"IgE"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The standard tube agglutination (STA / Wright) test primarily measures agglutinating IgM antibodies directed against the smooth lipopolysaccharide (LPS) antigen of Brucella during active early disease, though it also detects some agglutinating IgG. Non-agglutinating IgG and IgA predominate in chronic stages and require Coombs or ELISA testing."},{"id":"q_college_123","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hepatitis","text":"Which of the following is NOT correct for Hepatitis A virus (HAV)?","options":{"A":"It can be grown in cell cultures of primate and human cells","B":"It is one of the most stable viruses infecting humans","C":"It may cause hepatocellular carcinoma","D":"None of the above"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"HAV causes acute, self-limiting hepatitis and does NOT lead to chronic infection, cirrhosis, or hepatocellular carcinoma. Hepatocellular carcinoma is linked to chronic viral hepatitis (HBV, HCV, HDV)."},{"id":"q_college_124","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Leptospirosis","text":"The following statements are true regarding leptospirosis, EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"It is a zoonosis.","B":"Man is the dead-end host.","C":"Man is an accidental host.","D":"Lice act as reservoirs of infection."},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Leptospira interrogans is maintained in nature primarily by rodents (particularly rats) and domestic farm animals, which harbor the spirochete chronically in their renal tubules and shed it in their urine. Humans act as accidental, dead-end hosts through contact with urine-contaminated water or wet soil. Body lice are insect vectors for Rickettsia prowazekii (epidemic typhus) and Borrelia recurrentis, not Leptospira."},{"id":"q_college_125","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Leptospirosis","text":"The following symptoms and signs are true regarding anicteric leptospirosis, EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Flu-like symptoms.","B":"Nausea and vomiting.","C":"Exudative conjunctivitis.","D":"Pharyngitis."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The pathognomonic ocular finding in early anicteric leptospirosis is conjunctival suffusionظ¤a marked bilateral conjunctival hyperemia/erythema without purulent or exudative discharge, often accompanied by photophobia. An exudative (purulent) conjunctivitis argues strongly for a pyogenic bacterial ocular infection."},{"id":"q_college_126","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Leptospirosis","text":"How is leptospirosis transmitted?","options":{"A":"Exposure of mucous membranes or abraded skin to contaminated water.","B":"By mosquito bites.","C":"By contact with an infected person."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"ransmission occurs when pathogenic leptospires present in water, mud, or wet soil contaminated with the urine of infected animal reservoirs penetrate human mucosal membranes (conjunctival, oral, nasopharyngeal) or broken, abraded skin (e.g., during swimming, wading, flooding, or sewer work). Human- to-human spread is virtually non-existent."},{"id":"q_college_127","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Leptospirosis","text":"Which are the two types of leptospirosis?","options":{"A":"Icteric and anicteric.","B":"Edematous and non-edematous.","C":"Hemorrhagic and non-hemorrhagic.","D":"Benign and malignant."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Leptospirosis presents clinically in two distinct syndromes: 1. Anicteric leptospirosis (accounting for 85ظô90% of cases), a milder, biphasic febrile illness without liver jaundice. 2. Icteric leptospirosis (Weil''s disease, 10ظô15% of cases), a severe, life-threatening condition marked by profound conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, acute kidney injury, and diffuse hemorrhagic diathesis."},{"id":"q_college_128","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Leptospirosis","text":"Which form of leptospirosis is known as Weill''s disease?","options":{"A":"Icteric.","B":"Anicteric.","C":"Both."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Weil''s disease refers specifically to the severe icteric form of leptospirosis. It is classically defined by the clinical triad of severe hepatic dysfunction with deep jaundice, acute oliguric/non-oliguric renal tubular failure, and extensive hemorrhagic manifestations (subconjunctival hemorrhages, pulmonary hemorrhage, purpura)."},{"id":"q_college_129","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Leptospirosis","text":"A 44-year-old farmer presents with headache, fever, and muscle aches. He initially thought he had a bad cold, but his symptoms have progressively worsened over the past week. During the review of systems, he reports nausea and decreased urine output. On examination: Temperature: 38.2┬░C Pulse: 102/min Chest: clear Subconjunctival hemorrhages are noted No evidence of jaundice What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Mycoplasma pneumonia.","B":"Lyme disease.","C":"Legionella pneumonia.","D":"Listeria infection.","E":"Leptospirosis."},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"The constellation of occupational farm exposure, acute febrile illness, prominent calf/lumbar myalgias, renal impairment (oliguria), and distinct subconjunctival hemorrhages (without purulent discharge or jaundice) characterizes the second immune phase of anicteric leptospirosis with evolving acute tubulointerstitial nephritis."},{"id":"q_college_130","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Leptospirosis","text":"A 34-year-old sewage worker presents with a 3-day history of lower back pain, fever, myalgia, fatigue, jaundice, and subconjunctival hemorrhage. He has no past medical history and has not traveled abroad in the last 6 months. Laboratory investigations: Naظ║: 135 mmol/L Kظ║: 5.2 mmol/L Urea: 10 mmol/L Creatinine: 180 ╬╝mol/L What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Leptospirosis.","B":"Cysticercosis.","C":"Glomerulonephritis.","D":"Hepatitis A.","E":"Hepatitis E."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The combination of occupational contact with rodent-infested sewer water, acute febrile myalgias, subconjunctival hemorrhages, clinical jaundice, and renal insufficiency (elevated creatinine and urea) is the textbook presentation of severe icteric leptospirosis (Weil''s disease). Viral hepatitis (A or E) does not produce subconjunctival hemorrhage or early acute renal failure."},{"id":"q_college_131","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Leptospirosis","text":"A 37-year-old sewer worker presents to the Emergency Department with flu-like symptoms and pyrexia for the past 3 days. Since this morning, he has developed a headache and signs of meningism. Blood tests show: Sodium: 145 mmol/L Potassium: 4.7 mmol/L Urea: 10.3 mmol/L Creatinine: 133 ╬╝mol/L What is the antibiotic treatment of choice?","options":{"A":"Co-trimoxazole.","B":"Ciprofloxacin.","C":"Metronidazole.","D":"Benzylpenicillin.","E":"Erythromycin."},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"For severe or systemic leptospirosis (manifesting with meningism, renal impairment, or jaundice), high-dose intravenous Benzylpenicillin (penicillin G, 1.5 million units IV every 6 hours) or IV Ceftriaxone (1ظô2 g daily) is the first-line bactericidal regimen of choice. Oral doxycycline is reserved for mild, uncomplicated anicteric disease."},{"id":"q_college_132","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Leptospirosis","text":"A 50-year-old sewage worker presents with a one-week history of fever and feeling generally unwell. Which one of the following features would be least consistent with a diagnosis of","options":{"A":"Meningism.","B":"Conjunctival erythema.","C":"Productive cough.","D":"Decreased urine output.","E":"Severe myalgia."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Leptospirosis commonly causes aseptic meningitis (meningism), conjunctival erythema/suffusion, acute tubular necrosis (oliguria/decreased urine output), and excruciating paraspinal and calf myalgias. When respiratory involvement occurs, it presents as dry cough, dyspnea, or hemoptysis (alveolar hemorrhage); a purulent, productive sputum cough indicates typical bacterial lung consolidation rather than leptospirosis."},{"id":"q_college_133","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Leptospirosis","text":"Which of the following laboratory results is common in the severe form of leptospirosis?","options":{"A":"Leukocytosis","B":"Thrombocytopenia","C":"Hyponatremia","D":"Eosinophilia"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Thrombocytopenia is a classic laboratory finding in severe leptospirosis (Weil''s disease), occurring in over 50% of cases due to peripheral platelet consumption, widespread endothelial activation, and systemic microvascular injury. Severe thrombocytopenia directly correlates with the risk of pulmonary alveolar hemorrhage and acute renal failure."},{"id":"q_college_134","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Leptospirosis","text":"A 24-year-old female presents with a 2-day history of fever, headache, and jaundice. She reports swimming in lakes and rivers one month ago and recalls a flu-like illness that resolved spontaneously. On examination: Temperature: 39┬░C Heart rate: 124/min Respiratory rate: 24/min Conjunctival suffusion Jaundice Generalized lymphadenopathy Photophobia What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Acute HIV infection","B":"Leptospirosis","C":"Herpes simplex viral infection","D":"Dengue fever"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"A history of freshwater recreational exposure followed by a biphasic clinical course (an initial septicemic flu- like illness that abates, followed days to weeks later by an immune phase featuring fever, photophobia/meningismus, profound conjunctival suffusion, and jaundice) is the classic trajectory of icteric"},{"id":"q_college_135","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Schistosomiasis","text":"The intermediate host for Schistosoma mansoni is:","options":{"A":"Bulinus snail","B":"Biomphalaria snail","C":"Oncomelania snail","D":"Lymnaea snail"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Biomphalaria freshwater snails act as the intermediate hosts for Schistosoma mansoni (causing intestinal and hepatic schistosomiasis). Bulinus snails serve as the intermediate host for S. haematobium, Oncomelania for S. japonicum, and Lymnaea for Fasciola hepatica."},{"id":"q_college_136","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Schistosomiasis","text":"Humans acquire schistosomiasis mainly by:","options":{"A":"Ingesting contaminated water","B":"Eating infected fish","C":"Skin penetration by cercariae","D":"Inhalation of spores"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Transmission occurs when humans come into direct contact with fresh water containing free-swimming fork- tailed cercariae shed by infected snails. Cercariae actively penetrate intact human skin using histolytic enzymes, shed their tails, and transform into schistosomulae to enter the dermal venules."},{"id":"q_college_137","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Schistosomiasis","text":"The diagnostic form of Schistosoma mansoni in stool is:","options":{"A":"Egg with terminal spine","B":"Egg with lateral spine","C":"Miracidium","D":"Cercaria"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Schistosoma mansoni eggs are large (114ظô180 ╬╝m ├ù 45ظô73 ╬╝m) and characterized by an elongated oval shape with a prominent, sharp lateral spine near the posterior end. In contrast, S. haematobium eggs possess a sharp terminal spine (found primarily in urine), and S. japonicum eggs are more rounded with a rudimentary, inconspicuous lateral knob/spine."},{"id":"q_college_138","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Schistosomiasis","text":"The major pathological lesion in schistosomiasis is caused by:","options":{"A":"Adult worms","B":"Cercariae","C":"Eggs trapped in tissues and the host immune response","D":"Miracidia"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Adult schistosome pairs evade the host immune system by incorporating host proteins onto their tegument and do not cause significant local lesions. Pathology is mediated entirely by retained eggs that embolize into host tissues (liver, bladder wall, intestines), where they secrete soluble egg antigens (SEAs), inciting a vigorous CD4+ Th2-mediated granulomatous response that progresses to dense collagenous periportal or mural fibrosis."},{"id":"q_college_139","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Schistosomiasis","text":"Which test is most useful for detecting active schistosomiasis infection?","options":{"A":"Serological test (ELISA)","B":"Microscopic detection of eggs","C":"Skin test","D":"Complement fixation test"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Microscopic visualization of viable eggs in stool (via Kato-Katz smear) or urine (via polycarbonate membrane filtration), or in a rectal mucosal biopsy, provides definitive proof of active egg-laying infection. Serological antibody testing (ELISA) cannot reliably distinguish between past, successfully treated exposure and ongoing active infection due to persistent IgG titers."},{"id":"q_college_140","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Schistosomiasis","text":"Chronic Schistosoma haematobium infection may lead to:","options":{"A":"Hepatocellular carcinoma","B":"Bladder carcinoma (squamous cell type)","C":"Colorectal carcinoma","D":"Pancreatic carcinoma"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Chronic deposition of S. haematobium eggs in the bladder and pelvic venous plexuses induces chronic mural inflammation, oxidative DNA damage, and squamous metaplasia of the urothelium. Over decades, this markedly elevates the risk of developing squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) of the urinary bladder (unlike traditional tobacco-induced transitional/urothelial carcinoma)."},{"id":"q_college_141","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Schistosomiasis","text":"Which laboratory feature is typical in hepatosplenic schistosomiasis?","options":{"A":"Elevated serum bilirubin","B":"Elevated transaminases (AST, ALT)","C":"Normal liver enzymes despite massive splenomegaly","D":"Decreased alkaline phosphatase"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"In Schistosoma mansoni infection, periportal fibrosis (Symmers'' pipestem fibrosis) affects the presinusoidal terminal portal venules without destroying the hepatocytes themselves or altering hepatic lobular microarchitecture. Consequently, patients develop severe non-cirrhotic portal hypertension and massive splenomegaly with hypersplenism, while serum transaminases (ALT/AST), synthetic albumin, and bilirubin levels remain normal until decompensation."},{"id":"q_college_142","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Schistosomiasis","text":"Cerebral schistosomiasis is most commonly caused by:","options":{"A":"Schistosoma haematobium","B":"Schistosoma japonicum","C":"Schistosoma mansoni","D":"Schistosoma mekongi"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Schistosoma japonicum eggs are smaller and rounder than those of other species, and the female worms produce them in larger daily quantities. These properties allow the eggs to pass freely through the mesenteric-systemic venous shunts and the vertebral venous plexus of Batson into the cerebral microcirculation, making S. japonicum the predominant cause of intracranial granulomas and cerebral schistosomiasis presenting with focal seizures."},{"id":"q_college_143","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Schistosomiasis","text":"Ultrasound of hepatosplenic schistosomiasis typically shows:","options":{"A":"Nodular surface and shrunken liver","B":"Periportal fibrosis with thickened echogenic bands and splenomegaly","C":"Biliary duct dilatation","D":"Diffuse fatty infiltration"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Abdominal ultrasonography in advanced hepatosplenic schistosomiasis shows Symmers'' pipestem fibrosis, recognized as thick, hyperechoic, echogenic fibrous collars/cuffs encasing the intrahepatic portal vein branches (periportal fibrosis), accompanied by portal vein dilation and splenomegaly with patent hepatic veins and an otherwise smooth liver capsule."},{"id":"q_college_144","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Schistosomiasis","text":"In intestinal schistosomiasis, the typical colonoscopic appearance is:","options":{"A":"Aphthous ulcers","B":"Pseudopolyps with granulomas containing eggs","C":"Mucosal atrophy","D":"Thickened folds with bile staining"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Direct deposition of large clusters of S. mansoni eggs into the submucosa of the distal colon and rectum provokes inflammatory granulomas, vascular hyperplasia, and mucosal hyperplasia. On endoscopy, this manifests as multiple friable bilharzial pseudopolyps, petechial hemorrhages, and granular mucosal lesions whose biopsies reveal trapped lateral-spined eggs."},{"id":"q_college_145","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Schistosomiasis","text":"A patient with advanced hepatic fibrosis caused by Schistosoma mansoni is most likely to present with:","options":{"A":"Gross ascites","B":"Bleeding esophageal varices","C":"Marked gynecomastia","D":"Deep clinical jaundice","E":"Multiple spider nevi"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Schistosoma mansoni eggs lodge in presinusoidal portal venules, triggering granulomatous inflammation and Symmers'' pipestem periportal fibrosis. This produces severe presinusoidal portal hypertension, splenomegaly, and esophageal varices with preserved hepatocellular synthetic function (stigmata of liver failure such as jaundice, spider angiomas, and gynecomastia are absent until end-stage disease)."},{"id":"q_college_146","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"Which is the infective form of the malaria parasite?","options":{"A":"Oocyst","B":"Sporozoite","C":"Bradyzoite","D":"Tachyzoite"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The sporozoite is the motile infective stage of Plasmodium. Sporozoites reside within the salivary glands of the female Anopheles mosquito and are inoculated into human subcutaneous capillaries during a blood meal, rapidly migrating through the bloodstream to invade hepatocytes within 30ظô60 minutes."},{"id":"q_college_147","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"Trophozoites, schizonts, and gametocytes of all malarial parasites are seen in the peripheral blood smear, EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Plasmodium falciparum","B":"Plasmodium malariae","C":"Plasmodium ovale","D":"Plasmodium vivax"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"In Plasmodium falciparum, mature trophozoites and schizonts produce membrane-associated knob proteins (PfEMP1) that mediate cytoadherence to endothelial vascular receptors (CD36, ICAM-1). This sequesters parasitized erythrocytes within the deep microvasculature of vital organs. As a result, only young ring-form trophozoites and mature banana-shaped gametocytes circulate in the peripheral blood smear."},{"id":"q_college_148","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"Blackwater fever is a special manifestation of malaria caused by:","options":{"A":"Plasmodium falciparum","B":"Plasmodium malariae","C":"Plasmodium ovale","D":"Plasmodium vivax"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Blackwater fever is a severe manifestation of Plasmodium falciparum malaria characterized by massive, rapid intravascular hemolysis, severe hemoglobinemia, and acute hemoglobinuria (causing black- or dark burgundy-colored urine), which frequently precipitates acute renal tubular necrosis and renal failure."},{"id":"q_college_149","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"After the sporozoite gains entrance to the human body, it undergoes a developmental cycle first in the liver and then in red blood cells, after which fever appears. This incubation period varies between Plasmodium species and is longest in:","options":{"A":"Plasmodium falciparum","B":"Plasmodium malariae","C":"Plasmodium ovale","D":"Plasmodium vivax"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Plasmodium malariae exhibits the slowest pre-erythrocytic hepatic schizogony and the longest intraerythrocytic cycle (72 hours, producing quartan fever). Its incubation period ranges from 18 to 40 days (frequently longer), compared to 9ظô14 days for P. falciparum and 12ظô18 days for P. vivax and P. ovale."},{"id":"q_college_150","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"All of the following statements regarding Plasmodium falciparum are true, EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Causes more severe disease during pregnancy.","B":"Is associated with recurrent relapses after initial treatment because of liver hypnozoites.","C":"Is the only malarial parasite causing greater than 20% parasitemia.","D":"Infection is typically associated with thrombocytopenia.","E":"Is the only cause of cerebral malaria."},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Plasmodium falciparum does not produce dormant intrahepatic forms (hypnozoites); once hepatic schizonts rupture into the bloodstream, hepatic schizogony is complete. Late relapses from hypnozoites are seen exclusively with Plasmodium vivax and Plasmodium ovale. Recurrence in P. falciparum is termed recrudescence, resulting from surviving blood-stage erythrocytic parasites."},{"id":"q_college_151","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"Mosquitoes are the vector in the following disorder(s):","options":{"A":"Onchocerciasis","B":"Visceral leishmaniasis","C":"African trypanosomiasis","D":"Bancroftian filariasis"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Bancroftian filariasis (Wuchereria bancrofti) is transmitted by mosquitoes (Culex, Anopheles, and Aedes species). In contrast, onchocerciasis is transmitted by blackflies (Simulium), visceral leishmaniasis by phlebotomine sandflies, and African trypanosomiasis (sleeping sickness) by tsetse flies (Glossina)."},{"id":"q_college_152","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"All of the following are risk factors for the development of Legionella pneumonia EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Glucocorticoid use","B":"HIV infection","C":"Neutropenia","D":"Recent surgery","E":"Tobacco use"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Legionella is an intracellular pathogen held in check by cell-mediated immunity (macrophages and T cells), not polymorphonuclear leukocytes. Risk factors include impaired cell-mediated immunity (HIV, corticosteroid therapy), cigarette smoking, chronic heart/lung disease, and recent surgery. Isolated neutropenia is not a classic predisposing factor."},{"id":"q_college_153","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"Crescent-shaped or banana-shaped gametocytes are seen in infection with:","options":{"A":"Plasmodium vivax","B":"Plasmodium falciparum","C":"Plasmodium ovale","D":"Plasmodium malariae"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The presence of elongated, crescentic, or banana-shaped gametocytes on a Giemsa-stained thin blood film is a unique morphological feature that identifies Plasmodium falciparum. The gametocytes of P. vivax, P. ovale, and P. malariae are round or oval."},{"id":"q_college_154","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"A clinical situation in which the immune system or a therapy failed to eliminate all the Plasmodium spp.-infected erythrocytes and the numbers of Plasmodium spp. in RBCs begin to increase again with subsequent clinical symptoms is called:","options":{"A":"Latency","B":"Re-infection","C":"Relapse","D":"Recrudescence"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Recrudescence is the resurgence of symptomatic parasitemia originating from persistent sub-patent erythrocytic parasites that survived incomplete or ineffective antimalarial drug therapy. Relapse refers specifically to reactivation of latent liver stages (hypnozoites) characteristic of P. vivax and P. ovale."},{"id":"q_college_155","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"Worldwide, malaria accounts for approximately how many clinical cases annually?","options":{"A":"One million cases per year","B":"More than 200 million cases per year","C":"Less than one million cases per year"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"According to WHO World Malaria Reports, global annual incidence consistently exceeds 200 million clinical cases, primarily concentrated in sub-Saharan Africa."},{"id":"q_college_156","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"The sexual reproduction cycle (sporogony) of Plasmodium takes place in the:","options":{"A":"Liver parenchymal cells","B":"Red blood cells","C":"White blood cells","D":"Midgut of the female Anopheles mosquito"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Microgametocytes and macrogametocytes fuse in the mosquito midgut to form an ookinete and subsequently an oocyst, which ruptures to release sporozoites into the salivary glands."},{"id":"q_college_157","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"Severe or complicated malaria is most likely to develop in:","options":{"A":"Young children","B":"Pregnant females","C":"Both a and b"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Non-immune young children (<5 years) and pregnant women (due to placental parasite sequestration) are at highest risk for severe, life-threatening Plasmodium falciparum malaria."},{"id":"q_college_158","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"First-line therapy for uncomplicated Plasmodium falciparum malaria is:","options":{"A":"Chloroquine monotherapy","B":"Chloroquine + primaquine","C":"Artemisinin-based Combination Therapy (ACT)"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Because chloroquine resistance is widespread globally, oral ACTs (e.g., artemether-lumefantrine) are the first-line standard of care for uncomplicated P. falciparum."},{"id":"q_college_159","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"Individuals with sickle-cell trait (HbAS) are:","options":{"A":"More liable to severe malaria","B":"Less liable to severe malaria","C":"Unaffected in their malaria risk"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Sickle-cell trait provides innate protection against severe, complicated, and fatal Plasmodium falciparum malaria by impairing intraerythrocytic parasite proliferation and increasing clearance of parasitized cells."},{"id":"q_college_160","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"When should mefloquine chemoprophylaxis be started before traveling to a malaria-endemic area?","options":{"A":"1 day before travelling","B":"2 days before travelling","C":"3 days before travelling","D":"1 to 2 weeks before travelling"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Mefloquine is initiated 1 to 2 weeks prior to departure to ensure adequate steady-state blood concentrations and to monitor for potential neuropsychiatric adverse effects."},{"id":"q_college_161","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"Which drug destroys the late hepatic stages and latent tissue forms (hypnozoites) of P. vivax and P. ovale?","options":{"A":"Primaquine","B":"Proguanil","C":"Quinine","D":"Mefloquine"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Primaquine (and tafenoquine) is the primary 8-aminoquinoline active against dormant intrahepatic hypnozoites of Plasmodium vivax and Plasmodium ovale, providing radical cure and preventing delayed clinical relapses. G6PD activity must be documented prior to administration."},{"id":"q_college_162","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"Chloroquine is indicated in the following disorders, EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Hepatic amebiasis","B":"Type 2 Lepra reaction (Erythema nodosum leprosum)","C":"Discoid lupus erythematosus (DLE)","D":"Gouty arthritis"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Chloroquine/hydroxychloroquine possesses anti-inflammatory and antimalarial properties indicated in amebic liver abscess, autoimmune connective tissue disorders (DLE, RA), and type 2 lepra reactions, but has no therapeutic role in gouty arthritis."},{"id":"q_college_163","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"What is the recommended prophylaxis against malaria?","options":{"A":"Atovaquone-proguanil","B":"Chloroquine","C":"Doxycycline","D":"Mefloquine","E":"Any of the above can be used depending on geographic destination and resistance"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"Antimalarial chemoprophylaxis is tailored to destination-specific resistance patterns: atovaquone-proguanil, doxycycline, or mefloquine are used in chloroquine-resistant areas, while chloroquine remains effective for chloroquine-sensitive regions (e.g., parts of Central America and the Caribbean)."},{"id":"q_college_164","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"Which of the following is NOT a classic hallmark feature of severe Plasmodium falciparum","options":{"A":"Acute tubular necrosis (acute renal failure)","B":"Severe hemolytic anemia (Hematocrit <15%)","C":"Massive fulminant hepatic necrosis","D":"Severe hypoglycemia","E":"Cerebral malaria with obtundation and coma"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Severe falciparum malaria causes hyperparasitemia leading to severe anemia, acute kidney injury (blackwater fever / ATN), cerebral malaria, lactic acidosis, and hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia. While mild- to-moderate jaundice is common, massive ischemic or fulminant hepatic necrosis is not typical."},{"id":"q_college_165","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Malaria","text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding malaria?","options":{"A":"Malaria is transmitted by the bite of female Anopheles mosquitoes","B":"Malaria can be transmitted via contaminated blood transfusions or congenitally","C":"The most common presenting clinical symptom is fever","D":"Anemia and splenomegaly are common features","E":"All of the above"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"Malaria is vector-borne via female Anopheles mosquitoes, can spread parenterally through infected blood products, and presents with febrile paroxysms, rigors, hemolytic anemia, and reactive splenomegaly."},{"id":"q_college_166","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Intestinal Parasites","text":"All the following parasites inhabit the large intestine:","options":{"A":"Ascaris lumbricoides","B":"Strongyloides stercoralis","C":"Ancylostoma duodenale","D":"Enterobius vermicularis"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Enterobius vermicularis (pinworm) is a classic nematode of the human large intestine, primarily inhabiting the lumen of the cecum, appendix, and ascending colon. In contrast, adult forms of Ascaris lumbricoides, Strongyloides stercoralis, and Ancylostoma duodenale (hookworm) reside exclusively in the small intestine (jejunum and duodenum)."},{"id":"q_college_167","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Intestinal Parasites","text":"All the following tapeworms are transmitted via ingestion of improperly cooked meat, EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Taenia solium","B":"Taenia saginata","C":"Diphyllobothrium latum"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Taenia saginata and Taenia solium are contracted through the ingestion of undercooked or raw mammalian meat (beef containing Cysticercus bovis and pork containing Cysticercus cellulosae, respectively). In contrast, Diphyllobothrium latum (the broad fish tapeworm) is transmitted strictly through the consumption of raw or inadequately cooked freshwater fish harboring plerocercoid larvae (spargana)."},{"id":"q_college_168","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Intestinal Parasites","text":"All the following parasites can be complicated by acute appendicitis, EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Hymenolepis nana","B":"Taenia saginata","C":"Taenia solium","D":"Ascaris lumbricoides"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Ascaris lumbricoides and detached motile proglottids of Taenia species can mechanically obstruct the narrow lumen of the vermiform appendix, precipitating secondary bacterial stasis and acute appendicitis. Conversely, Hymenolepis nana (the dwarf tapeworm) measures only 15ظô40 mm in length and remains superficially attached to the mucosal villi of the ileum, lacking the bulk or mechanical potential to cause appendiceal luminal obstruction."},{"id":"q_college_169","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Intestinal Parasites","text":"The following parasite enters the human body via the skin:","options":{"A":"Enterobius vermicularis","B":"Ancylostoma duodenale","C":"Taenia solium","D":"Ascaris lumbricoides"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Infective filariform (L3) larvae of Ancylostoma duodenale actively penetrate intact human skin (most often through bare feet in contaminated soil), causing localized cutaneous pruritus (\"ground itch\") before entering venous circulation to begin cardiopulmonary migration. Enterobius, Ascaris, and Taenia are acquired through oral ingestion of embryonated eggs or cystic larvae."},{"id":"q_college_170","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Intestinal Parasites","text":"All of the following are clinical manifestations of Ascaris lumbricoides infection EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Asymptomatic intestinal carriage","B":"Acute eosinophilic meningoencephalitis with nuchal rigidity","C":"L├╢ffler syndrome (dry cough, wheezing, and pulmonary eosinophilia)","D":"Right upper quadrant pain and biliary colic","E":"Mechanical small-bowel obstruction"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Ascaris lumbricoides causes pulmonary larval migration (L├╢ffler syndrome), mechanical bolus intestinal obstruction in heavy infections, and erratic biliary migration (cholangitis/biliary colic). Eosinophilic meningoencephalitis is characteristic of Angiostrongylus cantonensis or Baylisascaris procyonis."},{"id":"q_college_171","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Intestinal Parasites","text":"All of the following statements regarding amebic liver abscess are true EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"It is the most common extraintestinal manifestation of amebiasis","B":"Deep clinical jaundice is usually present in the majority of cases","C":"Most individuals do not have concurrent active colitis symptoms","D":"Classic presentation consists of right upper quadrant pain, tender hepatomegaly, and fever","E":"Right-sided pleural effusion is a common reactive finding"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Amebic liver abscess (Entamoeba histolytica) classically presents with fever, dull RUQ pain, tender hepatomegaly, and reactive right pleural effusion; overt clinical jaundice is uncommon (<10% of cases) unless a large abscess mechanically compresses major biliary ducts."},{"id":"q_college_172","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Intestinal Parasites","text":"A mother brings her 5-year-old son to the clinic complaining that he has been experiencing severe perianal itching, especially at night, which disrupts his sleep. What is the diagnostic test of choice to confirm the suspected parasite?","options":{"A":"Formalin-ether concentration stool examination","B":"Cellophane tape test (Scotch tape test) applied to the perianal area in the morning","C":"Duodenal aspirate microscopy","D":"Serum IgE antibody levels"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The clinical presentation is highly indicative of Enterobius vermicularis (pinworm) infection. Adult female worms migrate out through the anus at night to oviposit on the perianal skin folds. Applying clear adhesive cellophane tape to the perianal area upon waking (before bathing or defecation) collects the asymmetric, planar-convex eggs for microscopic identification; standard stool examinations are positive in under 10% of cases."},{"id":"q_college_173","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Intestinal Parasites","text":"Which of the following cestodes is unique because it can complete its entire life cycle (both larval and adult phases) within a single human host without the absolute requirement of an intermediate host?","options":{"A":"Taenia saginata","B":"Hymenolepis nana","C":"Diphyllobothrium latum","D":"Taenia solium"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Hymenolepis nana (dwarf tapeworm) is the only human cestode capable of direct transmission and internal autoinfection. Ingested embryonated oncospheres penetrate intestinal villi to develop into cysticercoids, which subsequently re-enter the intestinal lumen to develop directly into mature adult tapeworms without requiring an obligate intermediate host."},{"id":"q_college_174","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Intestinal Parasites","text":"A 28-year-old agricultural worker in a tropical region presents with chronic watery diarrhea, epigastric pain, and a localized creeping serpiginous urticarial rash on his buttocks (larva currens). Stool examination reveals rhabditiform larvae. This parasite is unique because it can cause a life- threatening \"hyperinfection syndrome\" in immunocompromised hosts. What is the organism?","options":{"A":"Strongyloides stercoralis","B":"Necator americanus","C":"Trichuris trichiura","D":"Hymenolepis nana"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Strongyloides stercoralis can maintain an indefinite cycle of internal autoinfection in humans, where rhabditiform larvae transform into infective filariform larvae within the gastrointestinal lumen and penetrate the perianal skin or bowel wall. In immunocompromised individuals (such as patients taking systemic corticosteroids or those co-infected with HTLV-1), this transforms into hyperinfection syndrome or disseminated strongyloidiasis with secondary polymicrobial Gram-negative sepsis."},{"id":"q_college_175","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Intestinal Parasites","text":"A 6-year-old child from a rural area presents with chronic abdominal pain, bloody mucoid diarrhea, and iron-deficiency anemia. During a severe straining episode, the mother notices partial rectal mucosal prolapse. Stool examination shows barrel-shaped eggs with prominent clear bipolar plugs. What is the causative parasite?","options":{"A":"Ascaris lumbricoides","B":"Ancylostoma duodenale","C":"Trichuris trichiura (whipworm)","D":"Enterobius vermicularis"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Trichuris trichiura (whipworm) embeds its thin anterior portion into the cecal and colonic mucosa. Heavy infections in children cause Trichuris dysentery syndrome, chronic anemia, tenesmus, and rectal prolapse due to intense mucosal edema and straining. The eggs are morphologically diagnostic: thick-walled, barrel- shaped (lemon-shaped) with distinct transparent bipolar plugs."},{"id":"q_college_176","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Typhoid & Cholera","text":"The characteristic rash seen in typhoid fever is:","options":{"A":"Maculopapular rash on palms and soles","B":"Rose spots on the abdomen and chest","C":"Vesicular rash over the trunk","D":"Petechial rash over the lower limbs"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Rose spots represent transient, blanching, salmon-pink, non-itchy maculopapular lesions (2ظô4 mm in diameter) appearing characteristically on the anterior abdomen and lower chest during the second week of untreated typhoid fever. They are formed by localized bacterial embolization of Salmonella typhi into cutaneous capillaries within the papillary dermis."},{"id":"q_college_177","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Typhoid & Cholera","text":"The ظ£step-ladderظإ pattern of fever is characteristic of:","options":{"A":"Typhoid fever","B":"Malaria","C":"Brucellosis","D":"Dengue fever"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The classic first-week manifestation of typhoid fever is an insidious, daily step-ladder elevation of temperature: each day the fever rises to a higher evening peak with lower morning troughs until it reaches a continuous, high plateau (>39┬░C to 40┬░C) during the second week."},{"id":"q_college_178","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Typhoid & Cholera","text":"Which organ is primarily involved in the chronic carrier state of typhoid?","options":{"A":"Liver","B":"Gallbladder","C":"Spleen","D":"Kidney"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The gallbladder is the primary anatomical sanctuary for Salmonella typhi in chronic carriers (defined as shedding bacteria in feces or urine for $>12$ months). S. typhi establishes dense persistent biofilms on the surface of cholesterol gallstones and within the biliary epithelium, evading both circulating immunoglobulins and bile salts."},{"id":"q_college_179","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Typhoid & Cholera","text":"The Widal test detects antibodies against which antigens?","options":{"A":"O and H antigens of Salmonella typhi","B":"M and T antigens of Streptococcus","C":"O and K antigens of Vibrio cholerae","D":"A and B antigens of E. coli"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The classic Widal serological agglutination assay detects human serum antibodies directed against two primary structural antigens of Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi: the somatic lipopolysaccharide \"O\" antigen (indicating active/recent infection when $>1:80-1:160$) and the flagellar protein \"H\" antigen (titer $>1:160$, indicating recent infection, past illness, or immunization)."},{"id":"q_college_180","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Typhoid & Cholera","text":"One of the serious complications of untreated typhoid fever is:","options":{"A":"Hepatic failure","B":"Intestinal perforation","C":"Acute glomerulonephritis","D":"Meningitis"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Intestinal perforation typically occurs during the third week of untreated illness due to hyperplastic enlargement, ischemic necrosis, and subsequent ulceration of Peyer''s patches in the terminal ileum. It manifests with acute peritoneal signs, rigid abdomen, loss of liver dullness, and septic peritonitis requiring emergency laparotomy."},{"id":"q_college_181","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Typhoid & Cholera","text":"The most important laboratory finding in severe cholera is:","options":{"A":"Leukocytosis","B":"Hyponatremia and hypokalemia","C":"Hyperglycemia","D":"Elevated liver enzymes"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The voluminous secretory diarrhea produced in cholera contains isotonic to mildly hypotonic concentrations of sodium, but dangerously high concentrations of potassium and bicarbonate (often twice to five times that of plasma). Severe dehydration is accompanied by hypokalemia, metabolic acidosis (due to bicarbonate loss), and variable hyponatremia."},{"id":"q_college_182","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Typhoid & Cholera","text":"The most effective preventive measure against cholera in endemic areas is:","options":{"A":"Use of antibiotics","B":"Vaccination and safe water supply","C":"Avoiding raw vegetables","D":"Vitamin supplementation"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Cholera is transmitted primarily via the fecal-oral route through sewage-contaminated drinking water supplies. Long-term containment and prevention rely on clean municipal water sanitation, proper sewage treatment, and the strategic deployment of oral cholera vaccines (OCVs) in high-risk endemic communities. Prophylactic mass antibiotics are strictly avoided to prevent widespread drug resistance."},{"id":"q_college_183","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Typhoid & Cholera","text":"Vibrio cholerae is best described as:","options":{"A":"Gram-positive coccus","B":"Gram-negative comma-shaped bacillus","C":"Gram-negative cocci","D":"Gram-positive rod"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Vibrio cholerae is a highly motile, asporogenous, facultatively anaerobic Gram-negative curved rod exhibiting a characteristic \"comma\" morphology. It moves via a single polar flagellum producing rapid, darting (shooting star) motility on wet mount microscopy."},{"id":"q_college_184","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Typhoid & Cholera","text":"The cornerstone of treatment in cholera is:","options":{"A":"Antibiotic therapy","B":"Rehydration therapy (oral or IV)","C":"Antispasmodic drugs","D":"Corticosteroids"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Because cholera does not destroy the intestinal mucosa, the intact sodium-glucose cotransport mechanism allows oral rehydration solution (ORS) to rapidly restore circulating intravascular volume and correct acidosis. In patients presenting with severe hypovolemic shock, immediate aggressive intravenous volume replacement (using Ringer''s lactate) is lifesaving; antibiotics serve solely as secondary adjuncts to shorten the duration of shedding."},{"id":"q_college_185","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Typhoid & Cholera","text":"The characteristic stool appearance in cholera is described as:","options":{"A":"Bloody with mucus","B":"Foul-smelling and greenish","C":"Rice-water stool","D":"Mucoid with pus cells"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The pathognomonic stool in acute cholera is termed \"rice-water\" diarrhea: an odorless or faintly sweet/fishy- smelling, translucent gray, non-bilious fluid containing flecks of shed intestinal mucus, enterocytes, and high numbers of vibrios, devoid of gross blood or inflammatory polymorphonuclear leukocytes."},{"id":"q_college_186","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Typhoid & Cholera","text":"Which of the following infectious organisms is most characteristically associated with relative bradycardia (Faget''s sign) during a febrile illness?","options":{"A":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa","B":"Salmonella typhi","C":"Staphylococcus aureus","D":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","E":"Streptococcus pyogenes"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Temperature-pulse dissociation (relative bradycardia / Faget''s sign) is a classic clinical sign in intracellular bacterial and specific viral infections, including Typhoid fever (Salmonella typhi), Legionella pneumophila, Brucella, Yellow fever, and Psittacosis."},{"id":"q_college_187","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Typhoid & Cholera","text":"Which of the following statements regarding the pathophysiology of diarrhea in cholera is true?","options":{"A":"Cholera toxin induces irreversible ADP-ribosylation of the Gs alpha subunit, activating adenylate cyclase and elevating intracellular cAMP","B":"Diarrhea in cholera is caused by acute neutrophilic mucosal ulceration of the colon","C":"Diarrhea in cholera is primarily due to an enzymatic defect preventing sodium-glucose cotransport","D":"High spiking fevers and bloody stools invariably precede the onset of diarrhea","E":"The characteristic stool in cholera is dark brown and malodorous with sheets of pus cells"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The A1 subunit of cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates the Gs regulatory protein, maintaining adenylate cyclase in an active state. Elevated intracellular enterocyte cAMP opens apical CFTR chloride channels, driving massive secretion of chloride, sodium, bicarbonate, and water into the small intestinal lumen (producing effortless, non-inflammatory \"rice-water\" stools)."},{"id":"q_college_188","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Typhoid & Cholera","text":"Which of the following clinical descriptions accurately characterizes diarrhea in acute cholera?","options":{"A":"Small-volume bloody diarrhea with severe tenesmus and high-grade fever","B":"Profuse, painless, watery \"rice-water\" diarrhea followed subsequently by clear fluid vomiting","C":"Chronic intermittent greasy diarrhea with steatorrhea and flatulence","D":"Bloody diarrhea preceded by 3 days of severe migratory polyarthralgia","E":"Dark black melena with severe epigastric rebound tenderness"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The clinical hallmark of cholera is the sudden onset of effortless, voluminous, painless watery diarrhea with a characteristic fishy/rice-water odor, followed by vomiting, rapidly producing severe isotonic dehydration and hypovolemic shock without preceding fever."},{"id":"q_college_189","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"Symptoms of a heat-related illness include all of the following EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Dizziness and disorientation","B":"Thirst and light sweating","C":"No sweating, but dry, hot skin","D":"Cramps"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Anhidrosis with hot, dry skin is a classic sign of advanced non-exertional heat stroke, but sweating can persist in exertional heat emergencies. In general introductory occupational health screenings, early warning signs of heat illnesses encompass fatigue, orthostatic dizziness, excessive sweating, muscle cramps, and dehydration"},{"id":"q_college_190","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"The most important factors affecting evaporation when exercising are:","options":{"A":"Heat and humidity","B":"Duration","C":"Time of day","D":"Intensity"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Evaporative cooling depends strictly on the water-vapor pressure gradient between the sweat-wetted skin and surrounding ambient air. Elevated ambient temperature coupled with high relative humidity severely blunts the rate of sweat evaporation, impairing the primary mechanism of human thermolysis during physical exertion."},{"id":"q_college_191","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"Which of the following environments would allow for the best convective cooling?","options":{"A":"Running outside with a cool, light wind blowing","B":"Running in the winter wearing a sweat suit, cap, and gloves","C":"Running on a track in an enclosed gym","D":"Running outside in 80-degree F weather and 80-percent humidity"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Convective heat loss involves the transfer of thermal energy from the skin surface to moving air currents. A cool ambient airflow or light wind disrupts the static insulating boundary layer of warm, moist air adjacent to the body, optimizing convective cooling and facilitating cutaneous heat dissipation."},{"id":"q_college_192","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"Which of the following groups is the MOST susceptible to heat illness?","options":{"A":"The highly fit","B":"The elderly","C":"High school students","D":"Those jogging by themselves"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Elderly individuals are predisposed to classic heat stroke due to diminished dermal vascularity, reduced sweat gland responsiveness, blunted thirst perception, chronic underlying cardiovascular or renal comorbidities, and frequent use of medications that impede thermoregulation (such as diuretics, anticholinergics, and beta-blockers)."},{"id":"q_college_193","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"The result of evaporation is:","options":{"A":"No change in temperature","B":"Warming","C":"Cooling","D":"Either cooling or warming, depending on the humidity"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Evaporation is an endothermic phase change where liquid water transforms into vapor, absorbing heat energy (the latent heat of vaporization, approximately 0.58 kcal per gram of evaporated sweat) from the skin surface, cooling the body."},{"id":"q_college_194","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"The best way to determine if environmental conditions are imposing a heat load on your body is to:","options":{"A":"Take your temperature","B":"Weigh yourself before and after exercise to measure water weight loss","C":"Monitor your heart rate","D":"Measure your blood pressure"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Measuring acute changes in nude body weight before and immediately following physical activity provides an objective, direct metric of fluid deficit incurred through sweat evaporation (1 kg of acute body mass deficit equals approximately 1 liter of sweat loss). This guides personalized hydration protocols and monitors thermal strain."},{"id":"q_college_195","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"In order to minimize the possibility of overheating, exercise clothing should be all of the following EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Lightweight","B":"Minimal","C":"Dry","D":"Cotton or linen"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"While breathable, lightweight, loose-fitting, and moisture-wicking fabrics facilitate sweat dispersal and convective airflow, clothing does not have to remain dry to provide protection. Dampened or wet clothing enhances conductive and evaporative heat transfer away from the skin compared to completely dry synthetic insulating fabrics."},{"id":"q_college_196","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"Heat acclimatization:","options":{"A":"Decreases the exercise heart rate","B":"Increases the risk of heat injuries","C":"Needs more research before it should even be considered","D":"Increases body temperature"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Physiological heat acclimatization expands plasma volume, enhances cutaneous blood flow, lowers the core body temperature threshold for sweating, produces more dilute sweat (conserving sodium), and preserves stroke volume. This physiological adaptation reduces cardiovascular strain and lowers submaximal exercise heart rates at any given workload."},{"id":"q_college_197","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"Symptoms of heat exhaustion are:","options":{"A":"Profuse sweating and clammy skin","B":"Increased blood pressure","C":"Seizures","D":"Hot, red, dry skin"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Heat exhaustion is characterized by volume depletion secondary to prolonged sweating, presenting with profuse diaphoresis, pale/cool/clammy skin, orthostatic hypotension, tachycardia, headache, and weakness, with core body temperature remaining $<40^\\circ\\text{C}$ and intact central nervous system function."},{"id":"q_college_198","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"Heat cramps, heat exhaustion, and heat stroke are similar in all the following ways EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Very heavy sweating","B":"Significant loss of water and electrolytes","C":"High inner core temperature of the body","D":"Increased heat storage in the body"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Core body temperature remains normal or only mildly elevated ($<38^\\circ\\text{C}$) in heat cramps and moderately elevated ($<40^\\circ\\text{C}$) in heat exhaustion without central nervous system impairment. Severe hyperpyrexia with a core temperature exceeding $40^\\circ\\text{C}$ ($>104^\\circ\\text{F}$) combined with profound encephalopathy is unique to heat stroke."},{"id":"q_college_199","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"When exercising in the heat, it is recommended that all of the following be kept in mind EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Monitor your heart rate","B":"Keep the exercise session short","C":"Drink plenty of water before, during and after the workout","D":"Take a salt tablet after the workout"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Salt tablets are contraindicated in managing heat stress because they introduce concentrated hypertonic sodium loads into the gastric lumen, causing delayed gastric emptying, nausea, vomiting, and predisposing the patient to hypernatremic dehydration. Electrolyte replacement should instead occur via balanced oral electrolyte-carbohydrate beverages."},{"id":"q_college_200","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"Heat injury can occur when the activity heat load exceeds the body''s ability to regulate body temperatur","options":{"E":"Which of the following is not a common type of heat injury?","A":"Heat evaporation","B":"Heat cramps","C":"Heat exhaustion","D":"Heat stroke"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Heat cramps, heat syncope, heat exhaustion, and heat stroke are clinical categories of heat illness. \"Heat evaporation\" is a physiological mechanism of thermolysis (heat dissipation), not a clinical diagnosis or heat injury."},{"id":"q_college_201","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"What is the proper technical term for the heat load to which a worker is exposed?","options":{"A":"Heat pressure","B":"Heat strain","C":"Heat stress","D":"Heat tension"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"In industrial hygiene and occupational physiology, heat stress represents the net aggregate environmental heat load imposed on a worker (composed of metabolic heat generation plus environmental factors including ambient temperature, humidity, wind velocity, and radiant heat). Heat strain refers to the resulting physiological physiological response mounted by the body (e.g., heart rate, sweat rate, core temperature)."},{"id":"q_college_202","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"What are possible medical consequences from excessive levels of heat at work?","options":{"A":"Rashes","B":"Muscle cramps","C":"Fatigue","D":"Fainting","E":"Death F. All of the above"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Excessive thermal exposure causes clinical complications ranging from mild superficial problems (miliaria rubra / heat rash) to painful muscle spasms (heat cramps), peripheral pooling syncope, heat exhaustion, and fatal multiorgan failure resulting from heat stroke."},{"id":"q_college_203","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"__________ is brief fainting or near-fainting due to heat stress.","options":{"A":"Heat exhaustion","B":"Heat strain","C":"Heat stroke","D":"Heat syncope","E":"Heat trauma"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Heat syncope is a transient loss of consciousness or postural orthostatic collapse occurring in hot environments. It results from cutaneous vasodilation and peripheral blood pooling in the lower extremities combined with relative intravascular volume depletion, causing a transient decrease in cerebral perfusion."},{"id":"q_college_204","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"__________ is a life-threatening medical condition due to heat stress where the body temperature rises above 104┬░F.","options":{"A":"Heat exhaustion","B":"Heat strain","C":"Heat stroke","D":"Heat syncope"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Heat stroke is an emergency defined clinically by severe core hyperthermia (rectal temperature $>40^\\circ\\text{C}$ or $>104^\\circ\\text{F}$) accompanied by central nervous system dysfunction (encephalopathy, delirium, seizures, or coma) and systemic inflammatory response leading to multiorgan failure."},{"id":"q_college_205","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"What environmental factors affect the worker''s level of heat stress?","options":{"A":"Air temperature","B":"Relative humidity","C":"Air speed","D":"Direct sunlight","E":"Barometric pressure"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Occupational heat stress is governed by four primary environmental parameters: dry-bulb air temperature (convective gradient), relative humidity (evaporative gradient), air velocity/wind speed (boundary layer dissipation), and mean radiant heat from direct solar radiation or hot industrial machinery."},{"id":"q_college_206","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"The main way the body loses heat is through evaporative cooling of moisture on the skin surfac","options":{"A":"True","B":"False"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The explanation follows the heat physiology or occupational heat-illness principle tested in the source."},{"id":"q_college_207","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"High humidity increases heat stress by interfering with evaporative cooling.","options":{"A":"True","B":"False"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"When ambient environmental temperature equals or exceeds mean skin temperature (~35┬░C), the gradients for radiation, conduction, and convection diminish or reverse, transferring environmental heat into the body. In this setting, the evaporation of sweat becomes the primary physiological avenue available to dissipate metabolic"},{"id":"q_college_208","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"Increased air movement can prevent heat stress by increasing evaporative cooling.","options":{"A":"True","B":"False"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Air velocity enhances convective heat dissipation and rapidly disperses the thin, humid boundary layer of air resting over the skin surface. This maintains a steep water-vapor pressure gradient, facilitating continuous evaporation of sweat even in warm environments."},{"id":"q_college_209","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"The risk of heat stress indoors at 90 ° F equals outdoors in direct sunlight at 90 ° F.","options":{"A":"True","B":"False"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Ambient dry-bulb temperature alone does not reflect total environmental thermal load. Direct sunlight adds solar radiant heat energy, which significantly elevates the mean radiant temperature and overall Wet Bulb Globe Temperature (WBGT) index, substantially increasing the risk of heat strain outdoors compared to an identical indoor air temperature."},{"id":"q_college_210","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"How long should a newly assigned employee in a high heat area be closely observed (CAL/OSH","options":{"A":"3 Hours","B":"8 Hours","C":"2 Shifts","D":"5 Work Days","E":"1 Month F. 14 Days"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Occupational acclimatization protocols (including Cal/OSHA standards) require new workers or those returning from extended absences to undergo structured, supervised acclimatization over a 14-day window. Full cardiovascular and sudomotor adaptations (e.g., expanded plasma volume, lower resting core temperature, and increased, diluted sweat production) require 10ظô14 consecutive days of gradual thermal exposure."},{"id":"q_college_211","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"Most recent heat-related fatality in California occurred in which occupation?","options":{"A":"Picking strawberries","B":"Welding steel","C":"Pouring molten metal","D":"Washing dishes","E":"Cutting lawns"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Agricultural field workers, particularly strawberry and fruit pickers, perform sustained heavy physical labor in direct sunlight during summer peaks. The combination of high ambient temperatures, direct radiant load, and high humidity from irrigated soil places agricultural laborers at the highest statistical risk for fatal occupational heat stroke."},{"id":"q_college_212","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"The worker experienced early warning signs but ignored them and continued working.","options":{"A":"True","B":"False"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"A primary human behavioral factor leading to fatal heat stroke is continuing strenuous exertion despite early symptoms of heat exhaustion (such as headache, dizziness, nausea, and cramping). Failure to rest in shade and hydrate allows core body temperature to rapidly exceed 40┬░C, precipitating systemic inflammatory collapse and encephalopathy."},{"id":"q_college_213","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"Signs of impending heat-related illness include:","options":{"A":"Visible sweating","B":"Sweat-soaked clothing","C":"Nausea","D":"Elevated heart rate","E":"Agitation F. All of the above"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Impending heat decompensation activates profound sympathetic and thermoregulatory reflexes, presenting with intense diaphoresis, compensatory sinus tachycardia, splanchnic vasoconstriction causing nausea/vomiting, and early central nervous system neuroglycopenic/thermal distress manifesting as irritability or agitation."},{"id":"q_college_214","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"Measures that prevent heat-related illness include:","options":{"A":"Light clothing","B":"Rest breaks in shade","C":"Drinking water","D":"Staff training","E":"Written procedures F. All of the above"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Occupational heat illness prevention requires engineering and behavioral controls: breathable lightweight fabrics, mandatory rest cycles in cool shaded environments, proactive fluid hydration (1 quart per hour of water during heavy work), formal supervisor/worker heat illness training, and an active written compliance plan."},{"id":"q_college_215","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"The Heat Illness Prevention Program should be activated at 85 ° F.","options":{"A":"True","B":"False"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Under Cal/OSHA standards, basic heat illness prevention measures (including provision of water and shade) must be provided when temperatures reach or exceed 80┬░F (26.6┬░C). High-heat proceduresظ¤which mandate mandatory buddy monitoring and minimum rest breaksظ¤are triggered at 95┬░F."},{"id":"q_college_216","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"Employees should not self-limit work due to heat.","options":{"A":"True","B":"False"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Workers must be encouraged to self-limit exertion and seek immediate shade and hydration upon experiencing early warning signs of heat strain (e.g., lightheadedness, excessive thirst, nausea). Denying self-pacing or working through symptoms leads directly to exertional heat exhaustion and heat stroke."},{"id":"q_college_217","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"Employees have no role in preventing coworker heat illness.","options":{"A":"True","B":"False"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Peer monitoring via a \"buddy system\" is an essential safety component of heat illness prevention programs. Because advancing heat exhaustion and heat stroke impair judgment and induce delirium, coworkers are often the first to recognize subtle cognitive changes, ataxia, or lethargy in an affected individual."},{"id":"q_college_218","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"Employers are legally obligated to protect employees from heat illness.","options":{"A":"True","B":"False"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Under the OSHA General Duty Clause and specific state regulations (such as Cal/OSHA Title 8, Section 3395), employers have a binding legal obligation to provide a working environment free from recognized thermal hazards by providing potable drinking water, access to shade, acclimatization periods, and emergency medical response."},{"id":"q_college_219","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"Employers must provide air conditioning in summer.","options":{"A":"True","B":"False"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Occupational heat regulations do not mandate the installation of mechanical air conditioning for all work environments, particularly in agricultural, construction, or outdoor industrial settings. Instead, regulations mandate adequate natural or artificial shade, ventilation, scheduled cool-down breaks, and cold drinking water."},{"id":"q_college_220","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Heat Related Illness","text":"Employers are required to provide drinking water.","options":{"A":"True","B":"False"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Workplace safety standards legally mandate that employers provide fresh, pure, suitably cool potable drinking water free of charge, located as close as practical to work areas, with quantities sufficient to allow at least one quart per employee per hour of continuous physical labor."},{"id":"q_college_221","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"The most common organism causing community-acquired pneumonia (CAP) is:","options":{"A":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","B":"Haemophilus influenzae","C":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae","D":"Staphylococcus aureus"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Streptococcus pneumoniae (pneumococcus) is the leading bacterial cause of community-acquired pneumonia across all age groups worldwide, accounting for roughly 30ظô50% of all hospital-admitted adult bacterial pneumonia cases."},{"id":"q_college_222","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"Possible causative organisms causing pneumonia include:","options":{"A":"Viruses","B":"Fungi","C":"Bacteria","D":"All of the above"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Infectious pneumonia represents microbial invasion of the alveolar spaces that can be elicited by a wide spectrum of pathogens, including pyogenic and atypical bacteria (S. pneumoniae, Legionella), respiratory viruses (influenza, RSV, SARS-CoV-2), and opportunistic or endemic fungi (Histoplasma, Pneumocystis jirovecii)"},{"id":"q_college_223","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"Which of the following is an appropriate technique for obtaining a sample in suspected","options":{"A":"Thoracocentesis","B":"Bronchoscopy","C":"Sputum culture","D":"Blood culture"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Expectorated or induced sputum Gram stain and microbiological culture is the standard non-invasive frontline diagnostic test used to identify the lower respiratory pathogen in patients with productive cough, provided the sample contains $<10$ squamous epithelial cells and $>25$ leukocytes per low-power field (Murray-Washington criteria)."},{"id":"q_college_224","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"What is the important definitive diagnostic tool in pneumonia?","options":{"A":"Chest X-ray","B":"CT scan","C":"Blood culture","D":"Sputum culture"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"A plain chest radiograph is the cornerstone imaging modality required to confirm the clinical diagnosis of pneumonia by demonstrating the presence of acute parenchymal consolidation, air bronchograms, or patchy alveolar infiltrates, while differentiating pneumonia from alternative pathologies like acute bronchitis."},{"id":"q_college_225","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"An important complaint in elderly patients with pneumonia is:","options":{"A":"Confusion","B":"Altered mental status","C":"Coma","D":"All of the above"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Geriatric patients frequently present atypically with blunted inflammatory responses, often lacking classic high-grade fevers, leukocytosis, or cough. In this population, acute encephalopathyظ¤ranging from mild confusion and delirium to lethargy, obtundation, and comaظ¤is a frequent presenting manifestation driven by systemic hypoxia, cytokines, and microvascular hypoperfusion."},{"id":"q_college_226","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"A common clinical presentation of pneumonia in children is:","options":{"A":"Rapid breathing (tachypne","B":"Bradycardia","C":"Hypertension","D":"Hematuria"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"According to WHO pediatric diagnostic algorithms, tachypnea (fast breathing) is the most sensitive and reliable clinical sign of lower respiratory tract infection and alveolar consolidation in infants and young children, whose compliant chest walls and small airway diameters necessitate an increased respiratory rate to maintain alveolar minute ventilation."},{"id":"q_college_227","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"The most common mode of transmission of respiratory infections causing pneumonia is:","options":{"A":"Inhalation of respiratory droplets","B":"Fecal-oral transmission","C":"Blood transfusion","D":"Insect bites"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Most respiratory pathogens causing pneumonia are transmitted via infectious respiratory droplets ($>5$ ╬╝m) or fine aerosolized droplet nuclei expelled during coughing, sneezing, or talking, which are subsequently inhaled by a susceptible host or colonize the upper nasopharynx before microaspiration into the distal airways"},{"id":"q_college_228","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"A common viral cause/risk factor associated with pneumonia is:","options":{"A":"Influenza virus","B":"Hepatitis B virus","C":"HIV","D":"Rabies virus"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Influenza A and B viruses are common direct causes of primary viral pneumonia and serve as the primary predisposing viral risk factor for secondary bacterial pneumonia, primarily by denuding the respiratory ciliary epithelium and impairing mucociliary clearance."},{"id":"q_college_229","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"Causative agents of pneumonia include:","options":{"A":"Coronavirus","B":"Influenza virus","C":"Streptococcus","D":"All of the above"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Lower respiratory infections encompass diverse viral and bacterial pathogens; Streptococcus pneumoniae is the principal bacterial pathogen, while Coronaviruses (SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV) and Influenza viruses represent major viral causes of acute hypoxemic lung parenchymal consolidation."},{"id":"q_college_230","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"What are the drugs of choice for the treatment of very severe pneumonia?","options":{"A":"Cotrimoxazole","B":"Benzyl penicillin","C":"Ampicillin","D":"None of the above"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"In traditional and classical WHO guidelines for severe/very severe pneumonia, high-dose parenteral Benzylpenicillin (Penicillin G) administered intravenously or intramuscularly served as the historical first-line bactericidal drug of choice to cover penicillin-sensitive Streptococcus pneumoniae, before the widespread availability of third-generation cephalosporins."},{"id":"q_college_231","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"In which gender is the incidence of pneumonia more common?","options":{"A":"Male","B":"Female","C":"Equal involvement of male and female","D":"None of the above"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Epidemiological studies demonstrate a consistently higher incidence, hospitalization rate, and mortality from community-acquired pneumonia in males across all life stages. This is attributed to higher historical rates of smoking and alcohol use, anatomical airway variations, and protective immunomodulatory effects of estrogen on alveolar macrophage function in females"},{"id":"q_college_232","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"Fast breathing among 2-month-old children is defined as a respiratory rate of more than:","options":{"A":"49 breaths/min","B":"59 breaths/min","C":"19 breaths/min","D":"69 breaths/min"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Under WHO Integrated Management of Childhood Illness (IMCI) diagnostic criteria, age-specific cutoffs for tachypnea are defined as: ظت Under 2 months: $\\ge 60$ breaths/min (more than 59 breaths/min). ظت 2 to 11 months: $\\ge 50$ breaths/min. ظت 12 months to 5 years: $\\ge 40$ breaths/min."},{"id":"q_college_233","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"Which age groups are mostly at risk of developing pneumonia?","options":{"A":"Less than 2 years","B":"Greater than 65 years","C":"5ظô9 years","D":"Both a and b"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Pneumonia exhibits a bimodal age distribution, with severe incidence and complications concentrated at the extremes of age: children $<2$ years (due to an immature immune system, small airways, and lack of previous antibody exposure) and elderly adults $>65$ years (due to immunosenescence, blunted cough reflexes, and chronic comorbidities)."},{"id":"q_college_234","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"What is the most important feature for diagnosing severe pneumonia?","options":{"A":"Chest indrawing","B":"Fast breathing","C":"Severe respiratory distress","D":"Central cyanosis"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"In clinical pediatric algorithms, central cyanosis (bluish discoloration of the lips, tongue, and oral mucous membranes) represents critical arterial hypoxemia ($SpO_2 < 85\\%$) resulting from severe ventilation- perfusion mismatch and extensive alveolar consolidation, designating the condition as very severe pneumonia requiring immediate oxygenation."},{"id":"q_college_235","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"What is the period of communicability of pneumococcal disease?","options":{"A":"6ظô24 days","B":"5ظô15 days","C":"2ظô10 days","D":"Unknown"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"The precise period of communicability for Streptococcus pneumoniae is technically unknown. Asymptomatic nasopharyngeal carriage can persist for weeks to months in healthy individuals, and transmission ceases rapidly within 24ظô48 hours following the initiation of effective antimicrobial therapy."},{"id":"q_college_236","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"What is the follow-up period for the treatment of pneumonia?","options":{"A":"2ظô4 days","B":"2 days","C":"1ظô2 days","D":"5 days"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Clinical assessment at 5 days aligns with international guidelines (IDSA/ATS) evaluating clinical stabilityظ¤ ensuring resolution of fever for $>48$ hours, normalization of vitals, and absence of complicationsظ¤to determine whether oral outpatient antimicrobial therapy can safely be completed."},{"id":"q_college_237","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"What is the temporal pattern of pneumonia?","options":{"A":"More common in winter","B":"More common in summer","C":"More common in early spring","D":"Both a and c"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Community-acquired pneumonia demonstrates clear winter seasonality in temperate zones. This surge correlates with increased indoor crowding, cold, dry air impairing mucosal ciliary clearance, and coinciding seasonal epidemics of viral respiratory infections (Influenza, RSV) that prime the airways for secondary bacterial invasion."},{"id":"q_college_238","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"What is the major characteristic of pneumonia?","options":{"A":"Inflammation of air spaces in the lungs","B":"Persistent coughing","C":"High-grade fever","D":"None of the above"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Pathologically, pneumonia is defined as acute inflammation and consolidation of the pulmonary parenchyma distal to the terminal bronchioles, characterized by the exudation of inflammatory cells (neutrophils, fibrin, and erythrocytes) into the alveolar air spaces, impairing alveolar gas exchange."},{"id":"q_college_239","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"The single most useful clinical sign of severity of pneumonia in a person without underlying lung disease is?","options":{"A":"Respiratory rate >30/minute","B":"Pulse rate >100/minute","C":"Hypothermia","D":"Postural fall of SBP >10 mm Hg"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Tachypnea with a respiratory rate $\\ge 30\\text{ breaths/min}$ is a sensitive bedside indicator of significant hypoxemia, loss of pulmonary compliance, and physiological respiratory exhaustion. It is a core component of pneumonia severity indices (CURB-65 and PSI), warranting hospital admission."},{"id":"q_college_240","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"Hospital-acquired pneumonia (HAP) is defined as pneumonia occurring how many hours after hospital admission?","options":{"A":"At least 12 hours","B":"At least 24 hours","C":"At least 36 hours","D":"At least 48 hours"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Hospital-acquired (nosocomial) pneumonia is defined by IDSA/ATS as acute lower respiratory tract infection manifesting $\\ge 48$ hours after hospital admission, which was neither present nor incubating at the time of initial triage."},{"id":"q_college_241","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"Which bacterial pathogen is most common cause of community-acquired pneumonia in adults?","options":{"A":"Staphylococcus aureus","B":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae","C":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","D":"Chlamydophila pneumoniae"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Streptococcus pneumoniae remains the most frequent bacterial cause of community-acquired pneumonia across all adult populations, typically presenting with acute-onset high fever, rust-colored sputum, pleuritic chest pain, and lobar alveolar consolidation"},{"id":"q_college_242","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"Drug of choice in Pneumocystis carinii pneumonia is:","options":{"A":"Pentamidine isethionate","B":"Zidovudine","C":"Cefoperazone","D":"Aztreonam"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"While oral/IV Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX) is the universal first-line treatment for Pneumocystis jirovecii (formerly P. carinii) pneumonia, intravenous Pentamidine isethionate is the classical parenteral second-line alternative of choice for severe hypoxic disease in patients who exhibit refractory clinical failure or severe hypersensitivity reactions to high-dose sulfa drugs."},{"id":"q_college_243","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"A 54-year-old female is admitted with severe pneumonia following a holiday in Turkey. Bloods reveal both hyponatraemia and deranged liver function tests. A chest X-ray shows patchy alveolar infiltrates with consolidation in the right lower lob","options":{"E":"Lumbar puncture","A":"Sputum culture","B":"Urinary antigen","C":"Blood cultures","D":"Bone marrow aspirate"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Travel exposure (air-conditioned hotels/pools), severe pneumonia, marked hyponatremia (SIADH-like state), and deranged transaminases point to Legionella pneumophila infection. The urinary antigen test is the rapid diagnostic modality of choice, detecting Legionella pneumophila serogroup 1 antigens within hours, with a sensitivity of 70ظô90% and specificity $>99\\%$"},{"id":"q_college_244","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"A 52-year-old man with a history of alcohol dependence is admitted with fever and feeling generally unwell. An admission chest X-ray shows consolidation in the right upper lobe with early cavitation. What is the most likely causative organism?","options":{"A":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","B":"Legionella pneumophilia","C":"Staphylococcus aureus","D":"Klebsiella pneumoniae","E":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Klebsiella pneumoniae (Friedl├جnder''s bacillus) characteristically causes severe necrotizing lobar pneumonia in patients with chronic alcohol dependence or poor baseline functional status due to microaspiration. It classically produces cavitation, prominent bulging of the interlobar fissure on chest radiography, and thick \"red-currant jelly\" sputum."},{"id":"q_college_245","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"A 37-year-old woman who is being treated as an inpatient for Mycoplasma pneumonia is reviewe Unfortunately, she is unable to tolerate clarithromycin due to severe nause What is the most suitable alternative antibiotic?","options":{"A":"Linezolid","B":"Cefaclor","C":"Ciprofloxacin","D":"Co-amoxiclav","E":"Doxycycline"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae lacks a peptidoglycan cell wall, rendering it intrinsically resistant to all beta- lactams (penicillins, cephalosporins, and co-amoxiclav). When macrolides (clarithromycin/azithromycin) are contraindicated or not tolerated, Doxycycline (a tetracycline that inhibits the bacterial 30S ribosomal subunit) is the established first-line alternative agent of choice."},{"id":"q_college_246","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"A 31-year-old female with no past medical history of note is admitted to hospital with dyspnoea and fever. She has recently returned from holiday in Turkey. A clinical diagnosis of pneumonia is mad. On examination, she is noted to have an ulcerated lesion on her upper lip consistent with reactivation of herpes simplex. Which organism is most associated with this examination finding?","options":{"A":"Legionella pneumophilia","B":"Staphylococcus aureus","C":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","D":"Pneumocystis carinii","E":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The reactivation of latent herpes simplex virus around the mouth (herpes labialis / \"cold sores\") is classically and historically associated with acute pneumococcal pneumonia (Streptococcus pneumoniae), triggered by high fever, intense endogenous cytokine release, and transient cellular immune suppression."},{"id":"q_college_247","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"A 64-year-old woman presents to the Emergency Department with a cough, fever, diarrhoea and myalgi. The cough is non-productive and has been gradually worsening since she returned from holiday in Spain one week ago. Over the past 24 hours, she has become more drowsy and febril. She is normally fit and well but drinks around 20 units of alcohol per week. On examination, pulse is 76/min, blood pressure 104/62 mmHg, oxygen saturations are 94% on room air and temperature is 38.4┬░ Bilateral coarse crackles are heard in the chest. Initial blood tests show: Hb 13.6 g/dl Platelets 311 ├ù 10ظ╣/l WBC 14.2 ├ù 10ظ╣/l Na+ 131 mmol/l K+ 4.3 mmol/l Urea 9.2 mmol/l Creatinine 91 ╬╝mol/l Bilirubin 12 ╬╝mol/l ALP 31 U/l ALT 64 U/l A chest X-ray shows patchy consolidation in the left lower zone with an associated pleural effusion. What is the most likely causative organism?","options":{"A":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","B":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae","C":"Legionella pneumophila","D":"Klebsiella pneumoniae","E":"Staphylococcus aureus"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The constellation of recent international travel, prominent gastrointestinal symptoms (diarrhea), central nervous system confusion/drowsiness, marked hyponatremia ($Na^+ 131\\text{ mmol/L}$), elevated transaminases, and relative bradycardia (pulse 76 bpm despite a temperature of 38.4┬░C ظ¤ Faget''s sign) is the classic clinical profile of Legionella pneumophila pneumonia"},{"id":"q_college_248","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"A 41-year-old female presents with a 3-day history of a dry cough and shortness of breath. This was preceded by flu-like symptoms. On examination, there is a symmetrical erythematous rash with target lesions over the whole body (erythema multiform","options":{"E":"Legionella pneumophilia","A":"Pseudomonas","B":"Staphylococcus aureus","C":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae","D":"Chlamydia pneumoniae"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae is the most common infectious etiology of erythema multiforme (targetoid erythematous cutaneous lesions with central clearing or necrosis) and Stevens-Johnson syndrome in young adults, mediated by immune complex deposition and cross-reactive autoantibodies triggered by the bacterium."},{"id":"q_college_249","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"A 62-year-old man presents to the emergency department with a productive cough of green sputum and occasional bloody specks. On examination, his temperature is 38.3┬░C, respiratory rate 23/min, heart rate 100/min, and there are left basal coarse crackles with a small cold sore above his lips. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Streptococcal pneumonia","B":"Viral pneumonia","C":"Mycoplasma pneumonia","D":"Klebsiella pneumonia","E":"Legionella pneumonia"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Lobar consolidation with focal coarse basal crackles, productive purulent sputum with specks of hemoptysis (rust-colored sputum), and reactivation of herpes labialis (cold sores) on the lip during acute fever is a classical presentation of Streptococcal pneumonia (S. pneumoniae)."},{"id":"q_college_250","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"A 52-year-old male is admitted to hospital with a temperature of 38.2┬░C and a 3-day history of a productive cough. He has been generally unwell for the past 10 days with flu-like symptoms. On examination, blood pressure is 96/60 mmHg and heart rate is 102/min. Chest X-ray shows bilateral lower zone consolidation. What is the most likely causative organism?","options":{"A":"Moraxella catarrhalis","B":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae","C":"Klebsiella","D":"Staphylococcus aureus","E":"Chlamydia pneumoniae"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Secondary bacterial pneumonia presenting days to weeks after an acute viral influenza-like illness is classically caused by Staphylococcus aureus. S. aureus exploits the virus-damaged respiratory epithelium, frequently producing severe, necrotizing bronchopneumonia, cavitation, and pneumatoceles."},{"id":"q_college_251","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"A 55-year-old man who was admitted following a stroke two weeks ago is reviewe. Yesterday, he started to develop a temperature and became more confuse A septic screen has shown radiological evidence of pneumoniOn examination, his respiratory rate is 36/min, pulse 112/min, oxygen saturations are 95% on room air and blood pressure is 102/66 mmHg. What is the most appropriate antibiotic to use?","options":{"A":"Teicoplanin","B":"Cefuroxime","C":"Amoxicillin","D":"Imipenem with cilastatin","E":"Piperacillin with tazobactam"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"This patient has hospital-acquired, post-stroke aspiration pneumonia developed after two weeks of inpatient admission. Empiric therapy must cover multidrug-resistant Gram-negative hospital pathogens (including Pseudomonas aeruginosa), Enterobacteriaceae, and oral anaerobes. Piperacillin-tazobactam provides anti-pseudomonal coverage paired with potent anaerobic activity."},{"id":"q_college_252","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"A 55-year-old man is referred to the medical admissions unit. He recently returned from a holiday in Italy and has failed to respond to a course of co-amoxiclav for a suspected lower respiratory tract infection. Chest X-ray shows bilateral infiltrates. Bloods are as follows: Na+ 122 mmol/l K+ 4.3 mmol/l Urea 8.4 mmol/l Creatinine 130 ╬╝mol/l What is the likely diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Goodpasture''s syndrome","B":"Legionella pneumonia","C":"Pneumocystis carinii pneumonia","D":"Pulmonary eosinophilia","E":"Mycoplasma pneumonia"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Atypical pneumonia refractory to standard beta-lactam monotherapy (co-amoxiclav) in a returning traveler presenting with profound hyponatremia ($Na^+ 122\\text{ mmol/L}$) is the hallmark of Legionella pneumonia. Legionella is an intracellular organism uninhibited by beta-lactam cell-wall inhibitors, requiring treatment with macrolides or respiratory fluoroquinolones."},{"id":"q_college_253","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"A 69-year-old man is brought into the emergency department by ambulance with a few days'' history of increasing shortness of breath, fever and a productive cough.On review, you find:HR 105 bpm Oxygen saturation 90% on air BP 100/65 mmHg Temp 38.9┬░C Respiratory rate 32 breaths/min Chest X-ray: right mid-zone cavity with surrounding consolidation The patient is started on oxygen, antibiotics and IV fluids, and his observations improve to the point where taking a history is easier. He reports that he normally keeps fit and has no other long-term health conditions, but that he has been more tired for the past few weeks. He explains further that he and his wife both caught a \"bad cold\" from their grandchildren about a month ago. What is the most likely cause for this gentleman''s symptoms?","options":{"A":"Lung cancer","B":"Klebsiella pneumoniae infection","C":"Staphylococcus aureus infection","D":"Pulmonary tuberculosis","E":"Streptococcus pneumoniae infection"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Post-viral secondary pneumonia characterized by acute cavitary consolidation in an adult following a recent viral illness is classic for Staphylococcus aureus. The secretion of destructive bacterial enzymes (e.g., Panton-Valentine leukocidin) leads to rapid tissue necrosis, abscess formation, and cavitation."},{"id":"q_college_254","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"Chronic dialysis is associated with an increased risk of healthcare-associated pneumonia caused by which of the following?","options":{"A":"Acinetobacter species","B":"Candida species","C":"Methicillin-resistant S. aureus (MRS","D":"Multidrug-resistant Enterobacteriaceae","E":"P. aeruginosa"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Chronic hemodialysis patients face high risks of colonization and invasive infection with Methicillin- resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) due to frequent vascular cannulation, central venous catheters, repetitive healthcare exposure, and impaired cutaneous and neutrophil antimicrobial barriers."},{"id":"q_college_255","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"Which of the following bacteria are a common cause of community-acquired pneumonia in hospitalized patients but not in patients treated as outpatients?","options":{"A":"Chlamydia pneumoniae","B":"H. influenzae","C":"Legionella species","D":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae","E":"S. pneumoniae"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"While M. pneumoniae and C. pneumoniae are managed as mild ambulatory outpatient infections, Legionella species typically present with severe, toxic multisystem disease characterized by high fevers, encephalopathy, gastrointestinal disturbances, and hypoxemia, making it a common cause of pneumonia requiring inpatient hospital or intensive care admission."},{"id":"q_college_256","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"Which of the following represents the recommended duration for treatment of uncomplicated community-acquired pneumonia and improving VAP?","options":{"A":"7 days; 7ظô14 days","B":"5 days; 8 days","C":"7 days; 10 days","D":"5 days; 10ظô14 days","E":"10ظô14 days for both"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Evidence-based guidelines (IDSA/ATS) recommend a minimum of 5 days of antibiotic therapy for uncomplicated community-acquired pneumonia, provided the patient achieves clinical stability. For improving ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP), a short-course regimen of 8 days yields clinical outcomes and recurrence rates equivalent to traditional 14-day courses while reducing antimicrobial selective pressure."},{"id":"q_college_257","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Pneumonia","text":"The primary preventive strategy for community-acquired pneumonia is:","options":{"A":"Antibiotic therapy","B":"Good hand hygiene","C":"Adherence to healthy lifestyle behaviors","D":"Immunization with pneumococcal and influenza vaccines"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Active immunization with pneumococcal conjugate/polysaccharide vaccines and annual influenza vaccines is the established preventive strategy against community-acquired pneumonia, reducing primary viral infections, post-viral bacterial complications, and invasive pneumococcal disease"},{"id":"q_college_258","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fuo","text":"Which of the following best defines Fever of Unknown Origin (FUO) in adults?","options":{"A":"Fever >38┬░C for more than 3 days","B":"Fever >38.3┬░C on several occasions for more than 3 weeks, undiagnosed after 1 week of inpatient evaluation","C":"Fever >39┬░C for more than 1 week, undiagnosed after 48 hours of investigation","D":"Fever >37.5┬░C for more than 2 weeks, undiagnosed after outpatient assessment"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The classic diagnostic criteria for classical Fever of Unknown Origin (established by Petersdorf and Beeson, later modified by Durack and Street) require: (1) core body temperature $>38.3^\\circ\\text{C}$ ($>101^\\circ\\text{F}$) recorded on multiple occasions, (2) duration of fever lasting $>3$ weeks, and (3) lack of a definitive diagnosis despite 1 week of intensive inpatient investigation or three outpatient visits. Infections (such as occult abscesses, TB, and brucellosis) represent the leading diagnostic category responsible for these presentations"},{"id":"q_college_259","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fuo","text":"The most common cause of FUO in adults is:","options":{"A":"Infection","B":"Neoplasm","C":"Autoimmune disease","D":"Drug fever"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Across global adult epidemiological cohorts, infections remain the single largest diagnostic category of FUO (accounting for 20ظô40% of cases), led by tuberculosis (especially extrapulmonary/miliary), intra- abdominal and pelvic abscesses, subacute bacterial endocarditis, and zoonotic infections like brucellosis."},{"id":"q_college_260","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fuo","text":"Which of the following infections is a well-known cause of FUO?","options":{"A":"Influenza","B":"Brucellosis","C":"Viral gastroenteritis","D":"Common cold"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Brucella species cause an undulating, protracted fever accompanied by non-specific constitutional symptoms (sweating, arthralgias, back pain, and hepatosplenomegaly) without localized findings. Because blood cultures grow slowly and standard screenings may miss it, brucellosis is a classical etiology of prolonged FUO in endemic areas."},{"id":"q_college_261","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fuo","text":"Which malignancy is most commonly associated with FUO?","options":{"A":"Lung carcinoma","B":"Lymphoma","C":"Colon carcinoma","D":"Breast carcinoma"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Among neoplastic disorders presenting as FUO, lymphomas (both Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphoma) are the most frequent causes. Malignant lymphoid cells synthesize and secrete large quantities of pyrogenic cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-$\\alpha$), resulting in sustained fevers or characteristic cyclical Pel-Ebstein fevers in the absence of overt lymphadenopathy."},{"id":"q_college_262","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fuo","text":"Among the following, which autoimmune disease is a frequent cause of FUO?","options":{"A":"Systemic lupus erythematosus (SL","B":"Rheumatoid arthritis","C":"Adult-onset Stillظآs disease","D":"Hashimotoظآs thyroiditis"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Adult-onset Stillظآs disease (AOSD) is a systemic autoinflammatory disorder and a classic rheumatologic cause of FUO. It presents with high spiking quotidian fevers, an evanescent salmon-pink maculopapular rash coinciding with fever spikes, polyarthralgia, sore throat, and extreme hyperferritinemia."},{"id":"q_college_263","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fuo","text":"Which of the following features suggests drug-induced fever as a cause of FUO?","options":{"A":"Fever subsides with continued drug use","B":"Associated eosinophilia and rash","C":"Presence of focal infection","D":"Bradycardia is never seen"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Drug fever represents a hypersensitivity reaction characterized by the classic triad of persistent pyrexia, peripheral blood eosinophilia, and a pruritic erythematous maculopapular drug eruption. The diagnosis is confirmed when the fever resolves within 48ظô72 hours following cessation of the offending agent."},{"id":"q_college_264","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fuo","text":"The first step in the evaluation of FUO is:","options":{"A":"PET-CT scan","B":"Bone marrow biopsy","C":"Detailed history and repeated physical examination","D":"Empirical antibiotic therapy"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The initial step in evaluating FUO is obtaining a comprehensive history (travel history, zoonotic contacts, occupational exposures, medications, and sexual history) combined with daily, meticulous physical examinations looking for subtle diagnostic clues (such as new heart murmurs, peripheral petechiae, splinter hemorrhages, or lymphadenopathy). Empiric antimicrobials should be withheld, as they confound cultures and delay diagnosis."},{"id":"q_college_265","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fuo","text":"In children, the most common cause of FUO is:","options":{"A":"Malignancy","B":"Infection","C":"Rheumatologic disease","D":"Drug fever"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"In pediatric populations, infections account for the vast majority (up to 50ظô60%) of all FUO cases. These represent common infections presenting with atypical or prolonged courses (such as EBV, CMV, Bartonellosis/cat-scratch disease, urinary tract infections, and occult osteomyelitis), rather than rare diseases."},{"id":"q_college_266","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fuo","text":"A 45-year-old man presents with FUO. ESR is high, ferritin markedly elevated, ANA negative, and a rash appears during fever spikes. The most likely diagnosis is:","options":{"A":"Rheumatic fever","B":"Adult-onset Stillظآs disease","C":"Tuberculosis","D":"Hodgkin lymphoma"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The combination of prolonged FUO, a transient salmon-pink evanescent macular rash appearing strictly at the height of fever paroxysms, markedly elevated acute-phase reactants (ESR), negative autoantibodies (ANA/RF), and marked hyperferritinemia (often $>3000ظô5000\\text{ ng/mL}$) is diagnostic of Adult-onset Stillظآs disease (AOSD) (Yamaguchi criteria)."},{"id":"q_college_267","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Fuo","text":"Which imaging modality has the highest diagnostic yield in FUO after basic tests fail to reveal the cause?","options":{"A":"Ultrasound abdomen","B":"PET-CT","C":"Chest X-ray","D":"MRI brain"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Whole-body {18} {F-FDG PET-CT}$ has the highest diagnostic yield for identifying the underlying cause of unresolved FUO. It combines functional metabolic mapping with anatomical localization, detecting focal glucose uptake by activated leukocytes and hypermetabolic malignant cells to identify hidden occult abscesses, large-vessel vasculitis (giant cell arteritis), osteomyelitis, or occult lymphomas"},{"id":"q_college_268","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Which of the following regions is known to have a high prevalence of Lassa fever?","options":{"A":"Europe","B":"North America","C":"Southeast Asia","D":"West Africa"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Lassa fever is an acute viral hemorrhagic illness endemic to West African nations (most notably Nigeria, Sierra Leone, Liberia, and Guinea). The geographical distribution strictly mirrors the ecological habitat of its primary rodent reservoir, the multimammate rat (Mastomys natalensis)."},{"id":"q_college_269","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Yellow fever is primarily transmitted by:","options":{"A":"Fleas","B":"Flies","C":"Mosquitoes","D":"Ticks"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Yellow fever virus (a Flavivirus) is transmitted to humans through the bites of infected daytime-biting mosquitoes. In urban epidemics, transmission occurs via Aedes aegypti, while in forested/jungle (sylvatic) cycles, canopy mosquitoes belonging to the genera Haemagogus and Sabethes maintain transmission among non-human primates."},{"id":"q_college_270","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Severe dengue, which can lead to hemorrhagic symptoms, is also known as:","options":{"A":"Dengue fever","B":"Dengue hemorrhagic fever","C":"Dengue malaria","D":"Dengue shock syndrome"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Dengue Hemorrhagic Fever (DHF) represents a severe, life-threatening manifestation of dengue virus infection. It is classically triggered by secondary infection with a heterologous dengue serotype via antibody- dependent enhancement (ADE), culminating in acute capillary leak, marked thrombocytopenia, bleeding diathesis, and progression to Dengue Shock Syndrome (DSS)."},{"id":"q_college_271","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"How is Marburg virus primarily transmitted to humans?","options":{"A":"Contact with infected rodents","B":"Airborne transmission","C":"Consumption of contaminated water","D":"Bite of infected ticks"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Primary spillover of Marburg filovirus into humans occurs through prolonged exposure to caves, mines, or roosts colonized by the Egyptian fruit bat (Rousettus aegyptiacus), through contact with bat excreta or saliva. Secondary transmission spreads efficiently among humans through direct contact with infected body fluids and mucous membranes."},{"id":"q_college_272","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Crimean-Congo Hemorrhagic Fever (CCHF) is mainly transmitted to humans through:","options":{"A":"Consumption of undercooked pork","B":"Mosquito bites","C":"Respiratory droplets","D":"Tick bites"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Crimean-Congo Hemorrhagic Fever (CCHF, a Nairovirus) is transmitted primarily through the bite of hard ticks belonging to the genus Hyalomma, or via direct occupational contact with blood, viremic tissues, and fluids of infected livestock during slaughtering."},{"id":"q_college_273","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"The first outbreak of Marburg virus disease was identified in Germany among workers who handled imported African green monkeys. The virus was named:","options":{"A":"Lassa","B":"Ebola","C":"Marburg","D":"Zika"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The initial documented outbreak of Marburg virus disease occurred in 1967 simultaneously in Marburg and Frankfurt (Germany) and Belgrade (Serbia) among laboratory staff processing kidney cell tissue from infected African green monkeys (Chlorocebus aethiops) imported from Uganda."},{"id":"q_college_274","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"A 38-year-old female farmer from a rural village in West Africa presents with high fever, fatigue, severe joint and muscle pain, persistent nausea and vomiting, disorientation and slurred speech. Examination reveals hypotension, widespread petechiae and ecchymosis, active bleeding from the gums, congested conjunctiva and hepatomegaly. Laboratory investigations show decreased platelet and WBC counts with elevated liver enzymes. The health care team suspects Lassa fever. What is the most likely mode of transmission?","options":{"A":"Airborne transmission","B":"Direct contact with infected animals","C":"Food-borne transmission","D":"Person-to-person transmission"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"While primary Lassa virus infection occurs via ingestion or inhalation of aerosolized rodent excreta (Mastomys natalensis), nosocomial and household secondary transmission occurs through direct human-to- human contact with infectious blood, vomitus, pharyngeal secretions, urine, or mucosal surfaces."},{"id":"q_college_275","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Which of the following clinical findings is NOT typically associated with viral hemorrhagic fevers?","options":{"A":"Clear sensorium and alertness","B":"High fever","C":"Joint and muscle pain","D":"Petechiae and ecchymosis"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Patients with viral hemorrhagic fevers typically suffer from early, progressive central nervous system involvementظ¤presenting with profound prostration, encephalopathy, drowsiness, confusion, delirium, or coma driven by systemic cytokine storms, cerebral hypoperfusion, and metabolic derangements. A completely clear and alert sensorium is inconsistent with severe disease."},{"id":"q_college_276","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Which laboratory finding is commonly seen in patients with viral hemorrhagic fever?","options":{"A":"Elevated hemoglobin levels","B":"Low liver enzymes","C":"Prolonged prothrombin time","D":"Normal platelet count"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Widespread viral endothelial damage, hepatocyte necrosis impairing clotting factor synthesis, and systemic consumptive coagulopathy (disseminated intravascular coagulation / DIC) characteristically result in a markedly prolonged prothrombin time (PT/INR), elevated aPTT, elevated D-dimers, and profound thrombocytopenia."},{"id":"q_college_277","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"The fundamental pathophysiological hallmark of most viral hemorrhagic fevers is:","options":{"A":"Widespread neuronal damage","B":"Severe immunosuppression due to cell lysis","C":"Increased vascular permeability and endothelial dysfunction","D":"Primary hepatic necrosis"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The central pathology of all viral hemorrhagic fevers is microvascular damage. Viral replication in monocyte- macrophages triggers an uncontrolled release of inflammatory cytokines (TNF-$\\alpha$, IL-1, IL-6) and nitric oxide, driving endothelial barrier activation, loss of vascular integrity, generalized plasma leakage, hypovolemia, and distributive/hypovolemic shock."},{"id":"q_college_278","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Which of the following is considered the primary natural reservoir of Ebola virus?","options":{"A":"Domestic pigs","B":"Non-human primates","C":"Fruit bats","D":"Aedes aegypti"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Fruit bats belonging to the family Pteropodidae (such as Hypsignathus monstrosus, Epomops franqueti, and Myonycteris torquata) are the natural ecological reservoir hosts for ebolaviruses. Non-human primates (chimpanzees and gorillas) are accidental hosts that suffer high mortality when infected."},{"id":"q_college_279","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"A patient presents with high fever, headache and retro-orbital pain suggestive of dengue fever. Which of the following is considered a warning sign for a poor prognosis?","options":{"A":"Mild petechial rash","B":"Temperature of 38┬░C","C":"Leukocytosis","D":"Persistent vomiting and abdominal pain"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"According to WHO classification criteria, warning signs heralding progression to severe dengue and shock include: persistent vomiting, severe abdominal pain or tenderness, clinical fluid accumulation (pleural effusion/ascites), mucosal bleeding, lethargy/restlessness, hepatomegaly $>2\\text{ cm}$, and an abrupt increase in hematocrit concurrent with a rapid decrease in platelet count."},{"id":"q_college_280","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Lassa fever, caused by an Arenavirus, is primarily transmitted through contact with excreta of which animal?","options":{"A":"Hyalomma ticks","B":"Mastomys natalensis","C":"Aedes mosquitoes","D":"Anopheles mosquitoes"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The multimammate rat (Mastomys natalensis) is the natural reservoir of Lassa mammarenavirus. These rodents shed the virus chronically in their urine and feces without developing clinical disease; human contamination occurs via direct contact with surfaces, broken skin, or ingestion of food contaminated with rodent excreta."},{"id":"q_college_281","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Marburg virus belongs to which virus family?","options":{"A":"Filoviridae","B":"Flaviviridae","C":"Bunyaviridae","D":"Arenaviridae"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Marburg virus, along with Ebola virus, belongs to the family Filoviridae. Filoviruses are characterized morphologically by long, filamentous, enveloped particles containing a non-segmented, negative-sense, single-stranded RNA genome."},{"id":"q_college_282","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Ribavirin has clinical efficacy in the treatment of which of the following?","options":{"A":"Dengue","B":"Ebola","C":"Lassa fever","D":"Yellow fever"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Intravenous Ribavirin (a guanosine nucleoside analog) has demonstrated efficacy in reducing mortality in Lassa fever, particularly when initiated early (within the first 6 days of illness onset). In contrast, Ribavirin shows no meaningful clinical efficacy against Filoviruses (Ebola/Marburg) or Flaviviruses (Dengue/Yellow Fever)."},{"id":"q_college_283","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"A common early hematological finding in many viral hemorrhagic fevers, such as dengue, is:","options":{"A":"Marked thrombocytosis","B":"Profound leukocytosis","C":"Thrombocytopenia and leukopenia","D":"Eosinophilia"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Early bone marrow suppression alongside immune-mediated peripheral clearance results in leukopenia (predominantly neutropenia) and progressive thrombocytopenia, which serve as classic early hematological markers in dengue, yellow fever, and filoviral infections."},{"id":"q_college_284","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Describe the characteristics of the Yellow Fever (YF) vaccine:","options":{"A":"Killed virus vaccine","B":"Live attenuated virus vaccine developed by Pasteurian passage","C":"Live attenuated vaccine produced by direct mutation of the virus genome","D":"Experimental vaccine only"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The yellow fever 17D strain vaccine is a live-attenuated viral vaccine developed through serial passage in tissue culture (embryo tissue) using classical viral attenuation principles (Pasteurian serial passage). It provides long-lasting, single-dose protective immunity."},{"id":"q_college_285","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"What is urban yellow fever (YF)?","options":{"A":"Part of a cycle of mosquitoes biting humans in cities","B":"Part of a cycle of mosquitoes biting humans and monkeys in forested areas","C":"A mutant of the YF virus","D":"A YF species that has adapted to new mosquitoes"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"The urban cycle involves direct human-to-vector-to-human transmission mediated domestic mosquitoes (Aedes aegypti) in densely populated cities without the involvement of non-human primates. In contrast, the sylvatic (jungle) cycle involves tree-hole mosquitoes (Haemagogus/Sabethes) and non-human forest primates."},{"id":"q_college_286","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic symptom of yellow fever?","options":{"A":"Headaches","B":"Blindness","C":"Shock","D":"Nausea"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Yellow fever characteristically manifests with acute fever, severe headache, lumbosacral pain, nausea/vomiting, and relative bradycardia (Faget''s sign), potentially progressing to a toxic phase with jaundice, hemorrhagic manifestations, acute tubular necrosis, and hypovolemic shock. Blindness is not a feature of yellow fever infection."},{"id":"q_college_287","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"In what approximate period was the first effective live-attenuated yellow fever vaccine developed?","options":{"A":"1990''s","B":"1950''s","C":"1940''s","D":"1980''s"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The live-attenuated 17D yellow fever vaccine was developed by Max Theiler and his colleagues at the Rockefeller Foundation in 1937 and extensively deployed for global immunization and military protection during the late 1930s and 1940s, for which Theiler was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1951."},{"id":"q_college_288","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"What is the primary mode of transmission for most viral hemorrhagic fevers?","options":{"A":"Airborne","B":"Fecal-oral","C":"Vector-borne (or direct contact with animal reservoirs/body fluids)","D":"Water-borne"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Viral hemorrhagic fevers (VHFs) are zoonotic illnesses primarily transmitted via arthropod vectors (mosquitoes, ticks) or through direct contact with infected animal reservoirs (rodent excreta, bats) and human body fluids."},{"id":"q_college_289","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Which virus is responsible for Ebola virus disease?","options":{"A":"Hantavirus","B":"Dengue virus","C":"Ebola virus","D":"Lassa virus"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Ebola virus disease (EVD) is caused by orthoebolaviruses (such as Zaire ebolavirus), which belong to the Filoviridae family, characterized by filamentous, enveloped, negative-sense single-stranded RNA."},{"id":"q_college_290","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Which organ is most commonly and prominently affected in patients with viral hemorrhagic fevers?","options":{"A":"Heart","B":"Liver","C":"Kidneys","D":"Lungs"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Hepatic involvement is a central pathophysiological feature in most VHFs (notably Yellow Fever, Ebola, Marburg, and CCHF), characterized by viral tropism for hepatocytes and Kupffer cells, resulting in marked AST/ALT elevation, coagulopathy, and jaundice."},{"id":"q_college_291","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"What is the typical incubation period for most viral hemorrhagic fevers?","options":{"A":"1ظô2 days","B":"1ظô2 weeks (2 to 21 days)","C":"4ظô6 weeks","D":"2ظô3 months"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"While incubation varies slightly among specific agents (e.g., Filoviruses 2ظô21 days, Arenaviruses 1ظô3 weeks, Flaviviruses 3ظô7 days), the vast majority of VHFs manifest clinically within 1 to 2 weeks post- exposure."},{"id":"q_college_292","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Which of the following is a cardinal hallmark symptom of viral hemorrhagic fevers?","options":{"A":"Hemorrhage and increased vascular permeability","B":"Productive purulent cough","C":"Migratory polyarthritis","D":"Chronic watery diarrhea"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Endothelial cell activation, systemic cytokine storm, and platelet dysfunction collectively drive increased microvascular permeability, petechiae, ecchymoses, mucosal bleeding, and shock."},{"id":"q_college_293","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Which virus is classically associated with sudden high fever, severe myalgia, vomiting, and progression to bleeding with jaundice?","options":{"A":"Yellow fever virus","B":"Marburg virus","C":"Crimean-Congo hemorrhagic fever virus","D":"Zika virus"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Yellow fever characteristically presents with an acute febrile phase (fever, severe myalgia, headache, vomiting) followed by a toxic phase marked by hepatic dysfunction, jaundice, coagulopathy, and hematemesis."},{"id":"q_college_294","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"What is the most effective preventive measure to reduce the community transmission of Lassa fever?","options":{"A":"Rodent control and safe storage of food to prevent contamination by rodent excreta","B":"Routine childhood live vaccination","C":"Mass empirical antibiotic prophylaxis","D":"Application of aquatic larvicides for mosquitoes"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Mastomys natalensis (multimammate rat) is the natural reservoir of Lassa virus; preventing rodent ingress into homes, storing food in rodent-proof containers, and maintaining hygiene are the primary prevention strategies (as no licensed vaccine is widely available)."},{"id":"q_college_295","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Which of the following animals is the primary reservoir for Hantaviruses causing Hantavirus Pulmonary Syndrome (HPS)?","options":{"A":"Bats","B":"Rodents (e.g., Deer mice)","C":"Mosquitoes","D":"Birds"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Hantaviruses in the Americas (e.g., Sin Nombre virus) are chronically carried by wild rodents, particularly the deer mouse (Peromyscus maniculatus), shedding virus in urine, saliva, and feces."},{"id":"q_college_296","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"In which geographic regions is Dengue Hemorrhagic Fever (DHF) most commonly endemic?","options":{"A":"Sub-Saharan Africa","B":"Southeast Asia, Western Pacific, and the Americas","C":"North America","D":"Northern Europe"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Dengue virus and DHF are hyperendemic in tropical and subtropical urban and peri-urban regions of Southeast Asia, the Western Pacific, Latin America, and the Caribbean where Aedes aegypti thrives."},{"id":"q_college_297","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Which viral hemorrhagic fever is classically associated with a biphasic fever pattern (a period of remission followed by a severe toxic phase)?","options":{"A":"Yellow fever","B":"Rift Valley fever","C":"Junin virus","D":"Crimean-Congo hemorrhagic fever"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Yellow fever classically features a \"period of calm/remission\" lasting 24ظô48 hours after the initial 3-day viremic fever, before 15ظô25% of patients progress to the secondary, life-threatening toxic phase with hepatic failure, jaundice, and renal shutdown."},{"id":"q_college_298","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Bleeding from mucous membranes (gums, epistaxis) and gastrointestinal tract accompanied by severe prostration is characteristic of:","options":{"A":"Marburg virus disease / Filoviral fevers","B":"Hantavirus Pulmonary Syndrome","C":"Chikungunya fever","D":"Acute viral hepatitis A"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Filovirus infections (Marburg and Ebola) cause severe diffuse mucosal hemorrhage (epistaxis, gingival bleeding, hematemesis, melena) accompanied by profound systemic prostration and multi-organ failure."},{"id":"q_college_299","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"What is the primary vector responsible for the transmission of Dengue virus?","options":{"A":"Aedes aegypti mosquito","B":"Anopheles mosquito","C":"Culex mosquito","D":"Ixodes tick"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Aedes aegypti (and secondarily Aedes albopictus) is the principal anthropophilic daytime-biting vector transmitting all four serotypes of Dengue virus."},{"id":"q_college_300","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Which viral hemorrhagic fever is associated with severe back pain, abdominal pain, oliguria, and acute kidney injury?","options":{"A":"Hemorrhagic Fever with Renal Syndrome (HFRS / Hantavirus)","B":"Ebola virus disease","C":"Dengue fever","D":"Omsk hemorrhagic fever"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Old World Hantaviruses (Hantaan, Dobrava, Puumala) cause Hemorrhagic Fever with Renal Syndrome (HFRS), characterized by fever, severe flank/back pain, retroperitoneal edema, proteinuria, and oliguric acute renal failure."},{"id":"q_college_301","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"What is the natural reservoir host for Marburg virus?","options":{"A":"Non-human primates","B":"Fruit bats (Rousettus aegyptiacus)","C":"Wild rodents","D":"Domestic pigs"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The Egyptian fruit bat (Rousettus aegyptiacus) is the confirmed natural reservoir host of Marburg filovirus; non-human primates and humans are accidental dead-end hosts."},{"id":"q_college_302","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hemorrhagic Fever","text":"Which viral hemorrhagic fever is associated with the highest case fatality rate, reaching up to 88ظô 90% in some outbreaks?","options":{"A":"Crimean-Congo hemorrhagic fever","B":"Marburg virus disease","C":"Lassa fever","D":"Dengue hemorrhagic fever"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Marburg virus disease and Zaire ebolavirus carry the highest case fatality ratios among infectious pathogens, historically reaching up to 88ظô90% in hospital and community outbreaks."},{"id":"q_college_303","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"The following are correct for food poisoning except for:","options":{"A":"A person gets sick after consuming a particular pathogenic microorganism.","B":"Food poisoning must be suspected when an acute illness with gastrointestinal or neurological manifestations affects two or more persons who have shared a meal during the previous 72 hours.","C":"Intoxication involves food poisoning in which the organism grows in food and releases a heat-stable toxin from the cells on pre-formed food, as in Salmonella spp.","D":"Salmonella causes a gastro-flu-like infection which in most cases lasts about 2 to 5 days."},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Non-typhoidal Salmonella causes foodborne illness via mucosal infection and tissue invasion within the distal ileum and colon (requiring 12ظô48 hours of incubation), not via pre-formed heat-stable food intoxication. Classical pre-formed food intoxications are produced by Staphylococcus aureus, Bacillus cereus, and Clostridium botulinum"},{"id":"q_college_304","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"As regards food poisoning, the following organisms can cause food poisoning except:","options":{"A":"Staphylococcus aureus","B":"Hepatitis B virus","C":"Clostridium botulinum","D":"Salmonella spp.","E":"Hepatitis A virus"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Hepatitis B virus is transmitted strictly through parenteral inoculation, percutaneous exposure to infected blood, or sexual contact. It is not transmitted enterically via food or water. Conversely, Hepatitis A virus is transmitted via the fecal-oral route through contaminated shellfish, water, and food."},{"id":"q_college_305","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"Types of human botulism are all of the following except:","options":{"A":"Infant botulism","B":"Unclassified botulism","C":"Air-borne botulism","D":"Wound botulism","E":"Food-borne botulism"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The classical clinical forms of naturally occurring botulism are foodborne botulism (ingestion of pre-formed neurotoxin), infant botulism (intestinal colonization by C. botulinum spores in infants $<1$ year, often from honey), wound botulism (spore germination in devitalized tissues), and adult intestinal toxemia. Inhalation/air-borne botulism does not occur naturally and is seen solely in laboratory accidents or bioterrorism scenarios."},{"id":"q_college_306","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"As regards staphylococcal food poisoning:","options":{"A":"It is caused by Gram-positive cocci.","B":"The incubation period is 72 hours.","C":"It produces a heat-labile toxin.","D":"It is presented by bloody diarrhe","E":"Food handlers carry the organism in their gut."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Staphylococcus aureus is a Gram-positive cluster-forming coccus. Staphylococcal food poisoning occurs following the ingestion of heat-stable enterotoxins (enterotoxins AظôE) pre-formed in food, characterized by a rapid incubation period (1 to 6 hours) presenting with explosive projectile vomiting and cramping, usually without high fever or bloody diarrhea."},{"id":"q_college_307","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"As regards Campylobacter food poisoning, the following are correct except:","options":{"A":"It is one of the most common causes of food-borne illness.","B":"It is present in the gut of a wide range of animals, especially birds.","C":"Outbreaks have been linked to the consumption of undercooked poultry, mince and sausages.","D":"Unpasteurized milk does not cause Campylobacter food poisoning.","E":"Pets may also be a source of infection."},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Consumption of raw, unpasteurized cow''s milk is an established major source of Campylobacter jejuni outbreaks due to fecal contamination during milking. Undercooked poultry represents the leading food vehicle globally."},{"id":"q_college_308","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"As regards the neurological signs and symptoms of botulism, all the following are correct except:","options":{"A":"Symmetrical","B":"Ascending","C":"Flaccid paralysis","D":"Involvement of motor and autonomic nerves","E":"Usually beginning with cranial nerves"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Botulinum neurotoxin blocks presynaptic acetylcholine release at neuromuscular junctions and cholinergic autonomic synapses, producing a classic descending, symmetrical flaccid paralysis. Symptoms typically initiate with cranial nerve palsies (diplopia, ptosis, dysphagia, dysarthria, fixed dilated pupils) before descending symmetrically to the upper extremities, respiratory diaphragm, and lower limbs. Ascending paralysis is characteristic of Guillain-Barr├ر syndrome."},{"id":"q_college_309","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"As regards Salmonella food poisoning, all of the following are correct except:","options":{"A":"The incubation period is from 1 to 6 hours.","B":"It is caused by Gram-negative bacilli.","C":"Treatment includes correction of dehydration and antibiotics.","D":"Salmonella is associated mainly with raw meats, poultry and dairy products.","E":"It causes symptoms and signs of gastroenteritis."},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Salmonella gastroenteritis is an invasive mucosal infection requiring bacterial replication in the gut; therefore, its incubation period is typically 12 to 48 hours (rarely 6ظô72 hours). Ultra-short incubation periods of 1 to 6 hours are characteristic of pre-formed enterotoxins (Staphylococcus aureus or emetic Bacillus cereus)."},{"id":"q_college_310","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"A 25-year-old woman is evaluated for chronic intermittent non-bloody diarrhea with abdominal cramps and bloating. She has a history of selective IgA deficiency and no history of antibiotics taken in the last 6 months. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Giardia lamblia","B":"EHEC","C":"Clostridium difficile","D":"Nontyphoidal Salmonella"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Secretory mucosal IgA is the primary host defense mechanism preventing Giardia lamblia trophozoites from adhering to the brush border of the duodenal and jejunal mucosa. Patients with selective IgA deficiency or common variable immunodeficiency are predisposed to chronic, relapsing giardiasis presenting with malabsorption, watery/foul diarrhea, and flatulence"},{"id":"q_college_311","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"A 29-year-old woman develops severe vomiting four hours after having lunch at a local restaurant. What is the most likely causative organism?","options":{"A":"Escherichia coli","B":"Shigella","C":"Campylobacter","D":"Salmonella","E":"Staphylococcus aureus"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"Onset of explosive nausea and vomiting within 1 to 6 hours following meal ingestion is diagnostic of intoxication by pre-formed heat-stable enterotoxin, most frequently produced by Staphylococcus aureus multiplying in unrefrigerated proteinaceous food or dairy."},{"id":"q_college_312","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"A 24-year-old patient presents to the Emergency Department with watery diarrhoe","options":{"A":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli","B":"Non-typhoidal Salmonella","C":"Campylobacter spp","D":"Vibrio cholerae","E":"Giardia lamblia"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli (ETEC) is the leading etiologic agent of traveler''s diarrhea worldwide (accounting for 30ظô50% of episodes). It produces heat-labile (LT) and heat-stable (ST) enterotoxins that stimulate enterocyte adenylate and guanylate cyclases, causing acute, non-invasive, secretory watery diarrhea."},{"id":"q_college_313","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"A 23-year-old man develops watery diarrhoea 5 days after arriving in Mexico. Which one of the following is the most likely responsible organism?","options":{"A":"Salmonella","B":"Shigella","C":"Campylobacter","D":"Escherichia coli","E":"Bacillus cereus"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Montezuma''s revenge\" or traveler''s diarrhea occurring within the first week of arrival in developing or Latin American countries is predominantly caused by Enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli (ETEC) contracted via fecally contaminated food or tap water."},{"id":"q_college_314","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"A 55-year-old businessman presents with a 15-day history of watery, non-bloody diarrhoea associated with anorexia and abdominal bloating. His symptoms started 4 days after returning from a trip to Pakistan. On examination, he is apyrexial with dry mucous membranes but normal skin turgor. Given the likely organism, what is the most appropriate treatment?","options":{"A":"Hydroxychloroquine","B":"Aciclovir","C":"Benzylpenicillin","D":"Ciprofloxacin","E":"Metronidazole"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"Persistent watery, frothy diarrhea lasting $>10ظô14$ days accompanied by significant abdominal distension, sulfurous belching, and flatulence without systemic fever in a traveler is indicative of Giardiasis (Giardia duodenalis). The treatment of choice is oral Metronidazole (or Tinidazole), which targets protozoal anaerobic metabolic pathways."},{"id":"q_college_315","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"A 29-year-old man presents with a nine-day history of watery diarrhoea that developed one week after returning from Indi","options":{"A":"Amoebiasis","B":"Giardiasis","C":"Campylobacter","D":"Shigella","E":"Salmonella"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Subacute, prolonged, non-inflammatory diarrhea following travel to the Indian subcontinent that persists beyond one week without fever, tenesmus, or gross hematochezia is characteristic of Giardiasis. Bacterial causes (Campylobacter, Shigella) typically present acutely with fever and inflammatory dysentery."},{"id":"q_college_316","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"A 30-year-old man comes for review. He returned from a holiday in Egypt yesterday. For the past two days, he has been passing frequent bloody diarrhoea associated with crampy abdominal pain. Abdominal examination demonstrates diffuse lower abdominal tenderness but there is no guarding or rigidity. His temperature is 37.5┬║","options":{"C":"Staphylococcus aureus","A":"Giardiasis","B":"Enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli","D":"Salmonella","E":"Shigella"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"Shigella species (S. dysenteriae, S. flexneri, S. sonnei) possess a low infectious inoculum (as few as 10ظô 100 organisms) and invade the colonic epithelium, causing severe neutrophilic mucosal inflammation, micro- ulcerations, and dysentery characterized by small-volume bloody, mucoid stools accompanied by painful abdominal cramps and tenesmus."},{"id":"q_college_317","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"A 19-year-old female returns from Ghan She presents with pyrexia (40┬░and bloody stools. On examination, she has abdominal distension, hepatosplenomegaly and rose spots on her abdomen. Before empirical treatment is started, she develops bowel perforation resulting in overwhelming sepsis. Which organism is responsible for this type of pathology?","options":{"A":"Giardia lamblia","B":"Salmonella typhi","C":"Treponema pallidum","D":"Staphylococcus aureus","E":"Streptococcus pneumoniae"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The classic findings of persistent sustained high-grade pyrexia, hepatosplenomegaly, and transient blanching rose spots on the trunk identify Salmonella typhi (enteric fever). During the third week of untreated disease, marked hyperplasia and ischemic necrosis of Peyer''s patches in the terminal ileum frequently lead to life-threatening intestinal perforation and peritonitis."},{"id":"q_college_318","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"Which one of the following is the most likely presentation of Staphylococcus aureus food poisoning?","options":{"A":"Tenesmus","B":"Watery diarrhoea","C":"Dysentery","D":"Severe vomiting","E":"Presentation 24ظô48 hours after eating affected food"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Staphylococcus aureus enterotoxins stimulate local neuroreceptors in the upper gastrointestinal tract, transmitting signals via the vagus and sympathetic nerves to the medullary emetic center. This triggers intense, sudden-onset severe vomiting and retching within hours of ingestion, with diarrhea playing a less prominent or secondary role."},{"id":"q_college_319","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"A 44-year-old homosexual man presents to your GUM clinic with a 3-day history of diarrhoeHe has no history of recent foreign travel and is normally fit and well. His abdomen is soft and non- tender and he has normal observations. His last sexual encounter was 2 weeks ago. Tests for Chlamydia trachomatis, Neisseria gonorrhoeae, HIV and syphilis are negativ A stool sample is sent for culture and grows Shigell What is the most suitable management plan?","options":{"A":"Start loperamide and review in 48 hours","B":"Admit to the local infectious diseases unit for IV metronidazole","C":"Reassure the patient that this is a commensal bacterium unlikely to be the cause of symptoms","D":"Advise the patient to increase fluid intake and seek medical attention if they become unwell or develop bloody diarrhoea","E":"Inform Public Health England"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"Shigella is an extremely contagious pathogen transmitted via the fecal-oral route and sexual practices involving oral-anal contact. Because it carries a very low infectious dose and has significant outbreak potential (including multidrug-resistant strains circulating among men who have sex with men), isolation and statutory notification to public health authorities (such as UKHSA/Public Health England) is legally mandated for contact tracing and epidemiological control. Antiperistaltic agents like loperamide are contraindicated as they worsen toxicity."},{"id":"q_college_320","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"A 27-year-old student presents to the GP with a 24-hour history of explosive diarrhoea and vomiting. He has not noticed any blood in his stool and has no history of foreign travel. He tells you that he has been eating rice kept warm in a rice cooker for several days. What is the likely pathogenic organism?","options":{"A":"Bacillus cereus","B":"Campylobacter jejuni","C":"Shigella flexneri","D":"Norwalk virus","E":"Staphylococcus aureus"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Reheated fried rice or rice held warm in cookers provides an optimal environment for heat-resistant spores of Bacillus cereus to germinate and produce the pre-formed, heat-stable cyclic peptide enterotoxin cereulide, triggering acute vomiting and diarrhea within hours of ingestion."},{"id":"q_college_321","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"A man presents with severe vomiting. He reports not being able to keep fluids down for the past 12 hours. You suspect gastroenteritis and, when discussing possible causes, he mentions reheating curry with rice the night befor","options":{"E":"Bacillus cereus","A":"Escherichia coli","B":"Campylobacter","C":"Salmonella","D":"Shigella"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"Bacillus cereus produces an emetic syndrome closely mimicking staphylococcal food poisoning, characteristically associated with leftover or reheated rice dishes. Spores survive boiling and germinate during slow cooling or prolonged room-temperature storage"},{"id":"q_college_322","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"What is the first-line antibiotic in the treatment of Shigella dysentery?","options":{"A":"Flucloxacillin","B":"Vancomycin","C":"Ciprofloxacin","D":"Metronidazole","E":"Ampicillin"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Fluoroquinolones, specifically oral Ciprofloxacin, represent the standard first-line antimicrobial therapy for acute shigellosis in adults. Antibiotic therapy reduces the duration of fever, limits diarrhea, and shortens fecal shedding of the organism. (Azithromycin or Ceftriaxone are preferred alternatives in pediatric cohorts or areas with documented fluoroquinolone resistance)."},{"id":"q_college_323","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Food Poisoning","text":"Which of the following findings is unlikely to be found in Clostridium difficile infection?","options":{"A":"Bloody diarrhea","B":"Fever","C":"Ileus","D":"Leukocytosis","E":"Recurrence after therapy"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Clostridioides difficile infection (CDI) classically manifests with profuse, foul-smelling, non-bloody watery diarrhea, fever, marked leukocytosis (frequently $>20,000ظô50,000/\\mu\\text{L}$ in severe disease), and risk of toxic megacolon/ileus. Frank hematochezia or macroscopic bloody diarrhea is distinctly unusual and should prompt consideration of alternative etiologies such as ischemic colitis, EHEC, or Shigella."},{"id":"q_college_324","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Rabies & Viral Zoonoses","text":"The rabies vaccine:","options":{"A":"Is a live vaccine","B":"Is cell-cultured, freeze-dried and reconstituted prior to administration","C":"Is a polysaccharide vaccine derived from culture on human diploid cells","D":"Can not be given to breast feeding mother"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Modern human rabies vaccines (e.g., HDCV, PCECV) are whole-inactivated, cell-culture-derived, lyophilized (freeze-dried) preparations that are reconstituted immediately before intramuscular administration. Due to the fatal nature of rabies, lactation is not a contraindication."},{"id":"q_college_325","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Rabies & Viral Zoonoses","text":"The rabies virus:","options":{"A":"Is a flavivirus","B":"Is transmitted by aerosolized secretions","C":"Is a reverse-zoonosis","D":"Causes paralysis in dogs"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Rabies virus (Rhabdoviridae, genus Lyssavirus) causes furious (encephalitic) or dumb (paralytic) rabies in both animals (dogs) and humans, leading to progressive ascending flaccid paralysis."},{"id":"q_college_326","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Rabies & Viral Zoonoses","text":"Which unique form does the rabies virus take?","options":{"A":"The virion has a dumbbell appearance","B":"It is shaped like a bullet from a gun","C":"The virus is star-shaped","D":"The virion is very pleomorphic","E":"The virus is icosahedral"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Rhabdoviruses, including the rabies virus, possess a distinct, enveloped cylindrical morphology with a rounded end, classically described as bullet-shaped."},{"id":"q_college_327","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Rabies & Viral Zoonoses","text":"What does the word \"zoonotic\" mean?","options":{"A":"From animals","B":"From humans","C":"From a zoo","D":"Coming soon"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Zoonotic refers to an infectious disease that naturally jumps or transmits from vertebrate animals to humans."},{"id":"q_college_328","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Rabies & Viral Zoonoses","text":"Which common domestic animals carry zoonotic viruses? (Choose all that apply.)","options":{"A":"Pigs","B":"Cats","C":"Chickens","D":"Dogs"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Domestic animals serve as major reservoirs/vectors for viral zoonoses: pigs (swine influenza, Nipah virus), cats and dogs (rabies), and chickens/poultry (avian influenza)."},{"id":"q_college_329","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Rabies & Viral Zoonoses","text":"SARS is described as a zoonotic virus - what does this mean?","options":{"A":"Such viruses are confined to animals","B":"They do not cause disease in humans","C":"They emerge from animals to cross the species barrier infrequently","D":"They cause pandemics","E":"They transmitted by birds"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"SARS-CoV is maintained in natural animal reservoirs (bats/civet cats) and occasionally crosses the species barrier (zoonotic spillover) to infect human hosts."},{"id":"q_college_330","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Rabies & Viral Zoonoses","text":"What is the causal agent of Chikungunya?","options":{"A":"Bacteria","B":"Nematode","C":"Virus","D":"Fungus","E":"Prion"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Chikungunya fever is caused by an arbovirus belonging to the Alphavirus genus (Togaviridae family), transmitted by Aedes mosquitoes."},{"id":"q_college_331","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Rabies & Viral Zoonoses","text":"What category of bioterrorism agents does the CDC characterize as easily transmitted from person to person (or animal to person), having a high mortality rate, and including Anthrax, plague, and Ebola?","options":{"A":"Category A","B":"Category B","C":"Category C","D":"Category D"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Category A bioterrorism agents (Bacillus anthracis, Yersinia pestis, Ebola/Marburg, Smallpox, C. botulinum toxin) pose the highest priority and risk to public health due to easy dissemination, high case-fatality rates, and potential for public panic."},{"id":"q_college_332","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Rabies & Viral Zoonoses","text":"_________ is an RNA virus commonly carried in deer mice/wild rodents and causes flu-like symptoms in humans.","options":{"A":"Yersinia pestis","B":"Psittacosis","C":"Hantavirus","D":"Rat bite fever"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Hantaviruses (Bunyaviridae) are negative-sense RNA viruses shed in rodent droppings, saliva, and urine (e.g., deer mouse), causing Hantavirus Pulmonary Syndrome (HPS) or Hemorrhagic Fever with Renal Syndrome (HFRS) upon inhalation."},{"id":"q_college_333","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Rabies & Viral Zoonoses","text":"What bacteria causes headache, fever, and myalgia and is associated with Rat Bite Fever?","options":{"A":"Yersinia pestis","B":"Chlamydophila psittaci","C":"Streptobacillus moniliformis","D":"Bunyaviridae"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Streptobacillus moniliformis (in North America) and Spirillum minus (in Asia) are normal oral commensals in rodents that cause Rat-Bite Fever in humans following bites or scratches."},{"id":"q_college_334","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Rabies & Viral Zoonoses","text":"___________ is one of the most virulent food-borne diseases and is one of the reasons why we pasteurize milk.","options":{"A":"Psittacosis","B":"Listeria monocytogenes","C":"Streptobacillus moniliformis","D":"Cryptosporidium parvum"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Listeria monocytogenes can grow at refrigeration temperatures and carries high mortality in neonates, pregnant women, andthe elderly; commercial pasteurization of dairy milk eliminates this pathogen."},{"id":"q_college_335","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Rickettsial Diseases","text":"Which of the following are the primary reservoirs of flea-borne murine typhus?","options":{"A":"Rats","B":"Dogs","C":"Ticks","D":"Mites"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Endemic (murine) typhus is caused by Rickettsia typhi, maintained primarily in rodent reservoirs (Rattus rattus, Rattus norvegicus) and transmitted by the oriental rat flea (Xenopsylla cheopis)."},{"id":"q_college_336","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Rickettsial Diseases","text":"Which of the following is NOT the proper preventive or management method for Rickettsial diseases?","options":{"A":"Use of insecticides to control vectors and reservoirs","B":"Use of insect repellent while traveling to endemic areas","C":"Proper hygiene to eliminate lice and fleas","D":"Use of sulfonamides for the treatment of typhus fever"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Sulfonamides enhance rickettsial proliferation and worsen clinical severity, making them strictly contraindicated. Doxycycline is the drug of choice."},{"id":"q_college_337","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Rickettsial Diseases","text":"A PCR test is a commonly used molecular diagnostic method for rickettsial diseases; the test is less sensitive during which stage of the disease? (Select all that apply)","options":{"A":"Late stage of the infection","B":"Within 2ظô3 days of the infection","C":"Between 1ظô2 weeks of the infection","D":"1 week after antibiotic therapy"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Rickettsial DNA levels in blood drop rapidly after initiating effective antibiotic therapy (doxycycline) and during late convalescence once bacteremia clears."},{"id":"q_college_338","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Rickettsial Diseases","text":"Name the specimen most commonly used for the microscopic and direct molecular diagnosis of rickettsial diseases:","options":{"A":"Urine","B":"Nose swab","C":"Blood (or skin biopsy of eschar/rash)","D":"Throat secretions"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Rickettsiae are obligate intracellular bacteria infecting vascular endothelial cells; whole blood (buffy coat) or punch biopsy of an inoculation eschar or petechial lesion is the preferred diagnostic specimen."},{"id":"q_college_339","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Rickettsial Diseases","text":"Identify the definition for the term zoonotic disease:","options":{"A":"A disease that can be transmitted from humans to animals","B":"A disease that can be transmitted to zoo animals only","C":"A disease that is transmitted from zoo animals","D":"A disease that can be transmitted from animals to humans"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"A zoonosis is defined by the WHO as any infection naturally transmissible from vertebrate animals to humans."},{"id":"q_college_340","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Rickettsial Diseases","text":"Which of the following is azoonotic disease?","options":{"A":"Anthrax","B":"Typhoid","C":"Bacillary dysentery","D":"Cholera"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Anthrax (Bacillus anthracis) is a bacterial zoonosis acquired from infected herbivores or their products (hides/wool). Typhoid, shigellosis, and cholera are strictly human/enteric diseases."},{"id":"q_college_341","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Plague (Yersinia pestis)","text":"Plague is caused by which bacteria?","options":{"A":"Clostridium","B":"Mycobacteria","C":"Yersinia pestis","D":"None of the above"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Plague is a severe zoonotic infection caused by the Gram-negative coccobacillus Yersinia pestis."},{"id":"q_college_342","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Plague (Yersinia pestis)","text":"Which of the following is/are clinical forms of the plague?","options":{"A":"Bubonic Plague","B":"Septicemic Plague","C":"Pneumonic Plague","D":"All the above"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Yersinia pestis produces three primary clinical presentations: bubonic (lymphatic), septicemic (bloodstream), and pneumonic (pulmonary)."},{"id":"q_college_343","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Plague (Yersinia pestis)","text":"How do humans acquire infection from the plague?","options":{"A":"When bitten by an infected rodent flea","B":"Direct contact with infected animal tissues or body fluids","C":"Inhaling respiratory droplets from humans or cats with pneumonic plague","D":"All the above"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Plague transmission occurs via flea bites (Xenopsylla cheopis), handling infected animal carcasses, or inhaling infectious respiratory droplets."},{"id":"q_college_344","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Plague (Yersinia pestis)","text":"Which type of plague is the most serious form and is directly transmissible from person to person?","options":{"A":"Bubonic plague","B":"Septicemic plague","C":"Pneumonic plague","D":"None of the above"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Pneumonic plague causes severe respiratory failure with high lethality and is the only form capable of airborne person-to-person spread."},{"id":"q_college_345","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Plague (Yersinia pestis)","text":"What is the typical incubation period of bubonic plague?","options":{"A":"2 to 3 days","B":"3 to 4 days","C":"2 to 4 days","D":"3 to 7 days"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Bubonic plague symptoms generally develop 3 to 7 (range 2ظô8) days after a flea bite. Primary pneumonic plague has a shorter incubation period of 1 to 3 days."},{"id":"q_college_346","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Plague (Yersinia pestis)","text":"Which of the following are classic symptoms of bubonic plague?","options":{"A":"Fever","B":"Muscle pain","C":"Swollen, painful lymph nodes (buboes)","D":"All the above"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Bubonic plague presents with acute fever, chills, myalgia, and painful, suppurative regional lymphadenitis (buboes), most commonly in the groin or axilla."},{"id":"q_college_347","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Plague (Yersinia pestis)","text":"Which of the following are characteristic symptoms of pneumonic plague?","options":{"A":"Severe cough with bloody sputum","B":"Difficulty breathing","C":"Chest pain","D":"All the above"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Pneumonic plague presents with acute fever, dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain, and productive cough with hemoptysis and frothy pink sputum."},{"id":"q_college_348","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Plague (Yersinia pestis)","text":"What management is indicated for a confirmed plague patient?","options":{"A":"Parenteral antibiotics","B":"Immediate hospitalization","C":"Medical isolation","D":"All the above"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Plague requires early antibiotic therapy (streptomycin, gentamicin, or doxycycline), emergency hospitalization, and strict droplet isolation for suspected pneumonic cases."},{"id":"q_college_349","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Plague (Yersinia pestis)","text":"Which of the following preventive measures should be taken against plague?","options":{"A":"Reduce rodent habitat around homes and workplaces","B":"Apply flea-control products to domestic pets","C":"Use insect repellent in flea-endemic outdoor areas","D":"All the above"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Plague prevention involves rodent control, flea suppression with insecticides, avoiding sick/dead animals, and chemoprophylaxis for close contacts of pneumonic cases."},{"id":"q_college_350","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Plague (Yersinia pestis)","text":"Plague is classified as a:","options":{"A":"Bacterial disease","B":"Viral infection","C":"Genetic disorder","D":"Prion disease"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Plague is an acute bacterial infection caused by Yersinia pestis, a member of the Yersiniaceae family."},{"id":"q_college_351","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Plague (Yersinia pestis)","text":"Was COVID-19 an example of plague?","options":{"A":"Yes","B":"No"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"COVID-19 is a viral illness caused by the coronavirus SARS-CoV-2, whereas \"plague\" refers specifically to bacterial infections caused by Yersinia pestis."},{"id":"q_college_352","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Plague (Yersinia pestis)","text":"Is a licensed vaccine for plague widely available for routine commercial use?","options":{"A":"True","B":"False"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"There is currently no widely available commercial plague vaccine licensed for routine public immunization; newer recombinant candidate vaccines remain under investigational development."},{"id":"q_college_353","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hepatitis","text":"A previously unvaccinated healthcare worker sustains a needlestick injury from a patient with active, HBsAg-positive Hepatitis B. The appropriate post-exposure management is:","options":{"A":"Hepatitis B Immune Globulin (HBIG) alone","B":"Hepatitis B vaccine alone","C":"Hepatitis B vaccine series PLUS Hepatitis B Immune Globulin (HBIG)","D":"Hepatitis B vaccine plus Lamivudine","E":"Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate for 28 days"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) for non-immune individuals exposed to HBsAg-positive blood requires immediate combined passive-active immunization: a single dose of HBIG (0.06 mL/kg) administered within 24 hours at a separate anatomical site from the first dose of the 3-dose recombinant HBV vaccine series."},{"id":"q_college_354","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hepatitis","text":"All of the following statements regarding Hepatitis C virus (HCV) infection are correct EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"In patients with chronic HCV followed for 20 years, progression to cirrhosis occurs in approximately 20ظô 25%","B":"Disease progression is accelerated by older age at infection, male sex, and alcohol consumption","C":"Chronic HCV is associated with extrahepatic manifestations including Sj├╢gren''s syndrome, lichen planus, and porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT)","D":"HCV is more efficiently and frequently transmitted by the sexual route than Hepatitis B virus","E":"An episodic, fluctuating pattern of serum aminotransferase (ALT) elevation is characteristic"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Hepatitis B virus (HBV) is transmitted sexually with high efficiency (concentrated in semen/vaginal secretions), whereas Hepatitis C virus (HCV) is transmitted predominantly parenterally through percutaneous blood exposure; sexual transmission of HCV is relatively inefficient among heterosexual monogamous couples."},{"id":"q_college_355","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hepatitis","text":"A 42-year-old dentist is reviewed in the medical clinic complaining of persistent lethargy. Routine bloods show abnormal liver function tests so a hepatitis screen is sent. The results are shown below: Anti-HAV IgG: Negative HBsAg: Negative Anti-HBs: Positive Anti-HBc: Negative Anti-HCV: Positive What do these results most likely demonstrate?","options":{"A":"Hepatitis B infection","B":"Hepatitis C infection","C":"Previous vaccination to hepatitis B and C","D":"Hepatitis C infection with previous hepatitis B vaccination"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"C. He is Anti-HBs positive but Anti-HBc negative, which is the classic serological profile of a person who has immunity due to Hepatitis B vaccination (recombinant HBsAg vaccine) but has never been naturally exposed to the core of the virus."},{"id":"q_college_356","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hepatitis","text":"Which one of the following statements regarding the hepatitis B vaccine is correct?","options":{"A":"Ribavirin is the treatment of choice for chronic hepatitis B","B":"All patients immunised against hepatitis B require an anti-HBs check to assess their response to the vaccine","C":"10-15% of adults fail to respond or respond poorly to 3 doses of the vaccine","D":"The vaccine is of the live-attenuated type","E":"An anti-HBs level of 20 mIU/ml indicates an adequate response to the vaccine"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"to the standard 3 doses of the Hepatitis B vaccine. Risk factors for non-response include age over 40, obesity, smoking, and immunosuppression."},{"id":"q_college_357","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hepatitis","text":"A 26-year-old pregnant woman in her third trimester presents with acute onset of jaundice, fever, and altered mental status. Laboratory tests reveal elevated liver enzymes and positive IgM anti-HEV. Which of the following is the most significant clinical risk associated with this infection?","options":{"A":"Higher risk of chronic progression to cirrhosis","B":"Increased risk of hepatocellular carcinoma within 1 year","C":"High mortality rate due to fulminant hepatic failure","D":"High risk of autoimmune hepatitis development"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"pregnant women, particularly during the third trimester, HEV carries an extremely high mortality rate (up to 20%) due to the rapid development of fulminant hepatic failure and obstetric complications like disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)."},{"id":"q_college_358","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hepatitis","text":"A patient presents with symptoms of acute hepatitis. Blood tests are performed during the serological \"window period\" of Hepatitis B virus infection. Which of the following markers is the ONLY detectable serological indicator of infection during this period?","options":{"A":"HBsAg","B":"Anti-HBs","C":"Anti-HBc IgM","D":"HBeAg"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"appearance of protective Anti-HBs antibodies. During this phase, both HBsAg and Anti-HBs are undetectable. The only marker that confirms acute/recent HBV infection during this time is the IgM antibody to the Hepatitis B core antigen (Anti-HBc IgM)."},{"id":"q_college_359","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hepatitis","text":"A patient known to have chronic inactive Hepatitis B is exposed to Hepatitis D virus (HDV). What is this clinical scenario called, and what is its associated risk compared to HBV-HDV co-infection?","options":{"A":"Co-infection; associated with a low risk of chronicity","B":"Co-infection; associated with a high risk of acute liver failure only","C":"Superinfection; associated with a very high risk of rapid progression to chronic liver disease and cirrhosis ظ£ô","D":"Superinfection; typically self-limiting with zero risk of cirrhosis"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Superinfection. Compared to Co-infection (acquiring both viruses simultaneously, which usually resolves), HDV superinfection leads to chronic HDV infection in >80% of cases, significantly accelerating liver damage, cirrhosis, and hepatic failure."},{"id":"q_college_360","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Viral Hepatitis","text":"A patient screens positive for Anti-HCV IgG during a routine medical workup. What is the most appropriate next step to determine if this patient has an active, ongoing Hepatitis C infection?","options":{"A":"Repeat the Anti-HCV IgG test in 6 months","B":"Start empiric antiviral therapy immediately","C":"Order a qualitative or quantitative HCV RNA PCR test","D":"Perform a liver biopsy"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"resolved past infection and an active ongoing infection. To confirm active viral replication, a molecular testظ¤ HCV RNA PCRظ¤must be performed."},{"id":"q_college_361","source":"College MCQ","topic":"COVID-19 & Respiratory Viral Infections","text":"An antiviral drug administered via the dry-powder inhalation route for the treatment and prophylaxis of influenza is:","options":{"A":"Amantadine","B":"Zanamivir","C":"Rimantadine","D":"Oseltamivir"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Zanamivir is a viral neuraminidase inhibitor formulated as a dry powder for oral inhalation via the Diskhaler device. Oseltamivir is administered orally, and peramivir is administered intravenously."},{"id":"q_college_362","source":"College MCQ","topic":"COVID-19 & Respiratory Viral Infections","text":"Gastrointestinal (GI) symptoms frequently documented in patients with acute COVID-19 include all of the following EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Anorexia and loss of appetite","B":"Watery diarrhea","C":"Nausea and vomiting","D":"Massive upper gastrointestinal bleeding (hematemesis)"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"SARS-CoV-2 enters intestinal enterocytes via surface ACE2 receptors, producing diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and anorexia in up to 20ظô30% of patients. Hematemesis is not a standard direct symptom of acute infection."},{"id":"q_college_363","source":"College MCQ","topic":"COVID-19 & Respiratory Viral Infections","text":"All of the following clinical features are recognized manifestations of COVID-19 EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Loss of taste (ageusia) and/or smell (anosmia)","B":"Red, swollen eyes (conjunctivitis)","C":"Cutaneous rashes and \"COVID toes\" (pseudo-chilblains)","D":"Severe constipation"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"COVID-19 frequently presents with anosmia/dysgeusia, conjunctival injection, inflammatory cutaneous rashes, and loose watery stools; constipation is not an established clinical manifestation."},{"id":"q_college_364","source":"College MCQ","topic":"COVID-19 & Respiratory Viral Infections","text":"Which of the following represents a rare but serious extrapulmonary complication directly resulting from acute Influenza infection?","options":{"A":"Diffuse eczematous rash","B":"Acute necrotizing myositis with rhabdomyolysis","C":"Destructive oligoarthritis","D":"Purulent bacterial conjunctivitis","E":"Secondary bacterial pneumonia caused by Staphylococcus aureus"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Beyond pulmonary complications, Influenza viruses (especially type B and pandemic H1N1) can cause acute viral myositis with severe muscle tenderness, myoglobinuria, and rhabdomyolysis leading to acute renal failure. (Note: Secondary bacterial pneumonia is a pulmonary complication)."},{"id":"q_college_365","source":"College MCQ","topic":"COVID-19 & Respiratory Viral Infections","text":"A patient is being admitted to a medical unit with a diagnosis of COVID-19. Which type of room should this patient be assigned by the nurse?","options":{"A":"Private room","B":"Semiprivate room","C":"Room with windows that can be opened","D":"Room with negative pressure","E":"Room with positive pressure"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Standard management of COVID-19 requires Contact and Droplet precautions in a designated single private room. Airborne negative-pressure isolation (AIIR) is specifically reserved for aerosol-generating procedures (AGPs) such as intubation, bronchoscopy, or non-invasive ventilation."},{"id":"q_college_366","source":"College MCQ","topic":"COVID-19 & Respiratory Viral Infections","text":"A 12-year-old girl is prescribed Oseltamivir for suspected influenza. What is the mechanism of action of Oseltamivir?","options":{"A":"Inhibits RNA polymerase","B":"Interferes with the capping of viral mRNA","C":"Neuraminidase inhibitor","D":"Inhibits DNA polymerase"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"on the surface of influenza viruses, preventing the release of newly formed viral particles from infected host cells and halting further spread within the respiratory tract."},{"id":"q_college_367","source":"College MCQ","topic":"COVID-19 & Respiratory Viral Infections","text":"A patient is prescribed Zanamivir (Relenza) for suspected influenza. Which one of the following underlying problems may increase the likelihood of bronchospasm and side-effects?","options":{"A":"Asthma","B":"Epilepsy","C":"Diabetes mellitus","D":"Renal impairment"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"cause airway irritation and severe bronchospasm. Therefore, it is contraindicated or must be used with extreme caution in patients with reactive airway diseases like asthma or COPD."},{"id":"q_college_368","source":"College MCQ","topic":"COVID-19 & Respiratory Viral Infections","text":"A poorly controlled diabetic patient on high-dose dexamethasone therapy for severe COVID-19 infection develops sudden facial pain, swelling around the orbit, and black nasal discharge. Which opportunistic infection is most likely responsible?","options":{"A":"Invasive Aspergillosis","B":"Systemic Candidiasis","C":"Mucormycosis (Black Fungus)","D":"Cryptococcosis"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"infection seen typically in diabetic patients who receive high-dose corticosteroid therapy. The combination of hyperglycemia, immunosuppression, and elevated free iron levels creates the perfect environment for Rhizopus or Mucor species to invade nasal sinuses and the orbit."},{"id":"q_college_369","source":"College MCQ","topic":"COVID-19 & Respiratory Viral Infections","text":"When managing a hospitalized patient with suspected or confirmed active SARS-CoV-2 (COVID-19) infection during an aerosol-generating procedure, which transmission-based precaution is critically required?","options":{"A":"Positive-pressure isolation room and surgical mask","B":"Negative-pressure isolation room and an N95/FFP3 respirator mask","C":"Standard double-occupancy room and contact precautions only","D":"Protective environment isolation room with no mask required"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"procedures (like intubation). Negative-pressure rooms (prevent air from escaping into corridors) and N95/FFP3 respirators (filter out 95%+ of airborne particles) are non-negotiable for safety."},{"id":"q_college_370","source":"College MCQ","topic":"COVID-19 & Respiratory Viral Infections","text":"Which of the following chest imaging findings is most characteristic of severe COVID-19 or viral pneumonitis on a high-resolution CT (HRCT) scan?","options":{"A":"Dense lobar consolidation with air bronchograms localized to one lobe","B":"Multiple thick-walled cavitary lesions with air-fluid levels","C":"Bilateral, peripheral, and subpleural Ground-Glass Opacities (GGOs)","D":"Large unilateral pleural effusion with mediastinal shift"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"inflammation rather than alveolar space filling. This manifests on CT as bilateral, peripheral, and subpleural Ground-Glass Opacities (GGOs), which may progress to consolidation or a \"crazy-paving\" pattern as severity increases."},{"id":"q_college_371","source":"College MCQ","topic":"COVID-19 & Respiratory Viral Infections","text":"A patient hospitalized with severe COVID-19 is found to have worsening respiratory distress. Which of the following laboratory panels reflects a hyperinflammatory state (\"cytokine storm\") associated with a poor prognosis?","options":{"A":"Leukocytosis, low ferritin, low D-dimer","B":"Lymphopenia, markedly elevated Ferritin, and high D-dimer","C":"Lymphocytosis, low CRP, normal Ferritin","D":"Thrombocytosis, normal LDH, low CRP"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"characterized by lymphopenia (low absolute lymphocyte count), and highly elevated inflammatory markers including Ferritin, D-dimer, CRP, and LDH. Elevated D-dimer also signals a high risk of thromboembolic complications."},{"id":"q_college_372","source":"College MCQ","topic":"COVID-19 & Respiratory Viral Infections","text":"An 8-month-old infant is admitted with high respiratory rate, chest retractions, wheezing, and low-grade fever during winter. Which respiratory virus is the most common cause of this clinical presentation (bronchiolitis) in children under 1 year?","options":{"A":"Adenovirus","B":"Rhinovirus","C":"Respiratory Syncytial Virus (RSV)","D":"Parainfluenza virus"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"pneumonia in infants under one year of age. It presents with wheezing, tachypnea, and accessory muscle use due to small airway obstruction by mucus and cellular debris."},{"id":"q_college_373","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Helicobacter pylori","text":"Helicobacter pylori colonization is implicated in the pathogenesis of all of the following conditions EXCEPT:","options":{"A":"Duodenal ulcer disease","B":"Gastric adenocarcinoma","C":"Gastric mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) lymphoma","D":"Gastro-esophageal reflux disease (GERD)","E":"Chronic active type B gastritis"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"H. pylori is the primary etiologic cause of chronic active gastritis, peptic ulcer disease (gastric and duodenal), distal gastric adenocarcinoma, and B-cell MALT lymphoma. It is not an etiologic cause of GERD, and bacterial eradication does not consistently resolve GERD symptoms."},{"id":"q_college_374","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Helicobacter pylori","text":"Which of the following is an INEFFECTIVE or non-standard eradication regimen for Helicobacter pylori?","options":{"A":"Dual therapy with Amoxicillin and Levofloxacin for 10 days (without a PPI)","B":"Omeprazole + Clarithromycin + Metronidazole for 14 days","C":"Omeprazole + Clarithromycin + Amoxicillin for 14 days (Standard triple therapy)","D":"Omeprazole + Bismuth subsalicylate + Tetracycline + Metronidazole for 14 days (Bismuth quadruple therapy)","E":"Sequential therapy with PPI + Amoxicillin for 5 days followed by PPI + Clarithromycin + Tinidazole for 5 days"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Successful H. pylori eradication strictly mandates potent gastric acid suppression using a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI) combined with at least two or three synergistic antimicrobial agents. Dual antibiotic therapy lacking profound acid suppression produces unacceptably low eradication rates."},{"id":"q_college_375","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Helicobacter pylori","text":"Which ONE of the following statements is true regarding Helicobacter pylori infection?","options":{"A":"H. pylori is a Gram-positive spore-forming bacillus","B":"Colonization with H. pylori is the primary risk factor for gastro-esophageal reflux disease","C":"The prevalence of H. pylori among adults in developing countries is less than 30%","D":"Validated non-invasive diagnostic tests include stool antigen assay and ┬╣┬│C/┬╣ظ┤C urea breath test","E":"Domestic cattle represent the only important mammalian reservoir of H. pylori"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"H. pylori is a microaerophilic, spiral Gram-negative rod with humans as its primary reservoir. Highly sensitive and specific non-invasive tests for active infection and post-treatment eradication confirmation include the ┬╣┬│C/┬╣ظ┤C urea breath test and monoclonal stool antigen assays"},{"id":"q_college_376","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Helicobacter pylori","text":"One month after receiving a 14-day course of the antimicrobials you prescribed for Helicobacter pylori, the patient still has mild dyspepsia and pain after meals. What is the appropriate next step in management to confirm eradication?","options":{"A":"Empirical long-term proton pump inhibitor therapy","B":"Endoscopy with biopsy to rule out gastric adenocarcinoma","C":"Second-line therapy for H. pylori with omeprazole, bismuth subsalicylate, tetracycline, and metronidazole","D":"Urea breath test","E":"H. pylori serology testing"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Test (UBT) or stool antigen test must be performed at least 4 weeks after treatment. Serology is incorrect because IgG antibodies remain positive for years and cannot differentiate between active and past infections."},{"id":"q_college_377","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Helicobacter pylori","text":"In regions with high resistance (>15%) to Clarithromycin, which of the following represents the recommended first-line quadruple treatment regimen for H. pylori eradication?","options":{"A":"PPI + Clarithromycin + Amoxicillin + Metronidazole for 7 days","B":"PPI + Ciprofloxacin + Doxycycline + Metronidazole for 10 days","C":"PPI + Bismuth subsalicylate + Tetracycline + Metronidazole for 14 days","D":"PPI + Amoxicillin + Azithromycin + Levofloxacin for 14 days"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"preferred first-line regimen. It consists of a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI), Bismuth, Tetracycline, and Metronidazole taken for 14 days."},{"id":"q_college_378","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Helicobacter pylori","text":"To avoid a false-negative result when performing a Urea Breath Test (UBT) or Stool Antigen test for H. pylori, which of the following drugs must be discontinued for at least 2 weeks prior to testing?","options":{"A":"Antacids (Calcium carbonate)","B":"Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs)","C":"H2-receptor antagonists (Famotidine)","D":"Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors (SSRIs)"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"eradicating them, which can cause false-negative results on active tests like UBT or stool antigen. PPIs must be stopped for 2 weeks, while antibiotics and bismuth compounds must be stopped for at least 4 weeks before testing."},{"id":"q_college_379","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Helicobacter pylori","text":"A 50-year-old patient presents with dyspepsia. Gastric biopsy confirms active H. pylori infection and reveals low-grade gastric Mucosa-Associated Lymphoid Tissue (MALT) lymphoma. What is the recommended first-line management for this patient?","options":{"A":"Immediate chemotherapy and radiation","B":"Total gastrectomy","C":"H. pylori eradication therapy","D":"Long-term PPI monotherapy"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"MALT lymphoma. Eradication of H. pylori using standard triple or quadruple antibiotic therapy results in complete regression of the lymphoma in up to 70-80% of cases, avoiding the need for chemotherapy or surgery."},{"id":"q_college_380","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"An elderly patient, hospitalized with a gastrointestinal disorder, was on bed rest and required assistance for activities of daily living. The nurse''s hands were not washed before manipulating the tubing attached to the urinary catheter of the second patient who developed CAUTI 3 days later. What is your explanation?","options":{"A":"It is a community-acquired infection","B":"It is a hospital-acquired infection","C":"It is autoinfection","D":"It is a colonization","E":"It is an endogenous infection"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"A healthcare-associated / hospital-acquired infection (HAI) is defined as an infection occurring ظëح48 hours after hospital admission that was not present or incubating at the time of admission. The lack of hand hygiene resulted in exogenous cross-transmission of pathogens between patients via healthcare worker hands."},{"id":"q_college_381","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"The most important practice in reducing HAIs and the transmission of infectious agents is:","options":{"A":"Washing your hands after using the toilet","B":"Washing your hands when they are visibly soiled","C":"Carrying out hand hygiene frequently using the correct technique","D":"Wearing gloves and gowns","E":"Wearing gloves and surgical mask"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Adherence to the 5 Moments of Hand Hygiene using the appropriate technique (alcohol-based rub or soap and water) is universally recognized by the WHO and CDC as the single most effective, evidence-based measure to prevent healthcare-associated cross-transmission."},{"id":"q_college_382","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"Infectious waste includes:","options":{"A":"Radioactive materials","B":"Papers and food","C":"Needles and scalpels","D":"Laboratory cultures","E":"Broken thermometers"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Laboratory cultures and stocks of infectious agents are classified as bio-infectious waste requiring biohazard disposal/autoclaving. Needles and scalpels are classified specifically as sharps waste, whereas radioactive materials and mercury thermometers are regulated chemical/hazardous waste."},{"id":"q_college_383","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"Disinfection is a process in addition to cleaning. When is it necessary?","options":{"A":"When a patient touches a door handle","B":"When you re-use a bedpan made of pulp","C":"It isnظآt necessary unless you know a patient has an HAI","D":"When an item comes into contact with mucous membranes","E":"Before using a sphygmomanometer for measuring blood pressure"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"According to Spaulding''s criteria, semi-critical medical equipment that contacts intact mucous membranes or non-intact skin (e.g., endoscopes, laryngoscopes) requires high-level disinfection at minimum, whereas non- critical items contacting intact skin require low-level cleaning."},{"id":"q_college_384","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"Your patient is on airborne isolation, and he will undergo ultrasound-guided thoracentesis. How will you advise the attendant nurse who will transport him?","options":{"A":"N95 mask on the patient during transport out the room as well as accompanying personnel","B":"Surgical mask on the patient during transport as well as accompanying personnel","C":"N95 mask on the patient, surgical mask for the accompanying personnel","D":"Surgical mask on the patient, N95 mask for the accompanying personnel","E":"Surgical mask on the patient during transport, no need for a mask for accompanying personnel"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"For airborne isolation transport, source control is achieved by having the patient wear a standard surgical mask (which filters exhaled droplets/droplet nuclei). Accompanying healthcare personnel must wear a fitted N95/FFP2 respirator for active personal respiratory protection."},{"id":"q_college_385","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"A patient is to begin IV antibiotic therapy for a pulmonary infection. What should be completed before the first dose of antibiotic is administered?","options":{"A":"Urinalysis","B":"Sputum culture","C":"Chest X-ray","D":"Red blood cell count","E":"Tuberculin test"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Microbiological specimens (sputum and blood cultures) must always be collected prior to antimicrobial administration, as even a single dose of systemic antibiotics rapidly suppresses bacterial viability and causes false-negative culture yields."},{"id":"q_college_386","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"A surgical patient develops a wound infection during hospitalization. How is this type of infection classified?","options":{"A":"Primary","B":"Secondary","C":"Superimposed","D":"Nosocomial","E":"Opportunistic"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Infections acquired in a hospital setting that manifest ظëح48 hours after admission or within 30 days of a surgical procedure are classified as nosocomial / Surgical Site Infections (SSIs)."},{"id":"q_college_387","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"A nurse is teaching a new nursing assistant about ways to prevent the spread of infection. The cycle of the infectious process is broken primarily through:","options":{"A":"Hand washing before and between providing client care","B":"Thoroughly cleaning the environment","C":"Wearing infection control-approved protective equipment when providing patient care","D":"Using medical and surgical aseptic techniques at all times","E":"Proper use of disinfectants"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Hand hygiene between patient contacts directly interrupts the \"Mode of Transmission\" link in the chain of infection, representing the most impactful single intervention."},{"id":"q_college_388","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"A nurse discusses the procedure for protective isolation with the husband of a patient who is receiving chemotherapy and has been hospitalized for neutropenia. The husband asks: Does protective isolation help prevent the spread of infection:","options":{"A":"ظ£To my wife from the outside environment.ظإ","B":"ظ£From my wife to health care personnel and visitors.ظإ","C":"ظ£From my wife by disinfecting infectious fluids and secretions.ظإ","D":"ظ£To my wife by using sterilization techniques for personal items before use.ظإ","E":"ظ£To my wife by special washing machine for her linens.ظإ"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Protective (reverse/neutropenic) isolation is implemented to shield severely immunocompromised hosts (ANC < 500 cells/╬╝L) from opportunistic microorganisms in the hospital environment, healthcare personnel, and visitors."},{"id":"q_college_389","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"The nurse must assign a room to a patient with scabies. Which of the following options is the best choice for this patient?","options":{"A":"A negative-pressure isolation room","B":"A private room","C":"A semi-private room with any patient","D":"A room with another patient with scabies","E":"A room with open window"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Sarcoptes scabiei is transmitted via direct skin-to-skin contact and contaminated fomites. Patients require Contact Precautions in a private room (or cohorting if necessary) until 24 hours of effective topical scabicidal therapy (permethrin 5%) is completed."},{"id":"q_college_390","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"A nurse administers immunizations to prevent infections such as Influenza by which of the following mechanisms?","options":{"A":"Eliminating the mode of transmission","B":"Introducing a weak secondary infection","C":"Blocking the immune response of the host","D":"As a replacement of therapeutic methods","E":"Enhancing the defenses of the host"},"correctOption":"E","explanation":"Vaccination stimulates the adaptive immune response (inducing neutralizing antibodies and memory T cells), thereby enhancing host defenses and rendering the host non-susceptible in the chain of infection."},{"id":"q_college_391","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"A patient has an infection that is spread through droplets. Which of the following is essential for the nurse to use when taking this clientظآs temperature?","options":{"A":"Gloves","B":"Goggles","C":"A gown","D":"A mask","E":"A face shield"},"correctOption":"D","explanation":"Droplet Precautions mandate wearing a standard surgical mask upon entry or when working within 1 to 2 meters of the patient to protect against large respiratory droplets generated during coughing, sneezing, or close-range interaction."},{"id":"q_college_392","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"What should you do if your hands touch the sink while you are washing your hands?","options":{"A":"Add more soap to your hands","B":"Continue to wash your hands","C":"Repeat the procedure","D":"Apply more friction during procedure","E":"Take care and repeat this step only"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"The inside basin of a sink is heavily colonized with pathogenic environmental bacteria (Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter). Accidental contact contaminates the skin and requires restarting the entire hand hygiene process from the beginning."},{"id":"q_college_393","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"Which of the following infections requires handwashing with soap and water rather than alcohol- based hand rub?","options":{"A":"Pneumonia","B":"Clostridium difficile","C":"Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus","D":"Hepatitis A virus","E":"Mycotic infections"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Clostridioides difficile produces dormant bacterial endospores that are intrinsically resistant to alcohol-based hand rubs. Physical washing with soap, water, and mechanical friction is strictly required to physically rinse the spores off the skin."},{"id":"q_college_394","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"When caring for a patient with bronchitis, what is the best way the nurse can prevent the spread of infection?","options":{"A":"By empirical antibiotics","B":"ET Intubation","C":"Hand Washing","D":"Vaccination","E":"Wearing mask"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"Routine hand hygiene before and after patient contact remains the primary, gold-standard method for preventing cross-contamination of respiratory secretions in non-isolated bronchitis patients."},{"id":"q_college_395","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"Prophylactic surgical antibiotics are optimally administered:","options":{"A":"To treat colonized patients","B":"With the induction of anaesthesia","C":"To be changed after 5 days","D":"Until the wound is healed","E":"Until there is no painful wound"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Surgical antimicrobial prophylaxis (SAP) must be administered within 30 to 60 minutes prior to skin incision (typically during induction of anesthesia) to guarantee peak bactericidal drug levels in the tissue before bacterial contamination can occur."},{"id":"q_college_396","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"The habitat (animals, human, or environment) in which an infectious agent normally lives, grows, and multiplies is known as:","options":{"A":"The reservoir of infection","B":"The portal of exit","C":"Mode of transmission","D":"Portal of entry","E":"The susceptible host"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"In the epidemiologic chain of infection, the reservoir is the natural ecological niche or habitat (human, animal, soil, water) where the infectious agent resides and replicates."},{"id":"q_college_397","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"You should always perform hand hygiene even if you were wearing gloves:","options":{"A":"True","B":"False"},"correctOption":"A","explanation":"Gloves can harbor microscopic micropunctures, and hands are frequently contaminated during the process of removing (degloving) PPE; hand hygiene immediately after glove removal is mandatory."},{"id":"q_college_398","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"It is impossible to break the Chain of Transmission:","options":{"A":"True","B":"False"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"Infection prevention and control strategies (hand hygiene, isolation precautions, sterilization, vaccination, and source control) are specifically engineered to break one or more links in the chain of transmission."},{"id":"q_college_399","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"What is a pandemic?","options":{"A":"An outbreak which may occur in a community or geographical area","B":"It may last for a few days or weeks, or even for several years","C":"Affects a wider geographical area, often worldwide with a greater number of people","D":"It occurs when an infectious disease spreads rapidly to many people","E":"It occurs when an infectious disease is present in a certain locality for a long time"},"correctOption":"C","explanation":"A pandemic is an epidemic that has spread across multiple international borders, continents, or worldwide, affecting a substantial proportion of the global population."},{"id":"q_college_400","source":"College MCQ","topic":"Infection Prevention & Control (IPC) & HAIs","text":"What is the primary role of the Clinical Microbiology Laboratory in Infection Prevention and Control (IPC)?","options":{"A":"Helping in the diagnosis of clotting factors","B":"Helping in planning antibiotic policy","C":"Helping in the diagnosis of liver diseases","D":"Help in the epidemiological tracing of tumour markers","E":"Helping in the diagnosis of genetic diseases"},"correctOption":"B","explanation":"The microbiology laboratory provides essential antibiograms, identifies multidrug-resistant organisms (MDROs), tracks hospital-wide resistance trends, and directly guides empirical antimicrobial policies and stewardship programs."}]'::jsonb;
BEGIN
    -- 1. Update or Insert the master question bank for 'infection' course
    INSERT INTO public.hawari_global_questions (group_name, questions, last_updated)
    VALUES ('infection', v_new_bank, v_timestamp)
    ON CONFLICT (group_name) DO UPDATE
    SET questions = EXCLUDED.questions,
        last_updated = EXCLUDED.last_updated;

    RAISE NOTICE 'Successfully updated infection question bank with 966 questions at timestamp %', v_timestamp;
END $$;

-- 2. Ensure clients can check version timestamp without hitting 401 RLS restriction
GRANT SELECT (group_name, last_updated) ON public.hawari_global_questions TO anon, authenticated;

-- 3. Create / Replace fast version checking RPC
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION public.get_question_bank_version(p_group text DEFAULT 'infection')
RETURNS bigint
LANGUAGE plpgsql
SECURITY DEFINER
AS $$
DECLARE
    v_updated bigint;
BEGIN
    SELECT last_updated INTO v_updated
    FROM public.hawari_global_questions
    WHERE lower(trim(group_name)) = lower(trim(p_group))
    LIMIT 1;
    RETURN coalesce(v_updated, 0);
END;
$$;
GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION public.get_question_bank_version(text) TO anon, authenticated;

-- 4. Verification Query: Inspect resulting question counts and timestamp
SELECT 
    group_name, 
    last_updated,
    jsonb_array_length(questions) as total_questions,
    (SELECT count(*) FROM jsonb_array_elements(questions) elem WHERE elem->>'source' ILIKE '%college%') as college_mcq_count,
    (SELECT count(*) FROM jsonb_array_elements(questions) elem WHERE elem->>'source' ILIKE '%past%') as past_exam_count
FROM public.hawari_global_questions
WHERE group_name = 'infection';
